window.__DEST__=[{"n":"Chipiona","r":"Cádiz province, Andalusia","c":"Spain","lat":36.7373,"lon":-6.4363,"h":0.35,"km":16,"b":5,"why":"The Faro de Chipiona is the tallest lighthouse in Spain at 69m and you can climb it; at low tide the corrales de pesca — Roman-era stone fish traps — emerge as curving walls in the shallows off Playa de las Canteras, which is a genuinely odd and specific thing to see. The town itself is a low-rise Spanish family resort, pleasant rather than beautiful.","e":10,"drive":"Twenty minutes on the A-491. Flat, straight, boring in the best way. Wide seafront avenues in town.","toll":"No toll.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Parking Sur, the large free paved lot by the bus station — 24/7, ~10 min walk to the centre, and the reliable option when everything else is full. For the lighthouse and Playa de Regla specifically: the free unpaved Las Canteras lot, reached via Calle Dr. Tolosa Latour or Avenida de Sevilla, with direct beach access.","pd":9,"pb":"Easy. Parking Sur is big flat asphalt. The central blue zone is capped at 2 hours with fines over €150 including towing, so use the free lots instead. In July–August arrive before 10:00 or the beachside lots (Cruz del Mar–Muelle in particular) will be full.","lez":0,"lezn":"Well under 50,000 inhabitants — no ZBE, none planned.","cats":["coast","quirky"],"gem":false,"season":"May–June and September; August is packed with Sevillano families","one":"Climb the 344 steps of the Faro de Chipiona at a booked slot, then walk out onto the corrales de pesca at low tide.","book":""},{"n":"El Puerto de Santa María","r":"Cádiz province, Andalusia","c":"Spain","lat":36.5939,"lon":-6.2331,"h":0.35,"km":16,"b":6,"why":"The Castillo de San Marcos is a squat 13th-century mosque-turned-fortress you can walk the ramparts of, and the Ribera del Marisco is a solid wall of tile-fronted seafood bars along the Guadalete where Romerijo shovels prawns into paper cones. The Plaza de Toros (1880, one of Spain's largest) and the crumbling bodega quarter of Calle Valdés give it a faded-grandeur look Cádiz doesn't have.","e":10,"drive":"The single easiest trip from Rota. A-491 dual carriageway the whole way, one big roundabout at the Costa Ballena junction, then straight in. Twenty minutes, no hills, no surprises. Streets in the centre are wide by Andalusian standards.","toll":"No toll. Optional passenger catamaran to Cádiz from Muelle del Vapor (El Vaporcito route) — leave the car here and cross by boat, which neatly sidesteps the Cádiz ZBE.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Free asphalt lot at the Plaza de Toros (Calle Los Moros), ~650m from Castillo de San Marcos — open, marked, pull-in bays. Backup free lots: Monasterio de la Victoria (large, by the train station) and Bajamar (by the catamaran dock, ~700m from the centre). Paid fallback: Interparking Centro on Calle Isaac Peral, 250+ spaces, underground, ~€1.80/h.","pd":8,"pb":"No problem at all. The Plaza de Toros and Monasterio lots are open flat asphalt with room to swing a 4.85m car around. Avoid the tight lanes around Calle Luna and Plaza de España — one-way, cars parked both sides, hard to reverse out of. Note that informal attendants (gorrillas) work the free lots and will expect a euro or two; you're not obliged to pay.","lez":1,"lezn":"Population is over the 50,000 threshold so a ZBE is legally required, but as of mid-2026 El Puerto has no operational zone, no cameras, and no restrictions on unlabelled diesels. Nothing to worry about now; worth re-checking in a year.","cats":["coast","city","foodwine","history"],"gem":false,"season":"Spring and autumn; summer evenings for the Ribera","one":"Eat standing up at Romerijo on the Ribera del Marisco — buy a cartucho of langostinos de Sanlúcar by weight at the counter, then take it across to the bar with a cold beer.","book":""},{"n":"Doñana (boat from Bajo de Guía)","r":"Doñana National Park, across the Guadalquivir from Sanlúcar","c":"Spain","lat":36.794,"lon":-6.352,"h":0.5,"km":26,"b":8,"why":"You cross the Guadalquivir into a UNESCO wilderness that has no public road access at all — the boat noses up to the Doñana bank and you walk ashore at La Plancha, through umbrella pine at El Pinar, the reed-and-thatch chozas, and the open marsh flats of Los Llanos. Flamingos, deer and boar on the sandbanks, and the mouth of Spain's biggest river with nothing built on it.","e":9,"drive":"Same easy run as Sanlúcar. Bajo de Guía is at the bottom of town, flat, well signed, with big open parking areas by the river.","toll":"No toll. The trip itself is a boat — you drive to Bajo de Guía in Sanlúcar and board there. Your car stays on the Sanlúcar side.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"The open unpaved and street parking along Bajo de Guía / Calle Esteban de Boutelou, free, right by the boarding pontoon.","pd":8,"pb":"Open riverfront ground, plenty of nose-in space. Arrive 30 minutes before departure in summer as the seafood restaurants fill the same spaces at lunchtime.","lez":0,"lezn":"None. The park itself is car-free — that's the point.","cats":["nature","quirky"],"gem":true,"season":"Late autumn through spring for birdlife and comfortable heat; avoid midsummer midday","one":"The Real Fernando river cruise into Doñana — 2h30–2h45 depending on the tide, with about an hour ashore on guided stops at El Pinar, Las Chozas and Los Llanos. Booking ahead is mandatory (+34 956 363 813); it is not a turn-up-and-go boat.","book":""},{"n":"Sanlúcar de Barrameda","r":"Cádiz province, Andalusia","c":"Spain","lat":36.7787,"lon":-6.3533,"h":0.5,"km":25,"b":7,"why":"Bajo de Guía is the money shot — a row of white seafood restaurants facing the Guadalquivir with the empty pine-and-dune wall of Doñana on the far bank, sun setting behind it. Uphill in the Barrio Alto, the Palacio de los Duques de Medina Sidonia and the neo-Mudéjar Palacio de Orleans y Borbón sit above the Covachas, a bizarre 15th-century Gothic arcade of stone sea-monsters built into a retaining wall.","e":9,"drive":"A-491 through Chipiona, dual carriageway then good single-carriageway, flat farmland and pine. Easy. The only awkward bit is the climb from the Barrio Bajo to the Barrio Alto — narrow, steep, one-way in places. Park low and walk up.","toll":"No toll, no ferry for the car. The Doñana boat leaves from Bajo de Guía on foot.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Free: the wide open areas around Calle Esteban de Boutelou near the Paseo Marítimo, and the large unpaved areas near Bajo de Guía. Sanlúcar is unusually generous — you can park free almost anywhere except the Recinto Ferial and its boulevard. Paid fallback: Parkia La Calzada on Avenida Calzada Duquesa Isabel, ~€3.80 per 2 hours, right on the main axis between town and beach.","pd":8,"pb":"Very manageable. The Calzada Duquesa Isabel is a broad boulevard and its side streets have long free stretches. Bajo de Guía has plenty of open dirt/gravel to nose into. Do not drive up into the Barrio Alto lanes around the Castillo de Santiago — genuinely too tight. During the August beach horse races the whole seafront is closed and parking collapses; go early or go another day.","lez":1,"lezn":"Just over the 50,000 threshold so legally obliged eventually, but there is no operational ZBE, no cameras and no restrictions on unlabelled vehicles as of 2026.","cats":["coast","city","foodwine","nature","history"],"gem":false,"season":"Spring for the manzanilla and langostinos; the first two weekends of August for the beach horse races","one":"Langostinos de Sanlúcar and a cold copa of manzanilla en rama at Casa Bigote or Casa Balbino, at a table on the sand at Bajo de Guía facing Doñana.","book":""},{"n":"Jerez de la Frontera","r":"Cádiz province, Andalusia","c":"Spain","lat":36.6816,"lon":-6.1377,"h":0.55,"km":36,"b":7,"why":"The Alcázar's Almohad mosque and Arab baths sit inside palm gardens directly beneath the ochre baroque bulk of the Cathedral, and the sherry bodegas — the cathedral-like naves of González Byass and Lustau, stacked three barrels high in the dark — are architecture in their own right. Plaza del Arenal and the Barrio de Santiago's flamenco alleys fill in the rest.","e":9,"drive":"A-491 to El Puerto, then A-4/N-IV north. Motorway standard. Jerez itself has a ring of roundabouts and a fair amount of urban traffic, but the streets are wide and the underground car parks are well signposted from the ring road.","toll":"No toll.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Best value: Parking Los Cisnes (Calle Larga) — underground, about €6 for 24 hours, 550m / 7 min walk to the Cathedral. Plaza del Arenal underground is more central at ~€8.22/day. Free option if you'll walk: Parking Pino near the Fundador bodegas (~25 spaces, ~10 min to the Cathedral), or the big free lots at Feria / La Unión / Merca80, which are 2km+ out and a 25–30 min walk.","pd":7,"pb":"The underground garages are modern with normal bays — you pull in, no parallel parking. Los Cisnes at €6 a day is cheap enough that it's worth breaking the no-paying rule. Street parking is ORA-regulated Mon–Sat (blue 2h, orange 1h, green 5h) and free on Sundays — a Sunday visit means free street parking, but the bodegas mostly close Sunday.","lez":1,"lezn":"Jerez approved an initial ZBE ordinance in January 2025 and the ordinance formally entered into force by legal obligation, but the council explicitly stated it adds no new circulation restrictions, and the map delimiting the zone has still not been finalised. No cameras, no fines, no restriction on unlabelled diesels as of 2026. Fines of €200 are on the books for whenever it does go live.","cats":["city","foodwine","history"],"gem":false,"season":"Late April–May for the Feria del Caballo and horse season; September for the vendimia","one":"A tasting tour at Bodegas Tradición — small, appointment-only, holds VORS sherries aged 30+ years plus a private Spanish painting collection with Goya and Velázquez in the bodega itself.","book":""},{"n":"Puerto Real","r":"Cádiz province, Andalusia","c":"Spain","lat":36.5286,"lon":-6.1893,"h":0.55,"km":30,"b":4,"why":"Honestly the weakest stop in the cluster — a working shipyard town founded by the Catholic Monarchs, with a decent grid centre around Plaza de Jesús and the Iglesia de San Sebastián. Its real asset is what's around it: the Pinar de las Canteras umbrella-pine woods and the salt-pan channels of the Parque Natural Bahía de Cádiz, where the tide runs through 18th-century salinas.","e":9,"drive":"A-4 south past El Puerto, straight in. Motorway, wide streets, no drama. Comfortably the easiest way to reach the bay salt marshes without touching Cádiz.","toll":"No toll.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free open lots around the Recinto Ferial and the wide free street parking on the avenues near the Pinar de las Canteras; the Playa de la Cachucha / Río San Pedro areas also have free unpaved parking.","pd":9,"pb":"Nothing here is difficult. Streets are wide, lots are open, and the town has never had a parking shortage.","lez":0,"lezn":"Around 41,000 inhabitants — below the 50,000 legal trigger. No ZBE, none planned.","cats":["nature","history"],"gem":false,"season":"Autumn and winter for birdwatching on the salinas","one":"Walk the salina trails in the Parque Natural Bahía de Cádiz at Río San Pedro at low tide — spoonbills, flamingos and the old salt-pan geometry, ten minutes from a motorway.","book":""},{"n":"Trebujena","r":"Cádiz province, Andalusia","c":"Spain","lat":36.8686,"lon":-6.1789,"h":0.6,"km":45,"b":5,"why":"A white village on a low bluff staring out over the Guadalquivir marismas — from the Mirador del Guadalquivir by the church you see nothing but rice paddies, salt marsh and the Doñana horizon, with no built structure in view. The draw is the ring of family mosto bodegas below the village serving raw young wine straight from the tank between December and March.","e":8,"drive":"A-491 to Sanlúcar then the A-471 inland along the marsh edge — quiet, flat, big skies, almost no traffic. The last approach into the village climbs a short ramp; village streets are narrow and steep but you don't need to enter them.","toll":"No toll.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free open ground around the Plaza de España and the wide areas on the approach road below the church; the mosto bodegas out on the marsh road (Camino de la Marisma) all have their own free dirt yards.","pd":9,"pb":"Almost no competition for space. Park at the bottom and walk up. The marsh-road bodegas have farm-scale dirt yards where a big sedan is a non-issue.","lez":0,"lezn":"Tiny town, no ZBE, nothing planned.","cats":["village","foodwine","nature"],"gem":true,"season":"January–March for mosto season; spring for marsh birdlife","one":"Mosto season at one of the ventas on the Camino de la Marisma — a jug of unfortified new wine with a plate of chicharrones, at farm prices, surrounded entirely by locals.","book":""},{"n":"Cádiz","r":"Cádiz province, Andalusia","c":"Spain","lat":36.5298,"lon":-6.2925,"h":0.7,"km":40,"b":9,"why":"The Campo del Sur seawall with the yellow-tiled Cathedral dome rising straight out of it is one of the great urban seafronts in Europe, and La Caleta — a tiny crescent of sand pinned between the castles of San Sebastián and Santa Catalina — is where the sun sets into the Atlantic. Add the Torre Tavira camera obscura over a forest of 18th-century merchant watchtowers and the ficus giants in the Alameda Apodaca.","e":7,"drive":"Fast and easy until the last five minutes. A-491 to El Puerto, A-4 south, then CA-33 onto the 1812 bridge — genuinely scenic, 69m above the bay. Then it degrades: Cádiz is a dense grid on a sandbar with heavy traffic on Avenida del Puerto and the Paseo Marítimo, and the ZBE cameras add a layer of anxiety. Plan your parking before you're on the bridge.","toll":"No toll (the AP-4 Sevilla–Cádiz lost its toll on 1 Jan 2020 and is now free). You cross the Puente de la Constitución de 1812 — the huge cable-stayed bridge — which is spectacular but backs up badly at rush hour. Alternative: park in El Puerto and take the catamaran across the bay.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Navalips free lot, Extramuros (former Navalips shipyard grounds, off the Zona Franca / Avenida de Astilleros side) — 220 free marked spaces, opened July 2026, outside the ZBE. Second free option: Campo del Cura, ~70 free spaces. If you'd rather pay for a short visit, Parking Canalejas on Avenida del Puerto (~€2.85/h, ~€20/day) is 5 minutes' walk from the Ayuntamiento; Parking Reina Sofía at the port is far cheaper at ~€8.58/day but a 15–20 min walk.","pd":5,"pb":"Do not attempt to street-park in the old town — the streets inside the walls are 3–4m wide with parked cars on both sides and the few white-zone spaces are tight parallel slots. Navalips is open flat ground and completely painless for a big sedan. Canalejas is underground with normal-width bays but a tight spiral ramp; Reina Sofía is roomier. Santa Bárbara and Valcárcel near La Caleta have notably narrow bays.","lez":2,"lezn":"ACTIVE AND ENFORCED — the one place in the cluster where his car is a real problem. Cádiz's ZBE has been fining since July 2025, monitored by 33 cameras. It covers the whole walled historic centre (bounded by Cuesta de las Calesas, Campo del Sur, Alameda, Paseo de Carlos III, Avenida del Puerto) plus the Paseo Marítimo from the Hotel Playa roundabout to Cortadura. The ronda de circunvalación is excluded. A 2005 diesel is 'sin distintivo' (no DGT label — diesels need Euro 4 / 2006-on for even a B sticker), and non-resident unlabelled vehicles are barred. THE KEY EXCEPTION: the council does not sanction outside vehicles that are driving to a car park ('no se sancionará a los vehículos de fuera que se dirijan a un aparcamiento'). Safest play is still to park at Navalips outside the zone, or skip the car entirely and take the catamaran from El Puerto. From 2027 only Cero/Eco labels will be allowed for non-residents, so the drive-to-parking loophole may narrow.","cats":["coast","city","history","foodwine"],"gem":false,"season":"Year-round; February for Carnaval (parking impossible), September for warm sea and no crowds","one":"Climb the Torre Tavira for the camera obscura session — a live periscope image of the whole city projected onto a dish, narrated, and the only way to grasp how the watchtower skyline works.","book":""},{"n":"San Fernando","r":"Cádiz province, Andalusia","c":"Spain","lat":36.4728,"lon":-6.1978,"h":0.75,"km":48,"b":5,"why":"The Castillo de San Romualdo is a genuinely strange 14th-century brick fortress marooned in a modern square, and the Real Observatorio de la Armada — Spain's naval observatory, once the country's prime meridian — sits in gardens on the edge of town. The salinas and the Molino de mareas de El Zaporito, a tidal mill on a marsh creek, are the memorable bits; the town centre itself is workmanlike.","e":8,"drive":"A-4 then A-48/CA-33 — motorway all the way, but you skirt the Cádiz bottleneck and the approach at rush hour is slow. Once inside, the Calle Real axis is long and straight; side streets are ordinary grid.","toll":"No toll.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Free municipal surface lots on the town's periphery (the council opened a new 124-space free lot recently, and there is free public parking in the Santa María area) plus free unpaved parking near the Zaporito tidal mill and the Bahía Sur retail area. Central Calle Real is metered.","pd":7,"pb":"Fine on the outskirts. Avoid trying to park along Calle Real and the pedestrianised core — it's metered, tight and parallel-only. The Bahía Sur shopping centre car park is free and enormous if you want a guaranteed pull-in space and don't mind a 15-minute walk.","lez":1,"lezn":"Over 90,000 inhabitants so legally required, but no operational ZBE, no cameras and no restrictions in force as of 2026. Watch this one — it's the most likely in the province to follow Cádiz.","cats":["city","history","nature"],"gem":false,"season":"Spring and autumn","one":"The Molino de mareas de El Zaporito — a restored tidal mill on the Caño de Sancti Petri, one of very few left in Spain, with the marsh path running out from it.","book":""},{"n":"Chiclana & La Barrosa","r":"Cádiz province, Andalusia","c":"Spain","lat":36.3853,"lon":-6.1621,"h":0.8,"km":56,"b":5,"why":"La Barrosa is 8km of genuinely fine pale sand backed at its southern end by the Loma del Puerco pine-covered cliff — the far end past Novo Sancti Petri is wild and empty. The town behind it is a sprawl of urbanizaciones and hotels with a small old core around the Iglesia de San Juan Bautista; you come for the beach and the moscatel bodegas, not the streets.","e":8,"drive":"A-4 then A-48 motorway south, exit into a long run of roundabouts through the urbanizaciones. The last 10 minutes in July–August is stop-start resort traffic. Roads are wide throughout.","toll":"No toll.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Free unpaved lots behind the Paseo Marítimo of La Barrosa and along the access roads at each numbered beach entrance; the biggest and most reliable are at the southern (Loma del Puerco / Novo Sancti Petri) end. In town, free surface lots exist near the Recinto Ferial.","pd":7,"pb":"The beach-access lots are open sand-and-gravel, easy for a big car, but in August they fill by 10:30 and cars then park along the verges — which is where a 4.85m sedan gets awkward and where the local police ticket. Go early or go in June/September.","lez":1,"lezn":"Population is over 50,000 so a ZBE is legally due, but nothing is operational, no cameras, no restrictions on unlabelled vehicles as of 2026.","cats":["coast","foodwine"],"gem":false,"season":"June and September — full sun without the August crush","one":"Walk the southern end of La Barrosa from Loma del Puerco toward Sancti Petri at low tide, where the pine cliff drops to the sand and the crowds thin out completely.","book":""},{"n":"Medina Sidonia","r":"Cádiz province, Andalusia","c":"Spain","lat":36.457,"lon":-5.927,"h":0.9,"km":68,"b":7,"why":"A white town on an isolated 300m hill with the best all-round view in the province — from the Alcázar ruins and the Torre de Doña Blanca you can see the Bay of Cádiz, the Sierra de Grazalema and, on a clear day, the Strait. Beneath the streets are excavated Roman sewers you can walk through, and the Iglesia de Santa María la Coronada holds a 15m carved retablo.","e":7,"drive":"A-4 then the A-390/A-393 across open dehesa farmland — quiet, rolling, empty roads with almost no traffic. Pleasant driving. The final climb into the town is steep with tight bends, and the upper streets around the Alcázar are narrow and cobbled.","toll":"No toll.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free public lots on the lower ring of the town — Parking Público 'El Tejar', Aparcamiento La Pedrera and the Cementerio lot are the practical open-ground options; park at one and walk up.","pd":8,"pb":"The free peripheral lots are open, empty and easy — Medina Sidonia gets few enough visitors that parking is never a fight. But the climb to the Alcázar is on genuinely narrow cobbled lanes; walk it. The 10-minute uphill walk is the price of not paying and not parallel parking.","lez":0,"lezn":"About 11,500 inhabitants — no ZBE, none planned.","cats":["village","history","foodwine"],"gem":true,"season":"Spring and autumn; the hilltop catches a breeze in summer","one":"The guided walk through the excavated Roman sewers (Conjunto Arqueológico Romano) under the town, then up to the Alcázar for the panorama — and buy alfajores, the town's Moorish-era honey-and-almond pastries, from one of the historic obradores.","book":""},{"n":"Arcos de la Frontera","r":"Cádiz province, Andalusia","c":"Spain","lat":36.7497,"lon":-5.8103,"h":0.95,"km":68,"b":9,"why":"The town sits on a sandstone spur with a sheer cliff dropping to the Guadalete on three sides — the Mirador de la Peña Nueva and the Balcón de Arcos beside the Basílica de Santa María put you directly above a 100m drop over the river and the Arcos reservoir. The Plaza del Cabildo, with the castle wall on one side and the cliff on the other, is one of the great squares in Andalusia.","e":6,"drive":"A-4 to Jerez then the A-382/A-372 — easy, fast, open country. Then the climb into the old town, which is where it turns: steep switchbacks, extremely narrow one-way lanes with wing-mirror clearances, and blind corners. Arcos is the classic town where tourists get their hire car wedged. Do not go up.","toll":"No toll.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Free: the Recinto Ferial lot on Avenida Duque de Arcos — open-air, no cost, with a shuttle bus up to the historic centre every 30 minutes. Paid: the Paseo de Andalucía underground car park at roughly €0.33/min or €15 for 24 hours, with buses to the monumental area every 30 minutes from nearby Plaza de España, or a 10-minute walk up.","pd":8,"pb":"The free Recinto Ferial lot plus the half-hourly shuttle is exactly the right answer for this car — free, open ground, pull-in, and you never take the sedan up the hill. The Paseo de Andalucía underground has a tight ramp. If you ignore this advice and drive to the Plaza del Cabildo, be aware there are only a handful of spaces up there and the streets you'd have to reverse back down are single-car-width with stone walls.","lez":0,"lezn":"About 30,000 inhabitants — no ZBE, none planned. The old town is heavily restricted by physical width and one-way systems rather than by cameras.","cats":["village","history"],"gem":false,"season":"Spring and autumn; Semana Santa here includes a running of bulls through the old town","one":"Stand on the Balcón de Arcos beside the Basílica de Santa María at sunset, looking straight down the cliff over the Guadalete valley.","book":""},{"n":"Sancti Petri","r":"Chiclana de la Frontera, Cádiz province, Andalusia","c":"Spain","lat":36.3884,"lon":-6.2189,"h":0.95,"km":65,"b":7,"why":"The Poblado de Sancti Petri is a half-abandoned tuna fishermen's village of low white and ochre buildings on a spit, part restored and part still derelict, facing a tidal channel. Offshore, the Castillo de Sancti Petri stands alone on a rock island built over the site of the Phoenician Temple of Melqart — the Hercules temple Hannibal is said to have visited — and at sunset the whole channel goes copper.","e":7,"drive":"Motorway to Chiclana, then a long meander through Novo Sancti Petri urbanizaciones on roads designed by someone who loved roundabouts. The final kilometre to the marina is a narrow single road with a dead end at the water — turning a big car around at the end is the only pinch point.","toll":"No toll to drive. The castle itself requires a short boat from the Sancti Petri marina — it sits on an island and cannot be reached on foot.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free unpaved parking areas at the Sancti Petri marina / poblado approach — open ground, nose-in, no charge.","pd":7,"pb":"Open dirt parking is fine, but the approach road is single-width with limited passing places and it gets congested at sunset when everyone arrives for the boat trips and the beach bars. Arrive before 18:00 in summer or you'll be reversing down a narrow lane.","lez":0,"lezn":"Part of Chiclana municipality; no operational ZBE.","cats":["coast","history","quirky","village"],"gem":true,"season":"May–October for the boats; late September is ideal","one":"Take the sunset boat from the Sancti Petri marina out to the Castillo island — you land, walk the fort, and watch the sun drop behind the Atlantic from the site of the Temple of Melqart.","book":""},{"n":"Alcalá de los Gazules","r":"Cádiz province, Andalusia","c":"Spain","lat":36.4621,"lon":-5.7278,"h":1.05,"km":88,"b":8,"why":"Los Alcornocales is the largest cork-oak forest on earth, and its signature feature is the canutos — deep humid ravines holding relict laurel-forest vegetation left over from the Tertiary, essentially a fragment of subtropical Africa. Alcalá itself is a white town stacked on a ridge above the Barbate river with the Santo Domingo church and the ruined castle on top, and vultures circling constantly.","e":6,"drive":"A-381 dual carriageway from Jerez is fast and beautiful — cork oaks, deer fencing and wildlife bridges. Once you leave it for the park roads (A-2304 toward Benalup, or the forest tracks) things narrow considerably: single-lane tarmac, blind bends, cattle grids, and free-roaming cows and fighting bulls on the road. The town's own streets are steep and tight.","toll":"No toll.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free open parking around the Alameda de la Cruz and the lower approach roads in Alcalá; inside the park, free unpaved parking at signed trailheads (El Picacho and Llanos del Juncal are the main ones on the A-381 corridor).","pd":8,"pb":"No competition for space anywhere. The one real caution is the forest tracks: several of the park's interior roads are unsurfaced with potholes and drainage cuts, which a low-slung E-Class will not enjoy. Stick to the tarmac trailhead access from the A-381 and walk from there. Some park trails (El Picacho, Garganta de Puerto Oscuro) require a free permit from the Junta de Andalucía.","lez":0,"lezn":"None.","cats":["nature","village"],"gem":true,"season":"October–May; the canutos are lushest in late winter and spring, and summer is hot and fire-restricted","one":"Walk into one of the canutos — the Garganta del Capitán or El Picacho trail — where a north-facing ravine holds ferns and laurel forest that has survived since before the Ice Age, twenty minutes off a motorway.","book":""},{"n":"Conil de la Frontera","r":"Cádiz province, Andalusia","c":"Spain","lat":36.277,"lon":-6.0894,"h":1.05,"km":82,"b":7,"why":"The Torre de Guzmán, a 15th-century keep, anchors a small genuinely white old town of lime-washed alleys running down to the sea, and the coast either side is the good stuff: Cala del Aceite is a sheltered cove behind a pine-topped bluff, and the calas at Fuente del Gallo and Roche are little sandstone-cliff coves reached by wooden stairs.","e":9,"drive":"A-48 dual carriageway almost the entire way — genuinely fast, 55 minutes of motorway then a short run in. The old town is a warren but you never need to drive into it.","toll":"No toll.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Explanada María Zambrano — a large free lot near the Aldi and bus stops with 3.75m height clearance, and notably one that rarely fills even in August. Also free: Explanada del Piojito (250+ spaces on concrete, but unavailable Fridays 06:00–16:00 for the weekly market, and a 25-min walk to the coast) and Campo del Cachorro (~70 marked spaces, 15 min from the beach). Avenida del Río is beach-adjacent but fills fast.","pd":9,"pb":"One of the best-provisioned towns on this coast for a big car. María Zambrano is flat, open, marked and free. Avoid Parking La Vaguada (covered, paid) — its bays are described as tight. Never drive into the old town streets around the Torre de Guzmán.","lez":0,"lezn":"Around 22,000 inhabitants — no ZBE, none planned.","cats":["coast","village","foodwine"],"gem":false,"season":"May–June and September–October; the almadraba tuna season runs roughly April–June","one":"Atún de almadraba in season — order the tarantelo or the ventresca at one of the town's tuna specialists, from tuna netted by the ancient almadraba traps a few kilometres offshore.","book":""},{"n":"Playa de El Palmar","r":"Vejer de la Frontera, Cádiz province, Andalusia","c":"Spain","lat":36.2296,"lon":-6.0347,"h":1.15,"km":90,"b":7,"why":"Five kilometres of dead-straight open Atlantic beach with no high-rise on it — just a low string of surf shacks and chiringuitos behind the dune, and the Cabo de Trafalgar headland closing the southern end. It gets the best Atlantic sunsets on this coast because nothing blocks the horizon.","e":9,"drive":"A-48 then the CA-2233 beach road. The final road runs parallel to the beach and is straight, flat and wide — no cliff, no hairpins. Easy.","toll":"No toll.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free unpaved parking along the entire length of the beach-back road and in the open dirt areas behind the dune line. There's also a dedicated paid caravan/camper area if you ever wanted it.","pd":9,"pb":"Excellent for a big car — huge open sandy verges you simply nose into, no parallel parking anywhere. Only caveat: soft sand at the edges, so keep to the compacted areas. On August weekends and sunset in high season the near-beach spots go, but the road is long and you'll always find something.","lez":0,"lezn":"None.","cats":["coast","nature"],"gem":false,"season":"September–October for warm water and surf swell; any clear evening for the sunset","one":"Sunset with a drink at one of the beach shacks on the dune line — this is the beach locals from Vejer drive down to, and the light is the whole point.","book":""},{"n":"Vejer de la Frontera","r":"Cádiz province, Andalusia","c":"Spain","lat":36.253,"lon":-5.9663,"h":1.15,"km":92,"b":9,"why":"A pure-white Moorish hill town wrapped in 15th-century walls on a 190m crag, with the Arco de la Segur and Arco de la Villa gates still standing and the Plaza de España's Sevillian-tiled fountain with its frogs at the centre. From the Mirador de la Cobijada you see the Barbate river valley, La Janda's plain, the wind farms on the ridges and the Atlantic all at once — and on clear days, Africa.","e":6,"drive":"Easy A-48 motorway for the first hour, then the sting: a steep, twisting climb up the CA-5203 to the town on the crag, with switchbacks and blind bends. Not dangerous, but it is the first properly mountainous driving of this trip. The old town within the walls is effectively impassable — 'physically impossible for most cars', with steep grades, blind curves and slippery cobbles.","toll":"No toll.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Los Remedios (Calle los Remedios 45a) — the main free paved lot, 3–5 minutes' walk from the centre, and the most comfortable option. It fills first. Backups, all free: Las Cobijadas (~40 shaded spaces near the bus station, ~9 min walk), Zona Franca on Avenida Andalucía (spacious sand lot), Centro de Salud on Calle Limón (the locals' hidden one), Chorrillo (~50 spaces, no shade), Parque Nelson Mandela (~5 min walk, near the viewpoints), and the Recinto Ferial La Noria as a peak-August emergency (15–20 min walk). Paid fallback: Parking La Corredera, covered, with a lift straight up into the historic centre.","pd":7,"pb":"Plenty of free pull-in lots, which is exactly what he wants — but Los Remedios and Miralles are small and go early, so aim to arrive before 11:00 or after 16:00. Besaro's irregular surface puts people off and therefore usually has room. Under no circumstances drive inside the walls: a 4.85m car will get wedged, and turning around is not possible.","lez":0,"lezn":"Around 13,000 inhabitants — no ZBE. The old town is a de facto restricted zone by geometry, not by camera.","cats":["village","history","foodwine"],"gem":false,"season":"April–June and September–October; the town is genuinely cooler than the plain","one":"Walk the walls from the Arco de la Villa round to the Mirador de la Cobijada at dusk, then eat at one of the tiny courtyard places off Calle Corredera — Vejer punches far above its size for food.","book":""},{"n":"Barbate & La Breña cliffs","r":"Cádiz province, Andalusia","c":"Spain","lat":36.192,"lon":-5.9223,"h":1.25,"km":100,"b":8,"why":"The Torre del Tajo is a 16th-century watchtower on the lip of a 100m sheer cliff — the highest sea cliff between Cádiz and Gibraltar — with the drop falling straight into the Atlantic, kestrels working the updraft, and Morocco visible across the Strait on a clear day. The approach is through the Pinar de la Breña, a dense stone-pine forest that runs right to the cliff edge. Barbate town itself is a plain fishing port, redeemed by being the almadraba tuna capital.","e":8,"drive":"A-48 motorway then a short run into Barbate, all easy. The trail head is reached on a good road with the last stretch through the pinewood; it's a flat, well-marked walking trail (roughly 6–7 km round trip) from the parking area to the tower, not a scramble.","toll":"No toll.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free trailhead parking in the Pinar de la Breña at the Sendero Torre del Tajo access off the Barbate–Caños road; open compacted ground under the pines. In Barbate town, free lots and wide free street parking near the port and Paseo Marítimo.","pd":8,"pb":"Forest trailhead parking is open and shaded, no manoeuvring problem. Barbate town has genuinely easy free parking by the port. Note there are no facilities and no water at the trailhead.","lez":0,"lezn":"About 22,000 inhabitants — no ZBE, none planned.","cats":["nature","coast","foodwine"],"gem":true,"season":"October–May for the walk (the pinewood is brutal in August heat); April–June for almadraba tuna in the port restaurants","one":"Walk the Sendero Torre del Tajo out to the cliff-edge watchtower, then come back into Barbate port for a plate of almadraba tuna where it's actually landed.","book":""},{"n":"El Bosque","r":"Sierra de Grazalema, Cádiz province, Andalusia","c":"Spain","lat":36.758,"lon":-5.506,"h":1.3,"km":98,"b":6,"why":"The gateway village to the Sierra de Grazalema, sitting where the Majaceite river comes out of the mountains — the river runs cold and clear through the village with a trout hatchery on it, and the Sierra de Albarracín rises directly behind. It's a base rather than a spectacle, but the riverside is genuinely lovely and shaded.","e":7,"drive":"A-4 to Jerez, A-382, then the A-372 east into the sierra — the road starts climbing and winding here but El Bosque is at the low end of the range, so the worst of the curves come after it. Note the A-372 between El Bosque and Benamahoma was closed by storm rockfalls in January 2026 and reopened provisionally on 21 February 2026; permanent slope stabilisation is still outstanding and the Junta has warned restrictions could return after heavy weather. Check before a winter trip.","toll":"No toll.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free open parking by the Recinto Ferial and along the riverside access roads near the Área Recreativa; the visitor centre also has free parking.","pd":9,"pb":"Easy — this is a small village with generous open parking and no pressure outside summer weekends. Village lanes are narrow but you never need them.","lez":0,"lezn":"About 2,000 inhabitants — no ZBE.","cats":["village","nature"],"gem":false,"season":"Spring for water in the river; autumn for colour. Note Grazalema is the wettest place in Spain — expect real rain October–March","one":"Walk the Sendero del Río Majaceite from El Bosque to Benamahoma — about 5km one way, flat, following the river through gallery forest with waterwheels and pools, the single best easy walk in the sierra.","book":""},{"n":"Itálica","r":"Santiponce, Seville province","c":"Spain","lat":37.4436,"lon":-6.0453,"h":1.3,"km":126,"b":8,"why":"The amphitheatre held 25,000 — the third largest in the Roman world — and its arena floor and beast pits are open to walk through; it doubled as the Dragonpit in Game of Thrones. The Casa de los Pájaros and Casa del Planetario still have their full-room mosaic floors in situ, in the open air.","e":8,"drive":"All motorway/dual carriageway. Bypass Seville on the SE-30 west side — you never touch the city centre or its APR.","toll":"None. A-4/AP-4 to Seville, then SE-30 and N-630 north.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free public parking directly outside the Conjunto Arqueológico entrance on Avenida de Extremadura, Santiponce. No charge.","pd":9,"pb":"Purpose-built visitor parking, wide bays, no manoeuvring problem at all.","lez":0,"lezn":"Santiponce has no ZBE and is outside Seville's Cartuja zone.","cats":["history","nature"],"gem":false,"season":"October–April. There is almost no shade — avoid summer afternoons entirely.","one":"Stand on the arena floor of the amphitheatre and look up at the cavea, then find the mosaic of the birds in the Casa de los Pájaros.","book":"Free for EU citizens on presentation of ID; €1.50 otherwise. Closed Mondays. No advance booking required but check the Junta de Andalucía site for summer hours (it closes early in July/August heat)."},{"n":"Seville","r":"Seville province, Andalusia","c":"Spain","lat":37.3891,"lon":-5.9845,"h":1.3,"km":121,"b":10,"why":"The Real Alcázar's Patio de las Doncellas and Salón de Embajadores are the finest Mudéjar interiors in Europe, and the Cathedral's Giralda — a 12th-century almohad minaret with a Renaissance belfry bolted on top — is climbed by 34 ramps, not stairs. Plaza de España's 500m tiled crescent and the Metropol Parasol's timber waffle roof bracket four centuries of showing off.","e":8,"drive":"Motorway all the way (A-4/AP-4). The drive is trivially easy; the city is the hard part. Enter via SE-30 and stay on the ring road until you are at your parking target — never navigate through the centre.","toll":"None — the AP-4 Cádiz–Seville motorway lost its tolls in December 2019 and is free.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Free: Charco de la Pava, the huge free open lot across the river past Triana (bus C1/C2 or ~25 min walk to the centre) — the standard local answer. Also free white-line bays on Avenida de la Palmera / Reina Mercedes south of the centre. Paid: Mercado del Arenal (24h, ~€2/h, ~€25/day) two minutes from the Cathedral, or Cano y Cueto by Jardines de Murillo.","pd":4,"pb":"Charco de la Pava is flat open dirt/asphalt — zero manoeuvring stress, ideal for the E320. Central garages like Cano y Cueto have tight spiral ramps and short bays. The Santa Cruz quarter is impassable by car and largely pedestrianised.","lez":1,"lezn":"IMPORTANT NUANCE: Seville's ZBE has been live since 8 Jan 2024 and fining since July 2024, but it covers ONLY Isla de la Cartuja (Cartuja Norte and Cartuja Sur) — NOT the historic centre. It runs Mon–Fri non-holiday 07:00–19:00 only; weekends and evenings are unrestricted. A 2005 diesel is 'sin distintivo' and cannot enter Cartuja on a weekday, but Charco de la Pava, the Cathedral, Alcázar and Triana are all outside it. Separately, the historic centre has an APR (Área de Prioridad Residencial) with camera-controlled entry that applies to ALL cars regardless of emissions — do not drive into it.","cats":["city","history","foodwine"],"gem":false,"season":"March–May and October–November. July–August routinely exceeds 40°C. Avoid Semana Santa and Feria de Abril unless that IS the trip — parking becomes impossible.","one":"Book the Real Alcázar including the Cuarto Real Alto upstairs royal apartments, then walk into the Baños de Doña María de Padilla under the Patio del Crucero.","book":"Real Alcázar tickets sell out days ahead in high season — book online. Cathedral/Giralda also timed-entry. Both bookable 2–3 months out."},{"n":"Benamahoma","r":"Sierra de Grazalema, Cádiz province, Andalusia","c":"Spain","lat":36.7647,"lon":-5.4676,"h":1.4,"km":105,"b":7,"why":"A hamlet of about 400 people built around the Nacimiento del Majaceite — the spring where the river actually emerges from the rock — with old watermills, a trout farm and beds of watercress in the streambed, all under the steep wall of the Sierra del Pinar. The name is Arabic (Banū Mahomet) and it feels like the least-changed settlement in the range.","e":5,"drive":"The A-372 above El Bosque is proper mountain road — continuous curves, gradients, and a rock face on one side. Beautiful but demanding, and this is the stretch that was closed by rockfalls in early 2026 and reopened only provisionally, with containment netting still pending. Avoid in heavy rain.","toll":"No toll.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free parking on the wide verges and small open areas at the village entrance off the A-372, and at the Área Recreativa El Bosque–Benamahoma trailhead.","pd":7,"pb":"Park at the entrance and walk in — the village interior is a set of steep, narrow lanes that a 4.85m sedan should not attempt. Space at the entrance is limited on spring weekends when walkers arrive for the Majaceite trail.","lez":0,"lezn":"None.","cats":["village","nature"],"gem":true,"season":"March–May and September–November","one":"The Nacimiento del Majaceite — walk up to where the river bursts out of the rock face at the top of the village, then follow the old mill leats back down.","book":""},{"n":"Zahara de los Atunes","r":"Barbate, Cádiz province, Andalusia","c":"Spain","lat":36.1381,"lon":-5.8483,"h":1.4,"km":112,"b":7,"why":"A 12km run of pale sand from the ruined Palacio de las Almadrabas — the Duke of Medina Sidonia's 16th-century tuna-fishery compound whose walls still stand in the middle of the village — south past Atlanterra to the Faro de Camarinal. Playa de los Alemanes at the far end is a small cove under low cliffs that is the best-looking beach in the province after Bolonia.","e":8,"drive":"A-48 south then the CA-2216 down to the coast — good road, gently rolling, with the Sierra del Retín on your left. The Atlanterra road beyond the village narrows and climbs a bit as it heads to Los Alemanes, but it's paved throughout.","toll":"No toll.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Free: Aparcamiento Zahara, entered from Calle Quebrantamichos — a large asphalt lot with marked bays, some shade, 24/7, with walkway access straight to the beach. Cheap paid backups: Parking Playa Albatros (Calle Albatros), a dirt lot at just €2/day; Parking Playa Zahara (Calles Extramuros/Ola), attended dirt lot at €10/day Oct–Mar and €18/day Apr–Sep.","pd":7,"pb":"The free Quebrantamichos lot is marked, asphalt and pull-in — ideal. It gets very crowded in July–August, when the €2 Albatros lot becomes excellent value rather than a compromise. Informal attendants work the free lot for tips. Village streets are narrow and packed with parked cars in summer; don't cruise them looking for a space in a 4.85m car.","lez":0,"lezn":"None.","cats":["coast","foodwine","village"],"gem":false,"season":"June and September; May for tuna","one":"Drive on past Atlanterra to Playa de los Alemanes and walk the cove, then eat almadraba tuna back in the village at one of the places built into the old Palacio de las Almadrabas walls.","book":""},{"n":"Benaocaz","r":"Cádiz province, Sierra de Grazalema","c":"Spain","lat":36.6944,"lon":-5.4278,"h":1.5,"km":105,"b":8,"why":"The Barrio Nazarí is an intact fragment of the Moorish street plan — sub-two-metre alleys of whitewashed stone under the Sierra del Caíllo. The Mirador at the top of the village looks straight down the Ubrique valley.","e":5,"drive":"Same A-2302 narrow mountain road as Villaluenga. Short, steep, tight approach into the village.","toll":"None.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free open parking at the village entrance beside the A-2302 and by the Plaza de las Libertades.","pd":7,"pb":"Leave the car at the entrance. The Barrio Nazarí lanes are pedestrian-width; a 4.85m car has no business past the plaza.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE.","cats":["village","nature","history"],"gem":true,"season":"Spring and autumn.","one":"Walk the Calzada Romana down to Ubrique — 4km of genuine Roman paving through the Sierra, one of the best-preserved stretches in Spain.","book":""},{"n":"Olvera","r":"Cádiz province, Sierra de Cádiz","c":"Spain","lat":36.9339,"lon":-5.2622,"h":1.5,"km":110,"b":8,"why":"The neoclassical Iglesia de la Encarnación and the Arab castle keep stand shoulder to shoulder on one rock outcrop above a cascade of white houses — one of the cleanest 'church and castle on a crag' compositions in Andalusia. Olive groves run flat to the horizon in every direction.","e":9,"drive":"The A-382 from Jerez is one of the easiest drives in the region: wide, straight, dual in stretches, almost no traffic. Only the final climb into town has any gradient.","toll":"None. A-382 the whole way — a fast, near-empty road.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free open lot at Plaza del Ayuntamiento / Paseo area and along Avenida Julián Besteiro; free bays near the Vía Verde station.","pd":8,"pb":"Easy. Park on the flat lower town and walk up the 8 minutes to the castle — the upper lanes are cobbled and tight but you never need to drive them.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE.","cats":["village","history","nature"],"gem":false,"season":"Year-round; spring best for the Vía Verde.","one":"Walk or cycle a stretch of the Vía Verde de la Sierra from Olvera station — a disused railway with 30 tunnels and viaducts through a griffon vulture colony at Peñón de Zaframagón.","book":""},{"n":"Vía Verde de la Sierra","r":"Cádiz province","c":"Spain","lat":36.9256,"lon":-5.2861,"h":1.5,"km":112,"b":8,"why":"36km of a railway that was built in the 1920s and never had a train run on it, converted to a car-free greenway with 30 tunnels and 4 viaducts. It threads the Zaframagón gorge past a griffon vulture colony of several hundred birds — the observatory at Peñón de Zaframagón has live nest cameras.","e":9,"drive":"You drive to either trailhead (Olvera station or Puerto Serrano) on easy A-382-linked roads, then walk or cycle. No difficult driving involved.","toll":"None.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free parking at the Estación de Olvera (with a hotel and bike hire) and at the Estación de Puerto Serrano. Both are free open lots.","pd":9,"pb":"Old railway station yards — plenty of flat open space.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE.","cats":["nature","quirky"],"gem":true,"season":"October–May. The tunnels are cool but the open sections have no shade; avoid summer midday. Vultures are most active in spring nesting season.","one":"Hire a bike at Olvera station and ride to the Peñón de Zaframagón vulture observatory (about 15km), through the lit tunnels.","book":"Bike hire at the stations is worth reserving on weekends."},{"n":"Bolonia","r":"Tarifa, Cádiz province, Andalusia","c":"Spain","lat":36.0899,"lon":-5.7742,"h":1.6,"km":133,"b":9,"why":"A complete Roman town — forum, three temples to Jupiter, Juno and Minerva, a temple of Isis, a 2,000-seat theatre and the garum fish-salting vats — standing on the sand facing the beach, with the Rif mountains of Morocco filling the horizon across the Strait. At the western end of the bay, the Duna de Bolonia is a 30m white mobile dune climbing into the pine forest, declared a Monumento Natural.","e":7,"drive":"A-48 becomes N-340 past Tahivilla; the turnoff to Bolonia is a signed spur that drops over a ridge with a handful of switchbacks and one very good viewpoint over the whole bay before descending to the beach. Paved and fine, but narrow and slow with summer traffic. Watch for free-roaming cattle on the N-340 approach.","toll":"No toll.","pf":false,"pp":false,"ps":"Three municipal dirt lots run by Tarifa council at €1.50 for the whole day, card accepted, open 09:00–21:00 with attendants on site 10:30–18:00. Parking 2 is the largest and sits nearest the Baelo Claudia entrance; Parking 1 (central) fills first; Parking 3 is on the western side toward the dune. No overnight stays.","pd":8,"pb":"€1.50 for a full day is trivially cheap and buys open unmarked dirt with no bay lines at all — you simply drive in and stop, which is about as far from parallel parking as it gets. Dusty, no shade, so the car will bake. In July–August arrive before 10:00; parking outside the authorised zones is actively fined.","lez":0,"lezn":"None. But the dune is a protected Monumento Natural with access restrictions on climbing/walking on parts of it — stay on the marked boardwalks.","cats":["coast","history","nature"],"gem":false,"season":"April–June and September–October; midsummer is very hot with no shade on the site","one":"Baelo Claudia at opening time before the heat, standing in the forum with the sea behind the basilica. Entry is €1.50 for non-EU citizens (EU citizens go free with ID).","book":""},{"n":"Grazalema","r":"Sierra de Grazalema, Cádiz province, Andalusia","c":"Spain","lat":36.7597,"lon":-5.367,"h":1.6,"km":118,"b":8,"why":"A white village at 825m tucked directly under the grey limestone cliff of the Peñón Grande, so the rock face fills the sky at the end of the streets. The Plaza de España with its fountain and the surviving handloom blanket workshop (mantas de Grazalema) are the town; the surroundings — the Pinsapar forest of Spanish fir, a relict Ice Age species found in only a handful of places on earth — are the reason to come.","e":4,"drive":"This is the real mountain driving of the cluster. Either the A-372 through Benamahoma or, more spectacularly, over the Puerto de las Palomas at 1,357m — continuous hairpin switchbacks with big drops and no guardrail in places, plus wild goats standing in the road. Utterly gorgeous and genuinely tiring. Fog and ice are real winter risks at the pass.","toll":"No toll.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Free open parking areas on the village approach roads and around the Recinto Ferial; there are also free spaces on the wide bend below the Plaza de España. Trailhead parking for the Pinsapar (Puerto del Boyar / Los Llanos del Rabel) is free unpaved ground on the A-372.","pd":6,"pb":"The village core is steep, narrow and cobbled — park on the outskirts. Spaces are limited and Grazalema is a popular weekend destination, so a Saturday in spring can mean circling. Critically: the best walks here (Pinsapar, El Torreón, Garganta Verde, Llanos del Rabel) are restricted-access and require a free permit from the Junta de Andalucía, applied for in advance. Turning up without one means being turned back.","lez":0,"lezn":"About 2,000 inhabitants — no ZBE.","cats":["village","nature"],"gem":false,"season":"April–June and September–October. It records the highest rainfall in Spain, so winter is genuinely wet","one":"The Pinsapar trail from Grazalema to Benamahoma through the Spanish fir forest — book the free Junta permit weeks ahead; it's the only place in Cádiz you can walk through this relict Ice Age woodland.","book":""},{"n":"Ubrique","r":"Sierra de Grazalema, Cádiz province, Andalusia","c":"Spain","lat":36.6789,"lon":-5.447,"h":1.6,"km":118,"b":6,"why":"A dense white town crammed into a narrow gorge between the Sierra de Ubrique and the Cruz del Tajo, so it appears suddenly as you come over the pass. Above it sits Ocuri, a Roman hilltop settlement with an intact columbarium mausoleum and stretches of cyclopean wall, reached by a signed path. The town's identity is leather — it makes goods for most of the major European luxury houses.","e":5,"drive":"A-373 from Villamartín or the A-2302 over the pass from Grazalema — both are mountain roads with sustained switchbacks; the Puerto de los Acebuches descent into Ubrique is steep with tight bends. Scenic and demanding. The town's own streets are extremely narrow and congested.","toll":"No toll.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Free open parking on the wider avenues at the lower/newer end of town (around Avenida Doctor Solís Pascual) and at the Ocuri visitor centre trailhead. The old quarter has metered and very limited spaces.","pd":6,"pb":"Ubrique is a working town squeezed into a gorge, so central parking is genuinely tight and mostly parallel. Park at the lower end on the wide avenues and walk. The Ocuri site parking is easy open ground.","lez":0,"lezn":"About 16,000 inhabitants — no ZBE.","cats":["village","history","quirky"],"gem":true,"season":"Spring and autumn","one":"The guided visit to the Roman city of Ocuri above the town — the intact columbarium is the best-preserved Roman funerary monument in the province — then buy leather direct from a workshop in town.","book":""},{"n":"Villaluenga del Rosario","r":"Cádiz province, Sierra de Grazalema","c":"Spain","lat":36.7003,"lon":-5.3861,"h":1.6,"km":110,"b":8,"why":"The highest village in Cádiz province (858m), sitting in a limestone bowl beneath the Navazo Alto. Its bullring is carved into bare rock — a stone oval cut straight into the hillside — and the roofless ruined shell of the Iglesia del Salvador stands open to the sky in the middle of the village.","e":5,"drive":"Reached only by the A-2302, a narrow, twisting two-lane road between Ubrique and Grazalema with poor surface in places, blind bends and stone walls. Perfectly doable in a big sedan at 40km/h, but there is nowhere to pass a truck.","toll":"None.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free roadside and open ground at the village entrance by the Centro de Interpretación / the bullring. No charge anywhere.","pd":7,"pb":"Fewer than 500 residents, so space is not the issue — width is. Stop at the entrance; the interior lanes are single-vehicle with no turning circle.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE.","cats":["village","nature","foodwine"],"gem":true,"season":"Spring and autumn. It is cold and often fogbound in winter at 858m.","one":"See the rock-cut bullring (Plaza de Toros), then buy payoyo goat cheese from Quesos Payoyo, the artisan dairy that made the breed famous.","book":""},{"n":"Carmona","r":"Seville province, Los Alcores","c":"Spain","lat":37.4714,"lon":-5.6414,"h":1.7,"km":153,"b":8,"why":"The Puerta de Sevilla is a Carthaginian-Roman-Moorish gate stacked in layers you can walk through, and the Alcázar de la Puerta de Marchena (now a parador) sits on the escarpment edge with the entire Vega del Corbones plain spread 200m below. The Necrópolis Romana has painted Roman tombs cut into the rock, including the vast Tumba del Elefante.","e":8,"drive":"Motorway A-4 the entire way to the base of the hill, then a short climb. Easy.","toll":"None. A-4 via Seville then A-4/N-IV east.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free open parking at the Paseo del Estatuto / Alameda and around the Puerta de Sevilla; free lot beside the Necrópolis Romana on Avenida Jorge Bonsor.","pd":7,"pb":"Park outside the Puerta de Sevilla gate. The intramural streets are narrow and cobbled but the perimeter has plenty of open free space — genuinely fine for a big car.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE (population ~29,000).","cats":["village","history","foodwine"],"gem":true,"season":"Autumn to spring; the plain is furnace-hot in summer.","one":"The Necrópolis Romana — descend into the Tumba de Servilia, a full Roman house-tomb carved out of the rock, then look at the crematorium ustrinum next to it.","book":""},{"n":"Valdevaqueros & Punta Paloma","r":"Tarifa, Cádiz province, Andalusia","c":"Spain","lat":36.0658,"lon":-5.7085,"h":1.7,"km":143,"b":9,"why":"The Duna de Valdevaqueros is an enormous white sand mountain spilling off the headland into a turquoise bay, with hundreds of kitesurf canopies over the water on a levante day and the Moroccan coast dead ahead across the Strait. Punta Paloma next door has natural clay pits at the base of the cliff that people coat themselves in — genuinely odd and genuinely local.","e":8,"drive":"N-340 along the Strait — one of the best driving roads in the region, high above the sea with Africa across the water. The turnoffs down to the beaches are short unpaved or rough-paved spurs. Strong crosswinds here are constant and can be startling; a heavy sedan actually handles them better than most.","toll":"No toll.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Large free unpaved parking areas behind the dune at Valdevaqueros and at the Punta Paloma access; some seasonal attended paid areas operate in high summer at a few euros a day.","pd":8,"pb":"Big open dirt flats — perfect for a large car, no marked bays, straight nose-in parking. The surface is loose sand in places; stay on the compacted tracks or you'll dig in. Extremely windy, so expect sandblasting of the paintwork on a strong levante day.","lez":0,"lezn":"None.","cats":["coast","nature","quirky"],"gem":false,"season":"May–October; a levante wind day for the kite spectacle, a poniente day for calm swimming","one":"Climb the Valdevaqueros dune late in the afternoon and sit at the top watching the kites and the ferries crossing to Tangier.","book":""},{"n":"Jimena de la Frontera","r":"Campo de Gibraltar, Cádiz province, Andalusia","c":"Spain","lat":36.4338,"lon":-5.457,"h":1.75,"km":148,"b":7,"why":"A large Moorish castle-fortress on a long ridge with a distinctive horseshoe-arch gateway, free to enter, looking out over the cork forests of Los Alcornocales, the Campo de Gibraltar and — on clear days — the Rock and the African coast. The Hozgarganta river below has natural swimming pools, and the town is a genuinely lived-in white village rather than a tourist set.","e":6,"drive":"A-381 across Los Alcornocales — fast and scenic — then the A-405 north, a quieter rolling road. Straightforward until the town, whose streets are described bluntly by local guides as 'white, narrow and sloped', with driving through the village 'comprometido' because of the hills and tight lanes.","toll":"No toll.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Llano del Barato — the larger parking area, and the one to aim for with a big car. Secondary option: the lot near Plaza de la Constitución. Both free.","pd":7,"pb":"Local advice is explicit: leave the car in the lower areas and walk up. Llano del Barato is open ground with room. The castle approach on foot is steep but short. The castle is free and open 08:00–20:00 Oct–Mar, 08:00–22:00 Apr–Sep.","lez":0,"lezn":"About 7,000 inhabitants — no ZBE.","cats":["village","history","nature"],"gem":true,"season":"October–May; combine with Los Alcornocales","one":"The free Castillo de Jimena at the top of the ridge — walk in through the horseshoe-arch gate and take in the view across the cork oak forest to Gibraltar and Africa.","book":""},{"n":"Tarifa","r":"Cádiz province, Andalusia","c":"Spain","lat":36.0143,"lon":-5.6044,"h":1.8,"km":150,"b":8,"why":"Continental Europe's southernmost point, where you walk the Isla de las Palomas causeway with the Mediterranean on one side and the Atlantic on the other and Africa 14km away. The walled Moorish old town behind the Puerta de Jerez is a knot of whitewashed lanes around the Castillo de Guzmán el Bueno, and Playa de los Lances runs 7km northwest as a flat empty sheet of sand.","e":8,"drive":"A-4 to Jerez then the A-381 'Ruta del Toro' — a fast dual carriageway across the cork-oak country of Los Alcornocales, genuinely one of the better drives in Andalusia. Then N-340 down the Strait. Long but almost entirely easy. Constant crosswind on the final stretch. The old town inside the walls is very narrow and largely one-way; don't take the car in.","toll":"No toll to drive. Optional fast ferry to Tangier from Tarifa port (about an hour) if he wants to add Morocco — passport required, and check current rules before relying on it.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Free: the Plaza de Toros area (closed Tuesdays for the market), the Recinto Ferial / Tarifa West lot near Playa de los Lances, and the Municipal 'La Gasolinera' lot, about 10 minutes' walk from the old town, cars only. Paid: an attended municipal lot next to the walls (24h), Parking San Sebastián (covered, private, 24h security), and El Trocadero by the walls charging hourly.","pd":7,"pb":"Three decent free lots, all open ground with pull-in parking, all about a 10-minute walk in. In summer arrive before 10:00 or they're gone. Never drive inside the walls — the lanes off Calle Sancho IV el Bravo are barely wider than a car and there is nowhere to turn round.","lez":0,"lezn":"About 18,000 inhabitants — no ZBE, none planned.","cats":["coast","history","city","quirky"],"gem":false,"season":"May–June and September–October; it's windy year-round, which is the point","one":"Walk out along the Isla de las Palomas causeway to the marker at the southernmost point of continental Europe, with two seas meeting either side of you.","book":""},{"n":"Algeciras","r":"Campo de Gibraltar, Cádiz province, Andalusia","c":"Spain","lat":36.1275,"lon":-5.454,"h":1.85,"km":162,"b":4,"why":"Frankly the least attractive destination in this cluster — a huge container port and ferry city. Two things redeem it: the Parque Natural del Estrecho viewpoints just south of town, and the Marismas del Río Palmones, a small estuary reserve with a boardwalk where flamingos, ospreys and spoonbills feed within sight of the refinery, which is a bizarre and rather good juxtaposition. Palmones itself has a strip of estuary-side seafood restaurants locals rate highly.","e":7,"drive":"Easy A-381/A-7 approach, but Algeciras city traffic is heavy and dominated by port lorries, and the road layout is confusing. Palmones is much simpler — signposted off the A-7 with straightforward access.","toll":"No toll. Algeciras is the ferry port for Tangier Med and Ceuta if that's ever of interest.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Free open parking at the Marismas del Río Palmones boardwalk access and along the Palmones seafront; in Algeciras itself, paid underground car parks near the port and Plaza Alta are the realistic option.","pd":7,"pb":"Palmones is easy — open ground by the estuary. Algeciras city centre is not worth the hassle in a big car; if you go, use a garage rather than trying to street-park.","lez":1,"lezn":"Over 120,000 inhabitants so legally required, but Algeciras is publicly reported as one of the most delayed cities in Andalusia on ZBE implementation — nothing operational, no cameras, no restrictions on unlabelled vehicles as of 2026.","cats":["nature","foodwine"],"gem":false,"season":"Autumn and winter for bird migration through the Strait","one":"The boardwalk at the Marismas del Río Palmones at high tide, then grilled fish at one of the estuary-side restaurants in Palmones. Only worth the drive if combined with Gibraltar or Castellar.","book":""},{"n":"Castellar de la Frontera","r":"Campo de Gibraltar, Cádiz province, Andalusia","c":"Spain","lat":36.313,"lon":-5.4551,"h":1.85,"km":158,"b":8,"why":"One of the strangest places in Andalusia: a complete 13th-century Nasrid fortress whose interior is still an inhabited village — people live inside the castle walls, in houses lining the original lanes, with a hotel and craft workshops in the keep. From the ramparts you look down on the Guadarranque reservoir and straight across to the Rock of Gibraltar and the Rif mountains beyond.","e":5,"drive":"A-381 then A-405 and the CA-P-9106 spur up to Castellar Viejo — the final approach is a narrow, twisting climb through cork oak with tight bends and limited passing places. Slow going but paved. Note there are two Castellars: the modern Nuevo Castellar down on the plain and the old fortress up on the hill — set your navigation to the castle, not the town name.","toll":"No toll.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free parking area just outside the fortress gate at the top of the access road — you park on open ground outside the walls and walk in through the gateway.","pd":7,"pb":"Cars cannot enter the fortress village, which is exactly right for a big sedan — the interior lanes are medieval width. The parking outside the gate is open but not large, so on a spring weekend it can be full and you'll be leaving the car further down the narrow access road, which is the only real hassle. Weekday visits are effortless.","lez":0,"lezn":"Around 3,000 inhabitants — no ZBE.","cats":["village","history","quirky","nature"],"gem":true,"season":"Spring and autumn; clear winter days give the sharpest views to Africa","one":"Walk the fortress ramparts of Castellar Viejo at sunset with the Rock of Gibraltar and the Moroccan coast laid out below — then have a drink in the courtyard of the castle hotel inside the walls.","book":""},{"n":"Osuna","r":"Seville province","c":"Spain","lat":37.2378,"lon":-5.1058,"h":1.9,"km":175,"b":8,"why":"Calle San Pedro is lined with 18th-century ducal palaces — the Cilla del Cabildo and the Palacio de los Marqueses de la Gomera have some of the finest Baroque stone facades in Spain. The Colegiata de la Asunción and the University crown the hill, and the old quarry beneath doubled as Daznak's Pit in Game of Thrones.","e":9,"drive":"A-92 dual carriageway with a signed exit straight into town. Flat and simple.","toll":"None. A-4 to Seville then A-92 east.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free parking around the Plaza de Toros and on the wide streets near the Colegiata; free bays throughout the lower town.","pd":9,"pb":"Wide 18th-century streets built for carriages — one of the most big-car-friendly historic towns in Andalusia.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE (population ~17,000).","cats":["city","history","foodwine"],"gem":true,"season":"Autumn to spring; brutal in summer.","one":"The Panteón Ducal under the Colegiata — a private Renaissance burial chapel of the Dukes of Osuna, opened by guided visit only.","book":""},{"n":"Casares","r":"Málaga province, Serranía de Ronda foothills","c":"Spain","lat":36.4442,"lon":-5.2731,"h":2.0,"km":175,"b":9,"why":"The classic 'white avalanche' — houses cascading down a cone-shaped hill crowned by a Moorish castle and the cemetery inside its walls, with the Mediterranean and Gibraltar visible beyond. The Mirador on the road in gives the postcard view without leaving the tarmac.","e":6,"drive":"The MA-8300 up from the coast is 12km of continuous tight curves with a steep drop on one side — well-surfaced but demanding. Slow and steady in a big sedan; there are pull-offs.","toll":"None. A-381 to Los Barrios then A-7 and MA-8300.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free open parking at the village entrance below the church (Camino de la Fuente / near the Plaza de España approach) and free roadside on the Ronda side.","pd":6,"pb":"The lower entrance lots are fine. Everything above Plaza de España is single-track cobble with hairpin turns and no room to reverse — park at the bottom without exception.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE.","cats":["village","nature","history"],"gem":true,"season":"Spring and autumn. Winter days can be startlingly clear for the Africa view.","one":"Walk up through the village to the castle and the cemetery inside the Moorish walls — the graves have the best view of any burial ground in Spain.","book":""},{"n":"Gaucín","r":"Málaga province, Serranía de Ronda","c":"Spain","lat":36.5192,"lon":-5.3178,"h":2.0,"km":158,"b":9,"why":"From the Castillo del Águila you see the Genal valley, the Rock of Gibraltar and the Moroccan Rif in a single sweep — the best long view in the Serranía. The village is a long white ribbon along a ridge with an artist community and almost no coach traffic.","e":5,"drive":"The A-369 through the Genal valley (Gaucín–Algatocín–Benadalid–Ronda) is a superb but demanding cork-oak-and-chestnut mountain road: constant curves, narrow lanes, some rock overhangs. One of the great drives in Spain — but it is work in a 4.85m car. Approach from Jimena on the A-405 is gentler.","toll":"None. A-381 then A-405/A-369.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free roadside parking along the main street (Calle Convento / the A-369 through-road) and free open ground near the Castillo del Águila approach.","pd":7,"pb":"The through-road is reasonably wide by sierra standards, and the village is linear rather than a maze — easier than Casares or Setenil. Side lanes are still no-go.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE.","cats":["village","nature","foodwine"],"gem":true,"season":"October–November for chestnut season in the Genal valley (the 'Bosque de Cobre' turns copper), and spring.","one":"Drive the A-369 Genal valley road in late autumn when the chestnut forests turn copper, stopping at the Castillo del Águila for the Gibraltar–Africa panorama.","book":""},{"n":"Gibraltar","r":"British Overseas Territory (via La Línea de la Concepción, Cádiz province)","c":"United Kingdom / Gibraltar","lat":36.1408,"lon":-5.3536,"h":2.0,"km":172,"b":7,"why":"The Rock itself is a 426m limestone monolith rising vertically out of the sea at the mouth of the Mediterranean — from the Skywalk and O'Hara's Battery on the ridge you look down on two seas, Spain and Morocco simultaneously. Inside it are St Michael's Cave, a natural cathedral-sized chamber, and 55km of siege and WWII tunnels bored through the rock. Main Street is a slightly surreal strip of British pubs and red phone boxes under an African sky.","e":7,"drive":"A-4 to Jerez then the A-381 'Ruta del Toro' dual carriageway through Los Alcornocales — a genuinely enjoyable road — then A-7 to La Línea. Two hours, almost all fast dual carriageway. Historically the border queue was the killer; that changed on 15 July 2026 when the UK–EU Gibraltar treaty took effect and physical frontier controls at the La Línea crossing were removed, moving immigration checks to the airport and seaport instead. Vehicles and pedestrians now cross with no passport check and no queue. Inside Gibraltar the streets are tiny and parking is genuinely scarce — and you'd still be crossing the airport runway.","toll":"No toll. MAJOR 2026 CHANGE — see the low emission zone note: as of 15 July 2026 the land frontier checkpoint is gone.","pf":false,"pp":false,"ps":"Santa Bárbara car park on Avenida Príncipe de Asturias in La Línea — a port-authority lot by Alcaidesa Marina, literally steps from the frontier, about 5 minutes' walk to the border, with no maximum stay, so it suits a full day. Cheaper alternative: La Línea's blue zone street parking from about €1.30/h, Mon–Fri 09:00–18:00 and Sat 10:00–14:00 with a 6-hour cap — and free with no time limit on Sundays.","pd":7,"pb":"Park on the Spanish side and walk in — this remains the right call even now the queue is gone, because Gibraltar's own streets are narrow and its car parks are small and permanently full. Santa Bárbara is an open surface lot with normal bays, easy for a 4.85m car. A Sunday trip gets free unlimited street parking in La Línea. Do not take the sedan up the Rock — the Upper Rock roads are single-track with hairpins and are best done by cable car or taxi tour anyway.","lez":0,"lezn":"Gibraltar has no ZBE and no DGT-label system, so an unlabelled diesel is unrestricted there. La Línea de la Concepción is over 50,000 inhabitants and legally obliged to have a ZBE, but is one of the most delayed municipalities in Andalusia — nothing operational, no cameras, no restrictions as of 2026. The bigger 2026 news is the treaty: from 15 July 2026 Gibraltar joined the Schengen travel area and the EU customs union, land frontier controls were removed, and crossing is now effectively like moving between two towns. Carry a passport anyway.","cats":["city","history","nature","quirky"],"gem":false,"season":"Year-round; spring and autumn for the Upper Rock walking","one":"Cable car to the top, then walk the Upper Rock ridge from the Skywalk down through St Michael's Cave and the Great Siege Tunnels, with Barbary macaques the whole way.","book":""},{"n":"Ronda","r":"Málaga province, Serranía de Ronda","c":"Spain","lat":36.7426,"lon":-5.1669,"h":2.0,"km":146,"b":10,"why":"The Puente Nuevo drops 98m into El Tajo gorge and you can walk down to the Mirador de Aldehuela and the Jardines de Cuenca on the far rim to see it side-on. The Alameda del Tajo balcony hangs straight over a 100m cliff with the whole Serranía behind it.","e":7,"drive":"The comfortable approach from Rota is A-382 to Algodonales then A-384/A-374 — wide, gentle, well-surfaced. AVOID the A-397 from San Pedro de Alcántara (the coast road) unless you want it: 50 minutes of relentless switchbacks with trucks. From Rota you never need it.","toll":"None. A-4/A-382 via Jerez–Arcos–Algodonales, then A-384/A-374. All free.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Free: the San Cristóbal lot on Avenida Ricardo Navarrete (~50 bays, 24h, 15 min walk to the Puente Nuevo) or Plaza Pruna (~40 bays, 10 min walk). Paid: Parking Martínez Astein, ~€1.95/h, ~€13.95/day, wide bays.","pd":6,"pb":"Do not attempt the old town south of the bridge (La Ciudad) — Calle Armiñán narrows and the side lanes are sub-2m. Park north of the gorge and walk. Martínez Astein is the roomiest garage; Plaza del Socorro underground is tight on the ramp for a 4.85m car.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE. Population well under the 50,000 threshold that forces one.","cats":["village","history","nature","foodwine"],"gem":false,"season":"April–June and September–October; brutal midday heat July–August, and coach tours swamp it 11:00–16:00 year-round.","one":"Walk down the Camino de los Molinos path from the Casa del Rey Moro side to the base of El Tajo and look straight up at the Puente Nuevo — the view no coach passenger ever gets.","book":""},{"n":"Setenil de las Bodegas","r":"Sierra de Cádiz, Cádiz province, Andalusia","c":"Spain","lat":36.8637,"lon":-5.1809,"h":2.0,"km":150,"b":8,"why":"Houses built directly into and under a massive overhanging rock shelf carved by the Río Trejo, so entire streets — Cuevas del Sol and Cuevas de la Sombra — have a hundred tonnes of stone as their roof, with bars putting tables under the overhang. There is nothing else quite like it in Spain.","e":5,"drive":"The longest drive in this cluster and right at the two-hour edge. A-382/A-384 then the CA-4222/A-384 through the hills — winding and slow for the last 30km. The village itself is the problem: extremely narrow, steep, one-way streets with the rock overhang literally reducing headroom and width in places. Park on arrival and do not drive into the centre.","toll":"No toll.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Parking Municipal on Avenida de la Diputación — spacious, well-located, free, about 5 minutes' walk to the centre and the best bet for a big car. Also free: Aparcamiento Los Caños on the outskirts, and free street parking on Calle Cuevas del Sol (~500m out), Calle Cuevas de la Sombra, Calle Herrería and Calle Jabonería near the interpretation centre. Paid: Parking Privado El Almendral at €3–5/day, plus a small blue zone in the centre.","pd":6,"pb":"The Avenida de la Diputación municipal lot is the only place to aim for — the rest is street parking on narrow sloped lanes that a 4.85m car will hate. Free lots fill quickly, especially at weekends and in high season; arriving before 11:00 matters here more than almost anywhere else on this list. Genuinely do not attempt the rock-overhang streets in this car.","lez":0,"lezn":"About 2,700 inhabitants — no ZBE. Access restrictions are physical, not regulatory.","cats":["village","quirky","foodwine"],"gem":false,"season":"Spring and autumn; it's a day-trip magnet on summer weekends","one":"Eat lunch at a table on Calle Cuevas del Sol directly under the rock overhang — order the local chorizo and pork, which is what the village is known for besides the geology.","book":""},{"n":"Écija","r":"Seville province","c":"Spain","lat":37.5417,"lon":-5.0819,"h":2.0,"km":186,"b":7,"why":"Called 'the city of towers' — eleven glazed-tile Baroque church steeples rise over a low skyline, best seen from the Mirador at the Torre de San Juan. The Palacio de Peñaflor has a curved painted facade and a wrought-iron balcony that runs the whole length of the building.","e":9,"drive":"Straight off the A-4 motorway. Flat and easy.","toll":"None. A-4 via Seville.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free parking on the Avenida Miguel de Cervantes ring and around the Plaza de España perimeter streets; free bays are plentiful.","pd":8,"pb":"Reasonably wide streets outside the immediate core. Easy.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE (population ~40,000).","cats":["city","history"],"gem":true,"season":"November–March ONLY. Écija is nicknamed 'la sartén de Andalucía' — the frying pan — and regularly records the highest temperatures in Spain, over 46°C.","one":"Climb the Torre de San Juan for the eleven-tower skyline, then see the Roman Amazon mosaic in the Palacio de Benamejí museum.","book":""},{"n":"Acinipo","r":"Málaga province, near Ronda","c":"Spain","lat":36.83,"lon":-5.2497,"h":2.1,"km":155,"b":7,"why":"A Roman theatre from the 1st century BC whose stage wall still stands, built directly into the bedrock on an isolated plateau at 999m — and instead of a fence and a gift shop there are sheep, the wind, and a 360° view across the Serranía to Grazalema. Almost nobody is ever there.","e":7,"drive":"MA-449 north-west from Ronda — a good, quiet two-lane country road across high grassland. The site access track is short and unsurfaced but flat.","toll":"None.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free parking at the site gate on the MA-449. No charge, no attendant beyond opening hours.","pd":9,"pb":"Open field parking. Only concern is the unsurfaced last 100m — fine in the dry, muddy after heavy rain.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE.","cats":["history","nature"],"gem":true,"season":"Spring (wildflowers across the plateau) and autumn. Exposed and windy.","one":"Sit in the rock-cut cavea of the Roman theatre with the Serranía de Ronda behind the stage.","book":"Free entry but limited opening days and hours (typically Wed–Sun mornings, closed Mon/Tue) and it shuts in bad weather. Check with the Ronda tourist office before driving out — people regularly arrive to a locked gate."},{"n":"Ceuta","r":"Ciudad Autónoma de Ceuta","c":"Spain (North Africa)","lat":35.8894,"lon":-5.3213,"h":2.1,"km":175,"b":6,"why":"The Royal Walls (Murallas Reales) with a navigable 16th-century moat — the only one of its kind still in Africa — plus the Casa de los Dragones and a Mediterranean/Atlantic double coastline. It is more curiosity than beauty: a Spanish city with a Moroccan skyline behind it.","e":6,"drive":"Easy 2-hour drive to Algeciras, then a short ferry. Ceuta is Spanish territory so there is no border formality and no vehicle import paperwork — this is the one place you can take the car across with zero hassle. Ceuta is tiny (19 km2) so the car is barely needed once there. The land border with Morocco at Tarajal is a serious bottleneck and taking a foreign car through it triggers full Moroccan import formalities — do not treat Ceuta as a shortcut into Morocco.","toll":"No road tolls. Ferry Algeciras-Ceuta is roughly 1 hour (Baleària, FRS, Trasmediterránea), around €30-40 per foot passenger return and roughly €120-160 for the car return.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Free surface parking along the Muelle Cañonero Dato and the port perimeter; paid underground at Parking Plaza de los Reyes, roughly €1/hr, €12/day.","pd":7,"pb":"Port-side lots are open and easy. The central streets are narrow one-ways with parallel bays — stick to the port area.","lez":0,"lezn":"Ceuta has no ZBE. Spanish ZBE obligations apply to municipalities over 50,000 inhabitants on the mainland; Ceuta has not implemented an enforced zone. No restriction on the 2005 diesel.","cats":["city","quirky","history","coast"],"gem":true,"season":"April-June and September-October","one":"Walk the Royal Walls and take the small boat through the Foso de San Felipe navigable moat; then use the reduced IPSI tax rate to fill the tank and buy electronics cheaply before the ferry back.","book":"Book the ferry ahead if taking the car, especially around Operación Paso del Estrecho (mid-June to mid-September) when Moroccan diaspora traffic overwhelms Algeciras."},{"n":"Cueva de la Pileta","r":"Benaoján, Málaga province, Serranía de Ronda","c":"Spain","lat":36.6919,"lon":-5.2683,"h":2.1,"km":155,"b":9,"why":"Genuine Palaeolithic cave art — 20,000-year-old ochre and charcoal horses, deer, fish and a famous pregnant mare — still shown by the same family who found it in 1905, by paraffin lamp rather than electric light. There is nothing else like this open to the public in Andalusia.","e":5,"drive":"From Ronda take the A-374/MA-7401 through Benaoján toward Cortes de la Frontera. The final approach is a narrow, twisting lane on a hillside with limited passing places. Slow but manageable.","toll":"None.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free car park at the foot of the cave path on the Benaoján–Cortes road. Then 105 steps up, about 5 minutes.","pd":7,"pb":"The lot is small open ground and fine for a big car; the approach lane is the constraint. Reverse into a passing place if you meet a van.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE.","cats":["history","nature","quirky"],"gem":true,"season":"Year-round — constant cave temperature. Combine with Ronda.","one":"See the Palaeolithic pregnant mare by lamplight in the Sala del Pez.","book":"YES — book ahead. Maximum 20 people per tour, ~80 minutes. Tours at 11:00, 12:00, 13:00, 16:00 weekends/holidays; 11:30, 13:00, 16:00 weekdays. €15 adult, €10 child 5–10. Reserve by phone +34 677 610 500 (10:00–13:00) or online at cuevadelapileta.com. No photography inside."},{"n":"El Rocío","r":"Huelva province, Andalusia (Doñana west)","c":"Spain","lat":37.133,"lon":-6.4855,"h":2.15,"km":190,"b":7,"why":"A village with sand streets instead of tarmac, hitching rails outside the houses, and horses ridden through town as normal transport — it looks like a Western set and is entirely genuine. The vast white Ermita del Rocío faces directly onto the Madre de las Marismas, a permanently flooded marsh lagoon that is one of the best free bird hides in Europe: flamingos, glossy ibis, purple swamphens and storks, viewable from the promenade.","e":8,"drive":"Long but easy — A-4 north to Sevilla, A-49 west, then A-483 south. Motorway virtually the whole way, then a flat run through pine and marsh. The village streets are deep sand, which is why cars mostly stay on the perimeter.","toll":"No toll — but note there is no bridge or car ferry across the Guadalquivir mouth, so despite being visible across the water from Sanlúcar, everything on the Huelva side requires driving all the way inland via Sevilla. That's what pushes it past two hours.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free open parking on the hard-surfaced perimeter roads around the village and the large open areas near the Ermita — no charge anywhere.","pd":8,"pb":"Park on the paved perimeter and walk in. Do not drive a low sedan onto the sand streets — they are genuinely soft and cars do get stuck; that's what the horses are for. Avoid entirely during the Romería del Rocío at Pentecost, when a million pilgrims arrive and the area is impassable.","lez":0,"lezn":"None.","cats":["village","nature","quirky"],"gem":true,"season":"December–April when the marsh is flooded and full of birds; avoid Pentecost weekend","one":"Stand on the boardwalk in front of the Ermita at dawn watching flamingos on the Madre de las Marismas, with horses being ridden down the sand street behind you.","book":""},{"n":"Genalguacil","r":"Málaga province, Valle del Genal","c":"Spain","lat":36.5461,"lon":-5.2361,"h":2.2,"km":180,"b":8,"why":"A tiny white village that has been an open-air contemporary art museum since 1994 — sculptures and murals by visiting artists are permanently embedded in the streets, walls and squares, surrounded by chestnut forest. The 'Pueblo Museo' concept means you turn a corner and find a steel figure in a doorway.","e":4,"drive":"Reached by the MA-8304, a genuinely narrow single-track-with-passing-places mountain road off the A-377/A-369. Steep, twisting, and if you meet a van someone reverses. The most demanding approach in this cluster after Villaluenga.","toll":"None.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free open parking at the village entrance by the Museo de Arte Contemporáneo. That is essentially the only parking, and it is free.","pd":6,"pb":"Only about 150 residents so the lot is rarely full, but the approach road is the constraint, not the parking. A 4.85m sedan will fit — it just won't enjoy the last 8km.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE.","cats":["village","quirky","nature"],"gem":true,"season":"Autumn (chestnuts) and spring. The Encuentros de Arte biennial fills the village with new works in even-numbered Augusts.","one":"Walk the whole village hunting the permanent art installations, then eat at the one restaurant on the plaza.","book":""},{"n":"Huelva","r":"Huelva province","c":"Spain","lat":37.2614,"lon":-6.9447,"h":2.2,"km":205,"b":6,"why":"Not a beautiful city, but the Barrio Reina Victoria is a bizarre and genuine curiosity — an entire English garden-suburb of gabled brick cottages built by the Rio Tinto Company for its British staff. The Muelle del Tinto, a curving iron ore-loading pier by Gustave Eiffel's company, runs out into the estuary and is walkable at sunset.","e":9,"drive":"Motorway door to door, flat and fast. One of the easiest drives on the list.","toll":"None. A-4 to Seville then A-49 west.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free street parking is widely available outside the very centre — around the Muelle del Tinto and the Paseo de la Ría, and in the Isla Chica district. Paid underground at Plaza de las Monjas if needed, ~€1.50/h.","pd":8,"pb":"Modern grid city with wide streets — easy for a large car, unusually so for Andalusia.","lez":1,"lezn":"Technically yes but effectively irrelevant to a visitor: Huelva's ZBE launched in 2026 covering only the Barrio Obrero district (8.25 hectares, bounded by Av. Alcalde Federico Molina, Calle Roque Barcia and Av. Guatemala), with a one-year moratorium before fines. The city centre, the Muelle del Tinto and Barrio Reina Victoria are all outside it. A phase two in Centro–San Pedro is under discussion for ~2028.","cats":["city","history","quirky"],"gem":true,"season":"Autumn to spring. Combine with La Rábida and Palos de la Frontera.","one":"Walk out along the Muelle del Tinto at sunset, then drive 10 minutes to the Monasterio de La Rábida and the replica caravels at the Muelle de las Carabelas.","book":""},{"n":"Marbella","r":"Málaga province, Costa del Sol","c":"Spain","lat":36.5108,"lon":-4.885,"h":2.2,"km":205,"b":8,"why":"Plaza de los Naranjos is a 15th-century orange-tree square with the Casa del Corregidor's Gothic-Mudéjar facade on one side, entirely enclosed and pedestrianised — nothing like the glass-and-yacht Marbella of reputation. The Murallas del Castillo, remnants of the Moorish alcazaba, run through the back lanes.","e":8,"drive":"A-381 from Jerez to Los Barrios then A-7 up the coast — a good, fast, free run and one of the shorter drives on this list. The A-7 through Marbella itself gets very congested in summer.","toll":"None if you stay on the free A-7 rather than the AP-7 (which is tolled between Estepona and Málaga).","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Paid: Parking Municipal La Alameda / Parking Avenida del Mar, underground, right beside the old town, ~€2/h. Free: white-line bays north of Avenida Ricardo Soriano in the Divina Pastora / Miraflores residential streets, 10–15 min walk.","pd":5,"pb":"The Casco Antiguo is fully pedestrianised — do not try. The Alameda underground has narrowish bays; the surface lots by the Parque de la Represa are easier for a 4.85m car. Free street bays exist but need parallel.","lez":1,"lezn":"Marbella has over 50,000 inhabitants so is legally obliged to establish a ZBE, and one has been mapped/announced, but as of 2026 it is not enforcing fines against unlabelled vehicles. Verify before travel — status is moving.","cats":["coast","city","history"],"gem":false,"season":"Spring and autumn; the old town is pleasant year-round. August traffic on the A-7 is grim.","one":"Coffee under the orange trees in Plaza de los Naranjos, then walk the lanes to the Ermita de Santiago, the oldest building in town.","book":""},{"n":"Antequera","r":"Málaga province","c":"Spain","lat":37.0194,"lon":-4.5612,"h":2.4,"km":228,"b":8,"why":"The Alcazaba and the Real Colegiata de Santa María sit on a terrace directly facing the Peña de los Enamorados, a mountain shaped exactly like a sleeping human head — and the town's Neolithic dolmens are deliberately aligned on it. The Arco de los Gigantes frames the Peña perfectly.","e":9,"drive":"A-92 dual carriageway all the way, then a well-signed town. It is the crossroads of Andalusia — everything is easy to reach from here.","toll":"None. A-4 to Seville then A-92 east.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free open parking at the Dolmens site car park on the north edge (Avenida de Málaga), and free bays around the Plaza de Toros and Paseo Real. Paid underground at Plaza de San Sebastián if needed, ~€1.30/h.","pd":8,"pb":"Comfortable — Antequera has broad modern avenues around a compact old core. Park low, walk up to the Alcazaba.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE (population ~41,000, under the 50,000 threshold).","cats":["history","city","nature","foodwine"],"gem":false,"season":"Spring and autumn. It's an inland basin — summers are severe.","one":"Stand inside the Dolmen de Menga, a 6,000-year-old chamber roofed by a single 180-tonne capstone, and look out of its entrance, which is aligned on the Peña de los Enamorados rather than the sun.","book":"Dolmens (Menga, Viera, El Romeral) are UNESCO-listed and FREE, closed Mondays — no booking, but check hours, they close midday in summer."},{"n":"Costa de Huelva beaches","r":"Huelva province, Andalusia (Costa de la Luz west)","c":"Spain","lat":37.1305,"lon":-6.8318,"h":2.4,"km":215,"b":6,"why":"The stretch between Mazagón and Matalascañas is the best of it: the Cuesta de Maneli boardwalk descends through the Asperillo cliff, a 100m fossilised orange sand cliff, onto an entirely undeveloped beach inside Doñana. Punta Umbría is a pleasant but ordinary resort on a spit between the Odiel marshes and the sea; Matalascañas itself is a block of 1970s apartment towers dropped on a superb beach.","e":8,"drive":"Easy motorway driving — A-4 to Sevilla, A-49 west, then south — but it is a long haul, and coming back the same way after a beach day makes for a five-hour round trip of driving.","toll":"No toll. The killer is geography: the Guadalquivir has no crossing at its mouth, so this coast — which you can literally see from Sanlúcar — is a 200km+ drive inland via Sevilla.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free unpaved lots at every beach access along the Mazagón–Matalascañas road, including a large one at the Cuesta de Maneli boardwalk; free surface parking throughout Punta Umbría outside July–August.","pd":9,"pb":"Parking is genuinely abundant and free — big open dirt lots at every beach access, easy for any size of car. This is the one area where parking is a non-issue.","lez":0,"lezn":"None in any of these; Huelva city (which you'd bypass) has no operational ZBE either.","cats":["coast","nature"],"gem":false,"season":"June and September","one":"The Cuesta de Maneli boardwalk between Mazagón and Matalascañas — down through the Asperillo fossil dune cliff to a completely empty Doñana beach. Honest verdict: at 2h20–2h40 each way this is beyond the two-hour radius and only makes sense as an overnight, not a day trip. Punta Umbría is further still at roughly 2h35.","book":""},{"n":"Laguna de Fuente de Piedra","r":"Málaga province","c":"Spain","lat":37.1078,"lon":-4.755,"h":2.4,"km":220,"b":8,"why":"The largest natural lake in Andalusia and the biggest greater flamingo breeding colony in the Iberian Peninsula — up to 20,000 pairs nest here, and in spring the far shore is a solid pink band. Hides at the Centro de Visitantes José Antonio Valverde put you close to the water.","e":9,"drive":"Straight off the A-92 dual carriageway with a signed exit. Flat, easy access road to the visitor centre. One of the least demanding drives here.","toll":"None. A-4 then A-92 east, exit at Fuente de Piedra.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free car park at the Centro de Visitantes José Antonio Valverde on the lake edge, plus free parking at the various hides and miradores around the lake.","pd":9,"pb":"Purpose-built free visitor parking on flat ground. No issues at all.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE.","cats":["nature","quirky"],"gem":true,"season":"February–June is the window — flamingos arrive to breed in late winter, chicks are ringed in July, and the lagoon can dry out completely by late summer leaving almost nothing. Do not go in August/September without checking water levels.","one":"The Mirador Cantarranas boardwalk and the visitor centre hides during the spring breeding season, with thousands of flamingos in one view.","book":"Free entry, no booking, but the visitor centre has limited winter hours. Check water levels and flamingo presence at the Junta's Ventana del Visitante site before driving 2.4h — an empty lagoon is a wasted day."},{"n":"Almodóvar del Río","r":"Córdoba province","c":"Spain","lat":37.8106,"lon":-5.0203,"h":2.5,"km":235,"b":8,"why":"A near-perfect crenellated hilltop castle of Moorish origin, restored in the early 20th century into the most complete-looking fortress in Andalusia — it played Highgarden and Casterly Rock in Game of Thrones. It stands alone on a conical hill over the Guadalquivir with 360° views to Córdoba.","e":8,"drive":"A-431 west from Córdoba along the river — a pleasant, easy two-lane road. The castle access road up is short and paved.","toll":"None. A-4 then A-431 along the Guadalquivir.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free parking at the castle car park at the top of the access road, and free in the village below.","pd":8,"pb":"Purpose-built castle parking with room. The village below is narrow but you don't need it.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE. Well outside Córdoba's zone — reach it on the A-431 without entering the city.","cats":["history","village"],"gem":true,"season":"Autumn to spring; very exposed in summer.","one":"Walk the full circuit of the battlements and towers — you can climb almost every one, unusually for a Spanish castle.","book":"Ticketed; night-time and themed guided visits need booking. Check castillodealmodovar.com for hours, it closes some weekdays in winter."},{"n":"Mijas Pueblo","r":"Málaga province, Costa del Sol","c":"Spain","lat":36.5958,"lon":-4.6375,"h":2.5,"km":232,"b":7,"why":"A white village at 428m with the Mediterranean and, on clear days, Gibraltar and Morocco laid out below from the Mirador at the Muralla gardens. The rock-carved Ermita de la Virgen de la Peña was hollowed out by a Mercedarian monk in the 1600s. It is polished and touristy but the setting is genuinely superb.","e":7,"drive":"A-7 coastal dual carriageway then a 7km climb on the A-387 from Fuengirola — wide, well-graded switchbacks with good sightlines. Easy compared to most sierra roads.","toll":"None if you use the free A-7 rather than the AP-7.","pf":false,"pp":false,"ps":"Parking Municipal de Mijas (Avenida del Compás, underground, right at the village entrance), roughly €1.50/h with a daily cap around €12–15. Free alternative: white-line bays further down Avenida de México / Camino de Coín, 10–15 min walk uphill.","pd":6,"pb":"The Compás underground garage is modern with reasonable bay widths but a tight spiral ramp. The village itself is pedestrianised — you park at the edge either way, which suits a big car.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE currently active in Mijas. The municipality exceeds 50,000 so one is legally required eventually — currently 'planned' with no enforcement.","cats":["village","coast"],"gem":false,"season":"Year-round; it's cooler than the coast in summer. Mornings before the coach tours.","one":"The Mirador on the Muralla with Gibraltar and the Rif on the horizon, then the rock-cut Ermita de la Virgen de la Peña.","book":""},{"n":"Ardales & the turquoise lakes","r":"Málaga province","c":"Spain","lat":36.8783,"lon":-4.8464,"h":2.6,"km":230,"b":8,"why":"Three turquoise reservoirs — Conde de Guadalhorce, Guadalteba and Gaitanejo — ringed by pine and pale rock, with the Mirador del Embalse del Conde looking down on water the colour of a swimming pool. Ardales village itself climbs to a Mozarabic church-castle, the Iglesia de la Virgen de los Remedios, built inside the old alcazaba.","e":7,"drive":"A-357 then A-357/A-343. The MA-5403 lakeside road between Ardales and El Chorro is one of the prettiest short drives in Málaga province — narrow but paved, with pull-offs.","toll":"None.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free open parking at the Mirador and the Área Recreativa El Chorro / Conde de Guadalhorce lakeshore; free bays in Ardales village around Plaza de San Isidro.","pd":8,"pb":"Lakeside lots are open dirt/gravel, easy. Ardales village core is tight but the perimeter is fine.","lez":0,"lezn":"None.","cats":["nature","village"],"gem":true,"season":"April–June and September. The reservoirs are the summer swimming spot for inland Málaga.","one":"Book the Cueva de Ardales (Cueva de Doña Trinidad), a decorated Palaeolithic cave with Neanderthal-era ochre markings — guided visits only, and far less known than Nerja.","book":"Cueva de Ardales requires advance booking through the Ardales town hall / Museo de la Historia y las Tradiciones — small groups, limited days."},{"n":"Cazalla de la Sierra","r":"Seville province, Sierra Norte de Sevilla","c":"Spain","lat":37.9297,"lon":-5.7581,"h":2.6,"km":230,"b":7,"why":"A whitewashed sierra town famous for its anise and cherry liqueurs, with the ruined Cartuja de Cazalla — a 15th-century Carthusian monastery restored as an art centre — hidden in a wooded valley 3km out. The Sierra Norte around it is a UNESCO biosphere of holm oak and granite.","e":7,"drive":"Easy motorway to Seville then a good two-lane climb north into the Sierra Morena. Quiet roads.","toll":"None. A-4 to Seville then A-8002/A-432 north.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free street parking around Plaza Mayor and the Paseo del Moro; free lot at the Cartuja.","pd":8,"pb":"Ample space, wide-ish streets. No problem.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE.","cats":["village","foodwine","nature"],"gem":true,"season":"Spring and autumn; the Sierra Norte is a good escape from Seville's summer.","one":"Taste the anís and guinda (cherry liqueur) at a Cazalla distillery, then visit the Cartuja de Cazalla monastery-hotel-art-centre in its valley.","book":""},{"n":"Minas de Riotinto","r":"Huelva province, Cuenca Minera","c":"Spain","lat":37.6931,"lon":-6.5947,"h":2.6,"km":232,"b":8,"why":"The Río Tinto runs blood-red and acid-orange through terraced ochre spoil heaps — NASA studies it as an Earth analogue for Mars because of its extremophile bacteria. Corta Atalaya is a 1,200m-wide open-cast pit, once the largest in Europe, and you can look straight down into its concentric benches from the mirador.","e":8,"drive":"A-66 motorway then good two-lane roads through the dehesa and mining country. Easy driving; the landscape turns lunar in the last 15km.","toll":"None. A-66 then N-435/A-461.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free parking at the Museo Minero in Minas de Riotinto, at the Corta Atalaya mirador, and at Peña de Hierro. All free open ground.","pd":9,"pb":"Wide open mining-town parking — no constraints whatsoever.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE.","cats":["quirky","nature","history"],"gem":true,"season":"Autumn to spring. The colours are most intense in low sun; summer here is extremely hot and shadeless.","one":"Ride the Ferrocarril Minero — restored Victorian carriages on the old ore line for 12km alongside the red river. It runs limited days.","book":"Parque Minero de Riotinto combines the Museo Minero, the Casa 21 English colonial house, Peña de Hierro mine, Corta Atalaya viewing and the tourist train — each is separately ticketed and the TRAIN and Peña de Hierro run on fixed timetables with limited departures. Book via parquemineroderiotinto.es before driving out; Corta Atalaya interior visits are especially limited."},{"n":"Caminito del Rey","r":"Desfiladero de los Gaitanes, Ardales/Álora, Málaga province","c":"Spain","lat":36.9337,"lon":-4.7855,"h":2.7,"km":238,"b":10,"why":"A metre-wide boardwalk pinned to a vertical wall 100m above the Guadalhorce river through the Desfiladero de los Gaitanes, a gorge that narrows to 10m across. The glass-floored balcony and the swaying footbridge over the chasm at the end are the payoff; the rotted remains of the original 1905 concrete walkway hang below you the whole way.","e":7,"drive":"A-357 then A-343/MA-5403 — good roads through the Guadalhorce valley, with a scenic reservoir section past El Chorro. The lanes right around El Chorro station are narrow and get congested at entry times.","toll":"None.","pf":false,"pp":false,"ps":"North access: the Puerto de las Atalayas car park on the Ardales side, ~€2/day. South access: parking near El Chorro / Estación El Chorro, similar. Shuttle bus between the two ends ~€2.50 per leg — bring coins.","pd":6,"pb":"Open surface lots, no width problem, but capacity is genuinely limited — arrive before 09:00 on a spring weekend. There is no overflow field and roadside parking gets ticketed.","lez":0,"lezn":"None.","cats":["nature","history","quirky"],"gem":false,"season":"March–May and September–November. Closes in high wind/rain. Summer is punishing on exposed sections.","one":"The final glass-floor balcony and the hanging bridge over the Gaitanejo gorge.","book":"MANDATORY ADVANCE BOOKING. Tickets are timed, one-way only (north to south), and release on caminitodelrey.info; weekends and holidays sell out weeks ahead. Walk-ups at the visitor centre are unreliable — the centre is not the primary sales point. Budget 3–4 hours total including the shuttle. Minimum age 8. Helmets provided."},{"n":"Córdoba","r":"Córdoba province, Andalusia","c":"Spain","lat":37.879,"lon":-4.7794,"h":2.7,"km":250,"b":10,"why":"The Mezquita's hypostyle hall — 856 columns under double-tiered red-and-white horseshoe arches, with a full Renaissance cathedral erupting through the middle of it — has no equivalent anywhere. The Calleja de las Flores frames the bell tower down a two-metre alley, and the Puente Romano crosses to the Calahorra tower past the Molinos del Guadalquivir.","e":8,"drive":"Long but entirely dull motorway. Approach on the A-4 and use the Ronda de los Tejares / Vial Norte ring rather than aiming at the Judería.","toll":"None. A-4 north via Seville then A-4 to Córdoba, all free motorway.","pf":"mixed","pp":true,"ps":"Free: white-line street bays on Avenida de Fray Albino across the river in the Campo de la Verdad / Miraflores district — 15–20 min walk over the Puente Romano to the Mezquita, and it is the local trick. Paid: Parking Mezquita on Calle Cabezas or SABA/Parking Vial Norte, roughly €2/h, €18–22/day.","pd":4,"pb":"Fray Albino free parking is street parking, which means parallel — but the bays there are long and unpressured, not the tight-city kind. If he refuses to parallel, Parking Vial Norte (Avenida del Gran Capitán area) has wide modern bays and a 20-minute walk in.","lez":2,"lezn":"YES — active and fining since 17 Feb 2023, 24 hours a day, 15 camera-controlled entry points (Puerta del Puente, Tendillas, San Pablo, Cárcamo, etc.). It maps onto the old ACIRE historic-centre restricted zone. A 2005 diesel is 'sin distintivo' and is barred; fine €200 (€100 if paid within 20 days). Vehicles heading to a registered car park inside the zone are exempted, but the safe play is to park across the river on Fray Albino and walk the Roman bridge — free, legal, and a better arrival anyway.","cats":["city","history","foodwine"],"gem":false,"season":"March–May (the Patios festival is the first two weeks of May) and October. Córdoba is the hottest city in Spain — 45°C+ in July/August is normal.","one":"Enter the Mezquita free between 08:30 and 09:30 Monday–Saturday, before the tour groups, and walk the forest of columns in near-silence.","book":"Mezquita-Catedral: €15 general (raised from €13 on 1 April 2026). Free entry 08:30–09:30 Mon–Sat, individuals only, no guides. Book online for weekends/holidays. Alcázar de los Reyes Cristianos also timed."},{"n":"El Torcal de Antequera","r":"Málaga province","c":"Spain","lat":36.9536,"lon":-4.5464,"h":2.7,"km":245,"b":9,"why":"A 1,300m limestone plateau eroded into thousands of stacked-pancake rock towers — the Tornillo ('screw'), the Camello, the Sombrerillo — separated by grassy corridors. Nothing else in Europe looks like it. On clear days you can see the Rif mountains of Morocco from the Mirador de las Ventanillas.","e":6,"drive":"A-7075 south from Antequera then the 4km MA-9016 spur, a steep, narrow, hairpinned single climb up onto the plateau. Paved and fine, but there is no run-off and it fogs in fast.","toll":"None.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free car park at the Centro de Visitantes Torcal Alto at the top. On high-influx days it closes to private cars and a shuttle runs from the lower barrier at ~€2 return.","pd":7,"pb":"The upper lot is open gravel/asphalt with generous space. The risk isn't manoeuvring, it's the lot being full — arrive before 10:00 on spring weekends and holidays or you will be turned back at the barrier and put on the shuttle.","lez":0,"lezn":"None.","cats":["nature","quirky"],"gem":false,"season":"April–June and September–October. Spectacular in low winter sun; genuinely dangerous in summer midday heat with no shade.","one":"Walk the Ruta Amarilla (yellow route, ~2.5km, 1.5h) — it threads between the tallest rock stacks and past the Tornillo, and it's a loop from the visitor centre.","book":"No entry ticket needed and both entry and parking are free — but on peak days private vehicle access to the top is restricted and the shuttle is compulsory. Email reservas@torcaldeantequera.com or call +34 952 243 324 to check restriction dates before driving 2.7h."},{"n":"Medina Azahara","r":"Córdoba province","c":"Spain","lat":37.8874,"lon":-4.8672,"h":2.7,"km":255,"b":9,"why":"A caliphal palace-city built in 936 and destroyed within 75 years, terraced down a hillside — the reconstructed Salón Rico of Abd al-Rahman III has carved marble panels of the Tree of Life covering entire walls. From the upper terrace you look down over the whole excavated grid to the Guadalquivir valley.","e":8,"drive":"A-431 west out of Córdoba then a signed side road up to the museum. Easy road, but note the museum is 8km from Córdoba city and well OUTSIDE the ZBE — you never enter the restricted zone.","toll":"None.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free visitor car park at the Museo / Centro de Interpretación (the modern half-buried building). Ample, purpose-built.","pd":9,"pb":"Modern coach-sized car park. Zero difficulty — one of the easiest parks on this whole list.","lez":0,"lezn":"Outside Córdoba's ZBE. Reach it on the A-431 without entering the city centre.","cats":["history","nature"],"gem":false,"season":"October–April. Closed Mondays. Very exposed — no summer middays.","one":"The Salón Rico (Salón de Abd al-Rahman III) with its reconstructed marble ataurique carving.","book":"You CANNOT drive your own car to the ruins — a shuttle bus from the museum is the only authorised access for the final 1.5km uphill, ~€2.50–3.00 return, every 15–20 min. Site entry free for EU citizens with ID. Closed Mondays; last access 45 min before closing."},{"n":"Aracena","r":"Huelva province, Sierra de Aracena y Picos de Aroche","c":"Spain","lat":37.8925,"lon":-6.5606,"h":2.8,"km":250,"b":9,"why":"The Gruta de las Maravillas runs 1,200m directly under the castle hill through twelve chambers and six underground lakes — the Sala de los Desnudos and the mirror-still Baño de la Sultana are the standouts, and it was the first show cave opened in Spain (1914). Above it the Iglesia del Castillo, a Templar-era church, tops the hill with the whole chestnut-and-oak Sierra beyond.","e":7,"drive":"A-66 motorway then the N-433, a good sweeping two-lane road through the dehesa. The Sierra roads beyond Aracena (A-470 to Alájar, HU-8105) are narrower but scenic and paved.","toll":"None. A-4 to Seville then N-433/A-66 north-west.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free open parking on Avenida de Portugal / around the Plaza de San Pedro and the bus station area, 5 minutes from the Gruta entrance. Free roadside on the ring streets.","pd":8,"pb":"Aracena is a proper market town with room. Only the streets right around Plaza Alta are tight, and you don't need them.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE.","cats":["nature","village","foodwine","quirky"],"gem":false,"season":"Autumn (chestnuts, mushrooms, mild) and spring. The cave is a constant 16–19°C year-round, which makes it a great summer refuge.","one":"The Baño de la Sultana chamber inside the Gruta de las Maravillas, where the lake reflects the ceiling perfectly.","book":"Gruta de las Maravillas runs on fixed timed guided tours with limited group size — buy online at turismoaracena.sacatuentrada.es, especially for weekends and holidays. Photography inside is prohibited. Book before making the 2.8h drive."},{"n":"Málaga","r":"Málaga province, Costa del Sol","c":"Spain","lat":36.7213,"lon":-4.4214,"h":2.8,"km":265,"b":8,"why":"The Alcazaba and the Castillo de Gibralfaro are joined by a walled corridor up the hill, and from Gibralfaro you look straight down into the Roman theatre, the cathedral and the port in one frame. La Manquita — the cathedral with only one finished tower — dominates a walkable, palm-lined old town.","e":7,"drive":"Inland A-92 then A-45 over the Puerto de las Pedrizas — a long, fast, sometimes windy descent to the coast. Urban approach is busy but well signed.","toll":"None on the A-92/A-45 inland route. The AP-7 along the coast is tolled — avoid it, you don't need it from Rota.","pf":"mixed","pp":true,"ps":"Free: white-line street bays in El Ejido / Barrio Conde de Ureña (10 min walk to Plaza de la Merced) or the larger, easier La Rosaleda–Martiricos area by the stadium (15–20 min walk), which has the biggest supply. Paid: Aparcamiento Alcazaba (389 spaces, easiest access, right by Plaza de la Merced) or Parking Plaza de la Marina (450 spaces, 24h), ~€2.20/h, ~€28/day.","pd":4,"pb":"Free street parking here means parallel on a hill in El Ejido — not his thing. Parking Alcazaba is the pragmatic answer: modern, wide, and 10 seconds from the old town. Never drive into the old town grid.","lez":2,"lezn":"YES — active with camera enforcement over a 404-hectare central zone. A 2026 TSJA court ruling struck down the article that let Málaga-registered cars in regardless of label (as discriminatory), but the ZBE itself remains in force and is issuing €200 fines (€100 early payment). An out-of-town unlabelled 2005 diesel is currently NOT permitted in the central zone. The city may revise the ordinance or appeal — verify status the week you go at movilidad.malaga.eu. Safest plan: park in Martiricos/Rosaleda north of the centre, outside the perimeter, and walk or take the metro in.","cats":["city","coast","history","foodwine"],"gem":false,"season":"Year-round — the mildest winter city on this list. Avoid August.","one":"Walk the covered ramp from the Alcazaba up to the Castillo de Gibralfaro at sunset for the view down into the Roman theatre and the port.","book":"Museo Picasso and the Centre Pompidou are walk-in most days; no critical advance booking."},{"n":"Zufre","r":"Huelva province, Sierra de Aracena","c":"Spain","lat":37.8353,"lon":-6.3364,"h":2.8,"km":248,"b":8,"why":"The village sits on a cliff edge and its Paseo — a stone balcony walkway with arched seating built on the old Almohad walls — hangs directly over a 200m drop to the Zufre reservoir and the dehesa. The Iglesia del Salvador incorporates the old alcázar, and the town hall has a Moorish-era vaulted undercroft.","e":6,"drive":"Good road as far as the turnoff, then a narrow, winding, lightly-trafficked climb. Empty roads — you may not pass another car.","toll":"None. A-66 north then N-433 / HU-9105.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free parking on the entrance road and around the Plaza de la Constitución. No charge anywhere.","pd":8,"pb":"Very few visitors so space is easy; the streets are narrow but the entrance area is open.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE.","cats":["village","nature","history"],"gem":true,"season":"Spring and autumn.","one":"Sit on the arched stone Paseo balcony over the cliff with the reservoir 200m below — an almost unvisited version of the Ronda experience.","book":""},{"n":"Alcoutim","r":"Algarve / Baixo Guadiana","c":"Portugal","lat":37.4708,"lon":-7.4711,"h":2.9,"km":260,"b":8,"why":"A single street of whitewashed houses on the Guadiana with a ruined castle above, staring directly across 300m of green river at its Spanish mirror-twin, Sanlúcar de Guadiana. No bridge, no traffic, and the river gorge upstream is one of the emptiest landscapes in Iberia.","e":8,"drive":"The IC27 from Vila Real de Santo António is a well-surfaced, near-empty dual/single carriageway through cistus scrub. The descent into Alcoutim itself is a steep narrow lane. Remember Portugal is an hour behind Spain — this matters because the zip line crosses the time zone.","toll":"Avoid the A22 entirely: cross at Ayamonte then take the free N122/IC27 north along the Guadiana. Zero tolls if you route this way. Alternatively drive the Spanish bank on the A-499 to Sanlúcar de Guadiana and take the little ferry across (about €2 per person).","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free riverside car park on the quay below the castle (Cais de Alcoutim). Free, unmetered, no time limit.","pd":9,"pb":"Flat quayside lot, easy. The village lanes above are impassable for this car — do not try.","lez":0,"lezn":"None.","cats":["village","nature","quirky"],"gem":true,"season":"March-May and October; the inland Guadiana is furnace-hot in July-August","one":"The Limite Zero cross-border zip line — 720m from Sanlúcar de Guadiana (Spain) to Alcoutim (Portugal), the only zip line in the world that crosses an international border and a time zone. About €20, and you land an hour earlier than you left.","book":"Book the Limite Zero zip line online in advance — it runs limited slots and closes in high wind."},{"n":"Alájar","r":"Huelva province, Sierra de Aracena","c":"Spain","lat":37.8722,"lon":-6.6603,"h":2.9,"km":262,"b":9,"why":"The Peña de Arias Montano is a rocky spur above the village holding a 16th-century hermitage where Philip II's librarian retreated to study — the balcony looks over the entire Sierra de Aracena, an endless rolling sea of holm oak and chestnut. Alájar itself below is one of the best-preserved villages in the range, with cobbled streets and no modern edge.","e":6,"drive":"A-470 west from Aracena — a lovely, narrow, twisting sierra road through chestnut woods. Well-surfaced two-lane but with tight bends and stone walls.","toll":"None.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free open parking at the Peña de Arias Montano beside the hermitage, and free parking at the entrance to Alájar village.","pd":8,"pb":"The Peña lot is generous open ground. Alájar's cobbled centre is tight but you park before entering it.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE.","cats":["village","nature","history"],"gem":true,"season":"October–November (chestnut and mushroom season) and April–May.","one":"The view from the Peña de Arias Montano balcony over the sierra, then walk the old cobbled mule path down into Alájar.","book":""},{"n":"Linares de la Sierra","r":"Huelva province, Sierra de Aracena","c":"Spain","lat":37.8825,"lon":-6.6108,"h":2.9,"km":258,"b":8,"why":"Every doorway in the village has a hand-laid pebble mosaic 'llanos' in front of it, each different — a folk-art tradition unique to this valley. The village square doubles as the bullring, and the communal stone washhouse still stands at the bottom of the street.","e":6,"drive":"Off the A-470 by a short steep spur road into the valley. Narrow but brief.","toll":"None.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free parking on the road at the village entrance — the only option and it's free.","pd":7,"pb":"Around 250 inhabitants; the entrance verge is the car park. Do not descend into the village lanes — they are cobbled, steep and single-width.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE.","cats":["village","quirky","foodwine"],"gem":true,"season":"Autumn and spring.","one":"Walk the streets looking at the pebble-mosaic doorstep patterns, then eat at Arrieros — a serious restaurant hidden in a 250-person village.","book":"Arrieros restaurant needs a reservation and closes several months a year."},{"n":"Tangier","r":"Tanger-Tétouan-Al Hoceïma","c":"Morocco","lat":35.7595,"lon":-5.834,"h":2.9,"km":220,"b":7,"why":"A white amphitheatre of a city on the Strait where the Atlantic and Mediterranean meet — from the Kasbah terrace you see Spain across 14km of water. The Grand Socco, the Petit Socco cafés where Bowles and Burroughs sat, and Cap Spartel's lighthouse and Caves of Hercules 14km west.","e":4,"drive":"FERRY, and this is the key decision. Two options: (1) TARIFA to TANGER VILLE — 1 hour crossing, Baleària and AML, roughly 8 sailings a day each, lands you directly in the city centre. Foot passenger from about €37 one way / €75 return. (2) ALGECIRAS to TANGER MED — 1h30, ~21 sailings/day, but the port is 40km east of the city. STRONG RECOMMENDATION: LEAVE THE CAR IN SPAIN. Taking the car costs roughly €230-500 return, and Tangier city driving is aggressive, chaotic, and the medina is impassable. Park in Tarifa, walk on, and use petits taxis (10-20 MAD) or a hired driver in Morocco. Passport required (a Spanish DNI or military ID is not enough); US and EU citizens get 90 days visa-free. If he is active-duty US Navy, Morocco travel normally requires an approved leave/APACS travel clearance from his command — check before booking.","toll":"Spanish side is toll-free (A-48/N-340 or A-381 to Algeciras/Tarifa). Moroccan motorways are tolled but cheap — Tanger Med to Tangier is about 20 MAD (€2).","pf":false,"pp":false,"ps":"IN TARIFA (leaving the car): Parking del Puerto beside the ferry terminal, about €22/24h; Parking San Sebastián about €15-18/24h, 10 min walk; free street parking on Calle Mar de la Plata and by the Polideportivo outside peak season. IF taking the car to Morocco: guarded lots near the Tanger Ville port and the Grand Socco, roughly 20-40 MAD/day plus a tip to the gardien.","pd":5,"pb":"Tarifa's port lot is easy. Moroccan guarded lots are cramped and attendants will expect to move your car — a 4.85m Mercedes attracts attention. Another reason to leave it in Spain.","lez":0,"lezn":"No low-emission zones in Morocco. In Spain, note that neither Tarifa nor Algeciras has an enforced ZBE, so the drive down is unrestricted.","cats":["city","coast","history","foodwine"],"gem":false,"season":"March-May and September-November. Avoid Ramadan for restaurant hours (check dates); August is hot and crowded","one":"Mint tea on the Café Hafa terrace, cut into the cliff above the Strait, with Spain visible across the water — open since 1921.","book":"Book the ferry online (Baleària, AML, FRS) — walk-up is possible but summer sailings fill. If taking the car, book the vehicle space well ahead."},{"n":"Tavira","r":"Algarve","c":"Portugal","lat":37.1258,"lon":-7.6503,"h":2.9,"km":270,"b":8,"why":"An 18th-century Roman-footed bridge over the Gilão, 20-plus church towers and Moorish-style four-water pyramid roofs stacked up to a castle garden with bougainvillea over the ramparts. The salt pans east of town turn pink at sunset and hold flamingos.","e":9,"drive":"Motorway the whole way: A-491/A-4 to Sevilla, A-49 to Ayamonte, cross the Guadiana bridge, A22 west. Nothing technical. Portugal is UTC-1, so you gain an hour going west and lose it coming home. Border is a non-stop drive-through (Schengen).","toll":"A22 Via do Infante is electronic-only (no booths). Register the Spanish plate + a credit card at the EASYToll machine immediately after the Guadiana bridge at Castro Marim — €1.06 activation, valid 30 days, tolls auto-charged. Ayamonte-Tavira is roughly €2.50. Free alternative: the parallel N125, slow and full of roundabouts.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Free gravel lot on Rua Dr. José Pires Padinha by the riverfront market (Mercado da Ribeira), plus a second free earth lot by the municipal market on Rua Almirante Cândido dos Reis. Metered bays in the centre run about €0.60/hr, free after 19:00 and Sundays.","pd":8,"pb":"The riverside gravel lots are open bays, easy to nose in with a 4.85m car. Do not attempt the cobbled lanes above the castle — they narrow to under 2.5m.","lez":0,"lezn":"None. Portugal has no national emissions-sticker scheme and only Lisbon and Porto have ZERs. Tavira has no restriction whatsoever on a 2005 diesel.","cats":["village","coast","history","foodwine"],"gem":false,"season":"April-June and September-October; August is packed with Portuguese holidaymakers","one":"Take the ferry from Quatro Águas out to Ilha de Tavira and walk the dune boardwalk to the near-empty east end of the barrier island.","book":"Nothing required. Ilha de Tavira ferries are turn-up-and-go and run roughly every 30-60 min in summer, sparse in winter."},{"n":"Tétouan","r":"Tanger-Tétouan-Al Hoceïma","c":"Morocco","lat":35.5711,"lon":-5.3724,"h":2.9,"km":220,"b":8,"why":"A UNESCO-listed medina that is the most complete surviving Andalusi city in North Africa — built by refugees expelled from Granada, with distinctly Spanish-Moorish whitewash and ironwork rather than the ochre of the south. The Ensanche district outside the walls is Spanish colonial art deco.","e":4,"drive":"Ferry plus a short, good motorway run from Tanger Med (about 45 min). Easier than Chefchaouen. Tétouan gets far fewer tourists than Tangier or Chefchaouen, which cuts both ways — fewer hustlers, less infrastructure. Same ferry and vehicle-import considerations as Tangier; leaving the car in Spain remains the sane option.","toll":"Spanish side free. Tanger Med-Tétouan motorway toll about 15 MAD.","pf":false,"pp":false,"ps":"Guarded lots around Place Hassan II and Place Moulay el Mehdi in the Ensanche, roughly 20-30 MAD/day plus a tip. Free street parking exists in the Ensanche grid but attracts informal attendants who will expect payment anyway.","pd":5,"pb":"The Ensanche has wide Spanish-planned boulevards — actually the most big-car-friendly Moroccan town on this list. The medina is entirely pedestrian.","lez":0,"lezn":"None in Morocco.","cats":["city","history","quirky"],"gem":true,"season":"March-May and September-November","one":"The Musée Ethnographique in the Bab el-Okla bastion, then get lost in the tanners' and coppersmiths' quarters of the medina — still working, not staged for tourists.","book":"Ferry only."},{"n":"Almonaster la Real","r":"Huelva province, Sierra de Aracena","c":"Spain","lat":37.8722,"lon":-6.7869,"h":3.0,"km":275,"b":9,"why":"It has the only substantially intact rural mosque in Spain — a 10th-century mezquita with five horseshoe-arch naves and its original mihrab, built on a Visigothic basilica and never converted beyond a small apse. A bullring is built directly against its outer wall. The village below is a tumble of white houses in chestnut forest.","e":6,"drive":"N-433 then the N-435/HU-8114 — good roads to the turnoff, then a narrower winding lane into the village. Nothing frightening, just slow.","toll":"None.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free parking at the village entrance and on the road up towards the mosque/castle; free roadside throughout. No paid parking exists.","pd":7,"pb":"Park at the bottom and walk the 10 minutes up to the mezquita. The lane up is narrow and the turning space at the top is minimal for a 4.85m car.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE.","cats":["village","history","nature","foodwine"],"gem":true,"season":"Autumn for chestnut season; spring. The Cruces de Mayo festival in early May is the local event.","one":"Stand inside the 10th-century mezquita's arcade — the only rural mosque in Spain to survive essentially unaltered.","book":""},{"n":"Iznájar","r":"Córdoba province, Subbética","c":"Spain","lat":37.2578,"lon":-4.3078,"h":3.0,"km":278,"b":8,"why":"A white village on a peninsula jutting into the largest reservoir in Andalusia, crowned by an 8th-century Moorish castle. The Patio de las Comedias and the Rincón del Beso — a tiny flower-hung courtyard overlooking the water — are the postcard shots, and the lake below is a genuine inland beach (Playa de Valdearenas).","e":7,"drive":"A-92 then the A-333 through the Subbética — good roads with curves, then a short steep climb onto the peninsula.","toll":"None. A-92 then A-333.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free parking at the village entrance below the castle and free at the Playa de Valdearenas lakeside recreation area.","pd":7,"pb":"Park at the entrance. The village lanes up to the castle are narrow and steep with sharp corners — not for a 4.85m car.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE.","cats":["village","nature"],"gem":true,"season":"May–September for the lake beach; spring for the village and flowers.","one":"The Rincón del Beso courtyard viewpoint over the reservoir, then swim at the Playa de Valdearenas.","book":""},{"n":"Jabugo","r":"Huelva province, Sierra de Aracena","c":"Spain","lat":37.9186,"lon":-6.7297,"h":3.0,"km":268,"b":6,"why":"Not a scenic village so much as a working one — its reason to exist is the acorn-fed Iberian black pigs in the surrounding dehesa and the cellars where jamón ibérico de bellota hangs for four years. The drying lofts and the montanera oak pasture around El Repilado are the real sight.","e":7,"drive":"N-433 through the sierra — good sweeping two-lane road. Easy final approach.","toll":"None.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free parking throughout the village and at the visitor entrances of the big bodegas (Cinco Jotas, Eíriz). Free roadside on the N-433 through-road.","pd":9,"pb":"Working village with lorry access — plenty of room.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE.","cats":["foodwine","village"],"gem":true,"season":"October–February — montanera, when the pigs are out eating acorns and you can actually see them in the dehesa.","one":"Tour the Cinco Jotas cellars in Jabugo, where the hams hang in natural drying lofts, and taste bellota 100% cut by a cortador.","book":"Cinco Jotas and the smaller producers (Eíriz, Dehesa Maladúa) run guided cellar visits by appointment only — book ahead, they don't take walk-ins reliably."},{"n":"Alhama de Granada","r":"Granada province","c":"Spain","lat":37.0072,"lon":-3.9903,"h":3.1,"km":290,"b":8,"why":"The town sits on the edge of a 50m-deep gorge — the Tajo de Alhama — cut by the río Alhama, with houses built right to the lip and a walkway (the Paseo del Tajo) along it. Its loss to the Christians in 1482 produced the famous lament '¡Ay de mi Alhama!'. Natural hot springs at 47°C run in the valley below.","e":7,"drive":"A-92 then the A-402 south past the Embalse de Los Bermejales — good two-lane road with mild curves. Easy.","toll":"None. A-92 then A-402.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free parking around the Plaza de los Presos and the Paseo Montes Jovellar; free open ground near the Tajo walkway. Free at the balneario down in the valley.","pd":8,"pb":"Small town with an open modern edge — comfortable. Old core is narrow but compact and walkable.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE.","cats":["village","nature","history"],"gem":true,"season":"Spring and autumn; the hot springs are best in cool weather.","one":"Soak in the free natural thermal pool ('los Baños') where the 47°C spring water runs into the river below town, then walk the Tajo gorge path.","book":""},{"n":"Olhão","r":"Algarve","c":"Portugal","lat":37.0286,"lon":-7.84,"h":3.1,"km":290,"b":7,"why":"A cubist white town of flat-roofed açoteias and external staircases that looks transplanted from North Africa — the result of 18th-century trade with Morocco. Two red-brick iron-frame market halls sit directly on the water with the Ria Formosa islands beyond.","e":9,"drive":"Same easy motorway run as Faro, exit A22 at Olhão. Town centre streets are tight grid one-ways; stay on the waterfront avenue.","toll":"A22 electronic toll, EASYToll at Castro Marim. Ayamonte-Olhão roughly €3.50.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Free unpaved lot behind the Jardim Pescador Olhanense at the east end of Avenida 5 de Outubro, 300m from the markets. Metered bays along the avenue are about €0.50/hr and free after 19:00 and all Sunday.","pd":7,"pb":"The waterfront lot is open and flat. The old-town grid behind the markets is genuinely too narrow for this car — do not turn in.","lez":0,"lezn":"None.","cats":["village","coast","foodwine","quirky"],"gem":true,"season":"Year-round; the Saturday market is best. Avoid August crowds","one":"Saturday morning at the twin Mercados de Olhão — buy percebes and clams at the fish hall, then take the 15-minute ferry to Ilha da Culatra/Farol.","book":"None. Culatra and Armona ferries are ticket-at-the-pier."},{"n":"Ria Formosa","r":"Algarve","c":"Portugal","lat":37.0139,"lon":-7.8383,"h":3.1,"km":290,"b":8,"why":"60km of barrier islands, tidal channels and salt pans holding Europe's densest winter wader population plus the purple swamphen that is the park's emblem. At low tide the whole lagoon becomes a mirror of exposed mudflat with clam-diggers scattered across it.","e":9,"drive":"Drive to Olhão or Quatro Águas (Tavira) and stop — the park itself is boat-access. The Quinta de Marim environmental centre 2km east of Olhão has its own road access and car park.","toll":"A22 electronic toll, EASYToll at Castro Marim. Access via Olhão, Faro or Tavira.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free car park at Quinta de Marim / Centro de Educação Ambiental (2km east of Olhão) and the free lot at Quatro Águas ferry pier near Tavira.","pd":9,"pb":"Both are dirt/gravel lots with no marked bays. Quatro Águas fills by 11:00 in July-August.","lez":0,"lezn":"None.","cats":["nature","coast"],"gem":true,"season":"October-March for birds (flamingos, spoonbills); May-June for wildflowers and empty beaches","one":"The tidal mill and 2km interpretive trail at Quinta de Marim, then a solar-boat trip through the channels from Olhão.","book":"Birdwatching boat operators (Formosamar, Passeios Ria Formosa) should be booked a day or two ahead in season."},{"n":"Zuheros","r":"Córdoba province, Sierras Subbéticas","c":"Spain","lat":37.5442,"lon":-4.3164,"h":3.1,"km":288,"b":9,"why":"A castle built onto a bare limestone crag with white houses spilling below it and the flat olive plain beyond — one of the great pueblo blanco silhouettes and almost unknown outside Spain. The Cueva de los Murciélagos above the village has Neolithic wall paintings and 400m of chambers.","e":6,"drive":"Motorway to the A-318/N-432 then a short but steep, twisting climb into the Subbética. The 4km access road up to the Cueva de los Murciélagos is very narrow with hairpins and drops.","toll":"None. A-4 then A-45/N-432.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free parking at the village entrance on the CO-6203 and on Calle Llana; free lot at the Cueva de los Murciélagos.","pd":7,"pb":"Village is tiny (700 people) with steep narrow lanes — park at the entrance. The cave road is the real test: narrow with tight hairpins, doable but slow in a 4.85m car.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE.","cats":["village","nature","history","foodwine"],"gem":true,"season":"Spring and autumn. The Vía Verde del Aceite passes below.","one":"The Cueva de los Murciélagos guided tour — Neolithic paintings and the largest bat colony in Andalusia — combined with the castle terrace view.","book":"Cueva de los Murciélagos is guided-tour only with limited slots and must be booked in advance (cuevadelosmurcielagos.es). Closed some weekdays out of season."},{"n":"Faro","r":"Algarve","c":"Portugal","lat":37.0161,"lon":-7.935,"h":3.2,"km":300,"b":6,"why":"The walled Cidade Velha is genuinely lovely — the Arco da Vila gateway with its resident stork nests, an orange-tree cathedral square, and the Capela dos Ossos bone chapel — but it is small and the rest of Faro is workaday apartment blocks near an airport.","e":9,"drive":"Straight motorway run, A-4/A-49/A22. Faro's approach roads are modern dual carriageway. The only annoyance is the one-way system around the marina.","toll":"A22 electronic toll, EASYToll at Castro Marim (see Tavira). Ayamonte-Faro about €4. N125 is the free alternative and adds ~40 min.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Large free surface lot at Largo de São Francisco, a two-minute walk from the old-town wall. Paid alternative: Parque de Estacionamento do Mercado Municipal, roughly €0.70/hr.","pd":8,"pb":"São Francisco is a big open square-lot with unmarked bays — ideal for a long sedan. Avoid the Cidade Velha interior entirely; it is cobbled, mostly pedestrian, and the gates are barely 3m wide.","lez":0,"lezn":"None. No restriction on the 2005 diesel.","cats":["city","coast","history"],"gem":false,"season":"March-June, September-November","one":"The Capela dos Ossos behind Igreja do Carmo — a chapel lined with the bones of 1,245 monks, €2 entry.","book":"None. Ria Formosa boat trips from the Doca can be booked same-day at the pier."},{"n":"Priego de Córdoba","r":"Córdoba province, Sierras Subbéticas","c":"Spain","lat":37.4386,"lon":-4.1953,"h":3.2,"km":292,"b":9,"why":"The capital of Andalusian Baroque — the Sagrario chapel of the Iglesia de la Asunción is a white stucco explosion of a dome that stops people in the doorway. The Barrio de la Villa is a Moorish quarter of flower-hung white alleys ending at the Balcón del Adarve, a long balustraded balcony over a cliff and the Subbética beyond. The Fuente del Rey has 139 water spouts.","e":7,"drive":"Motorway then good regional roads (N-432/A-339) through the Subbética. Some climbing but nothing narrow.","toll":"None.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free parking around the Paseo de Colombia and on the streets near the Fuente del Rey and the recinto ferial. Free bays are widely available.","pd":8,"pb":"A town of 22,000 with modern avenues around a compact old core — easy for a large car. Only the Barrio de la Villa lanes are impassable and they're pedestrian anyway.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE.","cats":["village","history","foodwine"],"gem":true,"season":"Spring and autumn. Holy Week here is a major regional event.","one":"The Sagrario of the Iglesia de la Asunción — walk in and look up at the dome; it is the best Baroque interior in Andalusia and barely visited.","book":""},{"n":"Frigiliana","r":"Málaga province, Axarquía","c":"Spain","lat":36.7906,"lon":-3.8944,"h":3.3,"km":320,"b":9,"why":"The Barribarto — the Mudéjar quarter — is a maze of cobbled ramps with hand-painted ceramic panels telling the story of the 1569 Morisco rebellion set into the walls, and geranium pots covering every whitewashed surface. From the top you look down the valley to the Mediterranean.","e":6,"drive":"Easy motorway until Nerja, then a 6km climb on the MA-5105 — steep, tightly curved, with a couple of blind hairpins, but properly surfaced and two-lane. Fine for a big sedan at sensible speed.","toll":"None if you go inland A-92/A-45/A-7 (Málaga bypass is free).","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Free: the Cofradía car park at the village edge (~30–40 spaces, 3–5 min walk) or Calle Llanos de la Molineta (7–10 min, almost always has space). Paid: Plaza de las Tres Culturas, the largest, covered, ~€1–1.50/h or ~€11/day, 2.2m height limit.","pd":5,"pb":"The Cofradía lot fills by mid-morning. Llanos de la Molineta is the reliable big-car answer. The Barribarto lanes are stepped and pedestrian-only — no car goes in there, which is fine. Watch the green residents-only bays.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE (population ~3,000).","cats":["village","coast","foodwine"],"gem":false,"season":"Year-round; spectacular in spring. Very busy Thursdays and summer weekends.","one":"Buy the local sugar-cane honey (miel de caña) from the Nuestra Señora del Carmen factory — the last working cane-syrup mill in Europe — then walk the ceramic-panel route through the Barribarto.","book":""},{"n":"Mérida","r":"Extremadura","c":"Spain","lat":38.9161,"lon":-6.3436,"h":3.3,"km":305,"b":9,"why":"The best-preserved Roman provincial capital in Spain: a 6,000-seat theatre with its two-storey marble scaenae frons still standing, an amphitheatre, a 792m Roman bridge over the Guadiana still in use as a footway, a circus, and the Los Milagros aqueduct with storks nesting on the piers.","e":9,"drive":"One of the easiest long drives on this list — 110km to Sevilla then 195km of fast, empty, free dual carriageway north through dehesa. Mérida itself is a modest-sized town with wide streets and clear signage.","toll":"None. A-4 to Sevilla then the A-66 Autovía Ruta de la Plata — free the whole way.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Free surface parking along the Guadiana riverside (Paseo de Roma / near the Puente Lusitania) and around the Recinto Ferial, a 10-min walk to the theatre. Paid: Parking Plaza de España underground, roughly €1.20/hr.","pd":8,"pb":"The riverside lots are wide and open with angled or unmarked bays. Genuinely easy for a big car — one of the best on this list.","lez":0,"lezn":"None enforced. Mérida is on the national list of municipalities required to designate a ZBE but is NOT among the 13 Spanish cities issuing ZBE fines in 2026. The 2005 unlabeled diesel can drive into the centre.","cats":["history","city","foodwine"],"gem":false,"season":"March-June and September-October; the July-August Festival de Teatro Clásico is the highlight but Extremadura hits 42C","one":"Sit in the Teatro Romano's cavea looking at the columned scaenae frons — and if the dates line up, see a Greek tragedy performed on the original stage during the summer Festival Internacional de Teatro Clásico.","book":"The €17 combined ticket (Conjunto Monumental — theatre, amphitheatre, Casa del Mitreo, Los Columbarios, Alcazaba, Morería, Circo) is far better value than the €13 theatre+amphitheatre ticket; buy online at consorciomerida.org. Festival de Teatro Clásico performances sell out — book months ahead."},{"n":"Nerja","r":"Málaga province, Axarquía","c":"Spain","lat":36.745,"lon":-3.8756,"h":3.3,"km":316,"b":8,"why":"The Balcón de Europa is a palm-lined promontory built on an old gun battery, jutting into the sea with cliffs and coves on both sides. The Cueva de Nerja, 3km east at Maro, contains one of the world's largest natural columns — 32m from floor to ceiling in the Sala del Cataclismo.","e":8,"drive":"A-7 dual carriageway right to Nerja. The only fiddly part is the one-way system in the town centre.","toll":"None inland.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free: El Playazo lot (700+ spaces, the biggest and never full), Rotonda Playa Burriana (165), Giner de los Ríos near Plaza Cavana (~130). Nerja has NO blue-zone ORA — street parking is free wherever it isn't signed prohibited. Paid: Balcón de Europa / Plaza de España underground, ~€2.10/h, ~€21.90/day. Cueva de Nerja has its own lot, ~€2–3.","pd":8,"pb":"Outstanding for a large car and a man who hates paying — El Playazo is a huge free open lot with 700+ spaces, 15 min walk along the seafront to the Balcón. This is one of the best free-parking towns on the whole list.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE (population ~21,000).","cats":["coast","nature","quirky"],"gem":false,"season":"Year-round. May–June and September best; August is chaos.","one":"The Sala del Cataclismo in the Cueva de Nerja with its 32m column — and if the timing works, a concert in the cave's natural auditorium.","book":"Cueva de Nerja uses timed entry slots; book online in summer and at Easter. The nearby Acantilados de Maro–Cerro Gordo cliffs now require advance parking reservation for Cala de Maro/Cañuelo access in high season — check before going."},{"n":"Almuñécar","r":"Granada province, Costa Tropical","c":"Spain","lat":36.7342,"lon":-3.6906,"h":3.4,"km":340,"b":7,"why":"Phoenician, Roman and Moorish layers stacked in one small town: the Castillo de San Miguel on the headland, the Roman aqueduct still standing in the Río Seco valley, Roman fish-salting tanks under the Parque El Majuelo botanical garden, and the Peñón del Santo rock splitting the two main beaches.","e":8,"drive":"A-44 then the N-340/A-7 coast road — modern and tunnelled. Easy.","toll":"None.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free surface parking at Parque El Majuelo and along Paseo del Altillo / near the Río Seco; free bays inland from the Playa Puerta del Mar outside July–August.","pd":7,"pb":"Coastal town with reasonable street widths away from the old core. Free lots are open ground. Summer is the only real problem.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE (population ~26,000).","cats":["coast","history"],"gem":false,"season":"Year-round mild; May–June and September–October best. Packed in August with Granadinos.","one":"The Roman fish-salting factory (garum) tanks in the Parque El Majuelo, with tropical botanical gardens planted over them.","book":""},{"n":"Baeza","r":"Jaén province","c":"Spain","lat":37.993,"lon":-3.47,"h":3.4,"km":330,"b":9,"why":"Smaller and quieter than Úbeda but arguably prettier — Plaza del Pópulo (the 'Plaza de los Leones') with its Ibero-Roman lion fountain, the Casa del Pópulo and the Puerta de Jaén arch all in one enclosed golden-stone space. The Antigua Universidad's patio and the Cathedral's Vandelvira interior complete it.","e":8,"drive":"Same long motorway approach as Úbeda, 9km apart — do both in one trip.","toll":"None.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free open parking on the Paseo de las Murallas and around the Plaza de Toros / Avenida Puche Pardo, all within 5–8 minutes of the Plaza del Pópulo. Free bays are the norm here.","pd":8,"pb":"Baeza is small and surrounded by open free parking — one of the easier historic towns for a large car.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE (population ~16,000).","cats":["city","history","foodwine"],"gem":true,"season":"Spring and autumn.","one":"The Plaza del Pópulo with its Roman lion fountain and the Puerta de Jaén — and a walk along the Paseo de las Murallas over the olive sea.","book":""},{"n":"Montefrío","r":"Granada province, Poniente Granadino","c":"Spain","lat":37.3211,"lon":-4.0111,"h":3.4,"km":320,"b":9,"why":"National Geographic put it on a list of the best views in the world, and the claim holds: from the Mirador de las Peñas you see a domed Renaissance church (Iglesia de la Encarnación, a rotunda modelled on the Pantheon) in the valley and a Moorish castle-church on a vertical rock above it, with white houses between.","e":7,"drive":"A-92 then good regional two-lane roads through olive country with steady curves. Nothing difficult; the last few km climb.","toll":"None. A-92 then A-335/A-336.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free parking around the Plaza de España and along the main through-road; free at the Mirador de las Peñas itself.","pd":8,"pb":"Small town but with a reasonable modern edge — parking at the bottom is straightforward. The road up to the Villa/castle is narrow; walk it.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE.","cats":["village","history","nature","foodwine"],"gem":true,"season":"Spring and autumn.","one":"The Mirador de las Peñas view of the church-on-the-rock, then climb to the Iglesia de la Villa inside the Moorish castle walls.","book":""},{"n":"Mértola","r":"Alentejo","c":"Portugal","lat":37.6383,"lon":-7.6633,"h":3.4,"km":300,"b":9,"why":"A hill town where the parish church is a converted 12th-century mosque — the only one in Portugal that kept its mihrab — under a castle keep, with the Guadiana and Oeiras rivers meeting at the foot of the walls. The whole town is an open-air archaeological site layering Roman, Visigothic and Islamic.","e":7,"drive":"Good empty roads through cork-oak dehesa, but the last stretch is single carriageway with slow farm traffic. The climb into the walled town is steep and cobbled — stop at the bottom.","toll":"Toll-free if routed via Sevilla, Rosal de la Frontera and Serpa on free autovía/N-roads, or via Ayamonte and the free IC27/IP2. Skip the A22.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free car park at the foot of the old town by the river (Largo Vasco da Gama / riverside quay), and a free lot by the town gate on the Beja road.","pd":8,"pb":"Riverside lot is fine. Inside the walls the streets are under 3m with 90-degree corners — genuinely impossible in a 4.85m sedan.","lez":0,"lezn":"None.","cats":["village","history","nature","quirky"],"gem":true,"season":"March-May (wildflowers) and October. The biennial Islamic Festival in May is outstanding","one":"The Igreja Matriz — a mosque converted to a church with its mihrab preserved behind the altar and twelve Islamic columns still standing.","book":"None, except accommodation during the Festival Islâmico."},{"n":"Salobreña","r":"Granada province, Costa Tropical","c":"Spain","lat":36.7439,"lon":-3.5872,"h":3.4,"km":332,"b":8,"why":"A white town piled on an isolated rock rising straight out of green sugar-cane fields half a kilometre from the sea, topped by a 10th-century Nasrid castle where the last Granadan emirs kept their rivals imprisoned. From the castle you see the Mediterranean on one side and the snow line of the Sierra Nevada on the other.","e":8,"drive":"A-92 then the A-44 — a dramatic, modern, tunnelled motorway that drops from 1,000m to the sea through the Sierra Nevada foothills. Long descent, easy road.","toll":"None. A-92 then A-44 south to Motril.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free parking at the Paseo de las Flores / Plaza de Goya lots at the foot of the old town, and free bays along the beachfront Paseo Marítimo outside high season.","pd":7,"pb":"The old town on the rock is a spiral of very narrow one-way lanes — do not attempt. The lots at the base are open and fine, and there is a lift/escalator system partway up.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE.","cats":["coast","village","history"],"gem":true,"season":"Year-round — this is the Costa Tropical, the mildest winter climate in mainland Spain. April–June and September best.","one":"The Castillo de Salobreña ramparts, with sugar cane, sea and snow-topped Sierra Nevada in one 360° turn.","book":""},{"n":"Granada","r":"Granada province, Andalusia","c":"Spain","lat":37.1761,"lon":-3.5881,"h":3.5,"km":333,"b":10,"why":"The Nasrid Palaces' Patio de los Leones and the muqarnas dome of the Sala de los Abencerrajes are the high-water mark of Islamic decorative art in Europe; the Generalife's Patio de la Acequia is a long water garden aimed at the Sierra Nevada. From the Mirador de San Nicolás in the Albaicín the entire red fortress floats against snow-capped 3,400m peaks.","e":7,"drive":"A-92 is long, fast and monotonous with a couple of big climbs near Loja. Inside Granada, use the Ronda Sur (A-395) which climbs directly to the Alhambra car park without entering the city — it is explicitly excluded from the ZBE.","toll":"None. A-4 to Seville, A-92 east via Antequera to Granada — all free dual carriageway.","pf":false,"pp":false,"ps":"Parking Alhambra, Camino Viejo del Cementerio — 500 outdoor spaces, ~€3.17/h capped at ~€21.70/day, 24h security, reached directly off the A-395 Ronda Sur. Free alternative: white-line street bays out in the Zaidín or Chana districts plus a bus in, but they are a long way out.","pd":7,"pb":"The Alhambra lot is a big open-air surface car park with normal-width bays — a 4.85m sedan is a non-issue. The Albaicín, by contrast, is a 4x4-and-nerve zone; never take the E320 up there.","lez":2,"lezn":"YES — Granada's ZBE became fully enforceable with fines on 1 October 2025 and operates 24 hours a day, every day, over 23.55 km² covering the centre, Albaicín and Realejo. 18 perimeter cameras. Unlabelled vehicles (all pre-2006 diesels) are barred unless registered in Granada. CRITICAL EXEMPTIONS FOR HIM: (1) the A-395 Ronda Sur, the GR-30 ring and Avenida Santa María de la Alhambra are EXCLUDED from the zone, so the Alhambra car park is reachable legally; (2) unlabelled vehicles may enter if they park for at least one hour in a car park adhered to the municipal control system. Fine €200 / €100 early.","cats":["city","history","nature"],"gem":false,"season":"April–June and September–October. Winter is fine and quiet with snow on the sierra behind. August is hot and packed.","one":"The timed Nasrid Palaces slot — go for the last slot of the day or a night visit, when the Patio de los Leones is nearly empty.","book":"MANDATORY AND THE SINGLE MOST IMPORTANT BOOKING ON THIS LIST. Alhambra tickets are online-only, ~6,000 visitors/day, and routinely sell out weeks ahead — book 2–3 months out for spring/autumn. Your Nasrid Palaces entry is a hard timed slot; arrive late and you are refused. Bring the ID you booked with — names are checked."},{"n":"Úbeda","r":"Jaén province","c":"Spain","lat":38.0136,"lon":-3.3706,"h":3.5,"km":340,"b":9,"why":"Plaza Vázquez de Molina is a complete, unaltered Spanish Renaissance square — the Sacra Capilla del Salvador with its Vandelvira facade, the Palacio del Deán Ortega and the Santa María church face each other across bare stone with no modern intrusion. UNESCO listed it jointly with Baeza for exactly this.","e":8,"drive":"Long motorway run north past Córdoba, then good dual carriageway. Nothing difficult, just distance — an olive-grove landscape for the last 100km that goes on forever.","toll":"None. A-4 north via Córdoba then N-322/A-32.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Free: open lots on the Redonda de Miradores ring road on the south side of the old town, a 5-minute walk to Plaza Vázquez de Molina, with a valley view thrown in. Paid: Aparcamiento Plaza de Santa Clara, roughly €1.20/h.","pd":7,"pb":"The Redonda de Miradores is the answer — a free perimeter road with open bays and the old town right there. The intramural streets are narrow Renaissance lanes; leave the car outside.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE (population ~34,000).","cats":["city","history","foodwine"],"gem":false,"season":"Spring and autumn. Jaén is furnace-hot in summer.","one":"Stand in Plaza Vázquez de Molina at dusk when the floodlights come on and the square empties.","book":"The Sacra Capilla del Salvador and Sinagoga del Agua have limited-capacity guided visits — book the Sinagoga del Agua ahead, it's small."},{"n":"Asilah","r":"Tanger-Tétouan-Al Hoceïma","c":"Morocco","lat":35.465,"lon":-6.0347,"h":3.6,"km":265,"b":8,"why":"A small walled Portuguese-built port on the Atlantic where the entire medina is whitewashed with cobalt-blue trim, and every summer international artists paint large murals directly onto the walls — repainted annually. Ramparts drop straight into the sea.","e":5,"drive":"Ferry then 45 minutes of good motorway south from Tangier. The most relaxed of the four Moroccan options — small, calm, walkable, low-hassle. Reachable by train from Tanger Ville in 40 minutes if you left the car in Tarifa, which is the easiest way to do it.","toll":"Spanish side free. Tanger-Asilah motorway toll about 12 MAD.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Free and informal-attendant street parking along Avenue Hassan II outside the medina walls, typically 10-20 MAD to the gardien. Paid lot near the Bab el-Bahr gate.","pd":7,"pb":"The outside-walls avenues are wide and mostly angled parking. Easiest Moroccan parking of the four.","lez":0,"lezn":"None in Morocco.","cats":["coast","village","quirky","history"],"gem":true,"season":"May-June and September. The Asilah Arts Festival is in July-August and is when the murals are painted","one":"Walk the sea ramparts to the Portuguese-era Kasbah and the clifftop cemetery at sunset, then eat grilled sardines at the fishing port.","book":"Ferry only. Accommodation during the July-August arts festival needs booking."},{"n":"Benagil & Praia da Marinha","r":"Algarve","c":"Portugal","lat":37.09,"lon":-8.4128,"h":3.6,"km":345,"b":10,"why":"Praia da Marinha's twin arches and the honeycombed ochre cliffs are the postcard image of the Algarve; the Benagil sea cave next door has a collapsed dome that drops a shaft of sunlight straight onto an internal beach. The Percurso dos Sete Vales Suspensos clifftop trail links them.","e":7,"drive":"Motorway to the Lagoa/Porches exit, then narrow rural lanes through Caramujeira. The final approach to Benagil village is a single steep lane that jams solid in summer — park at Marinha and walk the cliff path (about 35 min) instead.","toll":"A22 electronic toll to Lagoa/Porches exit, roughly €6 from the border.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Praia da Marinha clifftop car park — free, large, unpaved. Benagil village lot is paid (about €5/day) and tiny.","pd":7,"pb":"Marinha is easy for a big car; Benagil's lot is cramped, sloping and requires reversing on a hill — avoid it in this car. Park at Marinha and walk.","lez":0,"lezn":"None.","cats":["coast","nature"],"gem":false,"season":"May-June, September; the cave light shaft is best mid-morning","one":"A small-boat tour into the Benagil cave from Praia de Benagil or Armação de Pêra (about €25-30 pp, 45 min).","book":"Book the Benagil boat a day ahead in July-August. Note that swimming and independent kayaking into the cave have been restricted — go with a licensed operator, and the trip is cancelled outright in swell."},{"n":"Lanjarón","r":"Granada province, Las Alpujarras","c":"Spain","lat":36.9203,"lon":-3.4794,"h":3.6,"km":350,"b":7,"why":"The gateway to the Alpujarras, a long spa town strung along a ridge above the Lecrín valley with mineral springs and a ruined Moorish castle on a spur below the road. Its balcony views down the Guadalfeo gorge are the first hint of what the Alpujarras look like.","e":7,"drive":"Motorway to the Béznar junction, then the A-348 — a good two-lane road with steady curves along the valley side.","toll":"None. A-92/A-44 then A-348.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free parking along Avenida de la Alpujarra (the main through-road) and at the balneario lots.","pd":8,"pb":"Linear town on a main road — easy for a big car, the easiest of the Alpujarra villages by far.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE.","cats":["village","nature","foodwine"],"gem":false,"season":"Spring through autumn. The Fiesta del Agua on 23–24 June is a town-wide water fight at midnight.","one":"Drink from the public mineral fountains, then walk down to the ruined Castillo de Lanjarón on its spur for the gorge view.","book":""},{"n":"Silves","r":"Algarve","c":"Portugal","lat":37.1892,"lon":-8.4386,"h":3.6,"km":355,"b":8,"why":"A complete red-sandstone Almohad castle — the largest surviving Moorish fortification in the Algarve — sitting above a whitewashed hill town with a 13th-century Gothic cathedral built from the same red stone. Below, a Roman-footed bridge crosses the Arade among orange groves.","e":7,"drive":"Motorway then 8km of good rural road. The climb through the town to the castle is steep and cobbled with hairpins — do not drive up, park at the river.","toll":"A22 electronic toll to the Silves/Alcantarilha exit, roughly €5.50 from the border.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free riverside car park on the Arade below the old bridge (Parque de Estacionamento junto à Ponte Romana), 8 min uphill walk to the castle.","pd":8,"pb":"Big, flat, tarmac, marked bays. There is also a free lot by the municipal market. The upper town is not big-car territory.","lez":0,"lezn":"None.","cats":["village","history"],"gem":true,"season":"October-May; inland Algarve hits 40C in July-August. The August medieval fair is a spectacle","one":"Walk the full circuit of the Castelo de Silves ramparts (€2.80) with the Monchique hills on one side and orange groves on the other.","book":"None."},{"n":"Cáceres","r":"Extremadura","c":"Spain","lat":39.4736,"lon":-6.3722,"h":3.8,"km":375,"b":10,"why":"The Ciudad Monumental is a walled medieval-Renaissance old town with almost no modern intrusion — over 30 fortified noble towers, Almohad walls, and streets so intact that Game of Thrones filmed King's Landing here without set dressing. At night, lit by lantern-style lamps and empty, it is extraordinary.","e":9,"drive":"Fast free motorway virtually to the edge of town. The modern city has wide boulevards; the walled old town is entirely pedestrian and cobbled, so there is no chance of getting the car stuck in it.","toll":"None. A-4 to Sevilla, A-66 north — free autovía throughout.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Free surface parking around the Recinto Ferial and along Ronda de San Francisco / Avenida de la Universidad, 10-15 min walk. Paid: Parking Obispo Galarza underground right beside Plaza Mayor, roughly €1.50/hr, €18-20/day.","pd":7,"pb":"Plaza Mayor sits immediately outside the walls with the Galarza garage under it — convenient but with tight underground bays. The free peripheral lots are far kinder to a long sedan.","lez":0,"lezn":"None enforced. Cáceres has a ZBE designation obligation but is NOT among the 13 cities fining in 2026. The old town is a pedestrian/restricted-traffic zone by street rules, not emissions. He can drive into the modern centre legally.","cats":["city","history","foodwine"],"gem":true,"season":"March-June and September-November. Extremaduran summers are severe","one":"Walk the Ciudad Monumental after dark, when the day-trippers have gone — start at the Arco de la Estrella, climb the Torre de Bujaco, and end in Plaza de San Jorge.","book":"Nothing mandatory. Michelin-starred Atrio, on Plaza de San Mateo inside the walls, needs booking months ahead if that appeals."},{"n":"Lagos","r":"Algarve","c":"Portugal","lat":37.1028,"lon":-8.6733,"h":3.9,"km":390,"b":9,"why":"Gold-ochre sandstone cliffs collapsed into stacks, arches and hidden beaches — Praia do Camilo, Dona Ana, Praia do Pinhão — all within walking distance of a walled old town with the 18th-century gilt-carved Igreja de Santo António.","e":8,"drive":"Motorway all the way to the A22's western end. The last few km into town via the EN125 gets congested in summer. The old town is a one-way cobbled maze — park on the perimeter and walk in.","toll":"A22 full length, electronic toll. EASYToll at Castro Marim; Ayamonte to Lagos is roughly €8 one way. This is the single biggest toll bill of any Portugal trip on this list.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Large free earth lot on Avenida dos Descobrimentos next to the Complexo Desportivo/bus station, 5 min walk to the walls. Paid alternative: Parque da Marina de Lagos, roughly €1/hr, €10/day.","pd":7,"pb":"The Descobrimentos lot is unpaved but big and open, no manoeuvring needed. The Ponta da Piedade clifftop lot up the hill is also free and holds full-size cars, but the road up (Rua Porto de Mós) is narrow with blind bends.","lez":0,"lezn":"None.","cats":["coast","history","city"],"gem":false,"season":"May-June and September; July-August is heaving","one":"Walk the clifftop path from Praia Dona Ana south to the Ponta da Piedade lighthouse at golden hour.","book":"Grotto boat trips from the Marina/Doca — book the morning of, or a day ahead in August."},{"n":"Pampaneira","r":"Granada province, Barranco de Poqueira, Alpujarras","c":"Spain","lat":36.9414,"lon":-3.3617,"h":3.9,"km":378,"b":9,"why":"The lowest of the three Poqueira villages — flat-roofed Berber-style houses with 'tinaos' (covered passageways) and cylindrical clay chimneys, stacked on a wall of the Poqueira ravine with the Mulhacén massif at the head of the valley. Water runs in open channels down the middle of the streets.","e":5,"drive":"The GR-421 up the Poqueira ravine from the A-348 is narrow, steep and continuously hairpinned with sheer drops — properly surfaced and two-lane, but slow and demanding. Coaches use it, so it is passable, but expect to pull in.","toll":"None.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free parking at the village entrance (the lot on the GR-421 just below Plaza de la Libertad). Free roadside on the through-road.","pd":6,"pb":"The entrance lot is the only realistic option and fills on weekends. Inside the village the streets are 2m wide with tinao overheads — absolutely not for a 4.85m car.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE.","cats":["village","nature","foodwine"],"gem":false,"season":"May–June and September–October. Winter can bring snow and ice to the GR-421 — check before driving it. August is busy but cool at 1,058m.","one":"Buy a hand-woven Alpujarran jarapa rug from a village loom and eat plato alpujarreño with local cured ham.","book":""},{"n":"Capileira","r":"Granada province, Barranco de Poqueira, Alpujarras","c":"Spain","lat":36.9603,"lon":-3.3597,"h":4.0,"km":383,"b":9,"why":"The highest of the Poqueira three at 1,436m, and the best viewpoint — from the Mirador de la Cruz you look straight down the ravine over Bubión and Pampaneira with terraced fields and the Sierra Nevada peaks behind. Same flat roofs, tinaos and clay chimneys, with mountain light.","e":4,"drive":"The GR-421 continues up past Bubión, getting narrower, steeper and tighter each village. The final section into Capileira is genuinely constricted. This is the hardest normal-road drive in the cluster short of Genalguacil.","toll":"None.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free parking at the village entrance lot on the GR-421 below the church. Free roadside above the village on the road toward the Hoya del Portillo.","pd":5,"pb":"The entrance lot is small and fills by 11:00 on weekends. If it's full you have a difficult reverse or a long back-track. Arrive early. The upper roadside toward Hoya del Portillo is a reliable fallback with room.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE.","cats":["village","nature","foodwine"],"gem":false,"season":"May–October. The road can ice in winter at 1,436m.","one":"The Mirador de la Cruz view down the Poqueira ravine to Bubión and Pampaneira with the Sierra Nevada behind.","book":"The Hoya del Portillo road above Capileira into the high Sierra Nevada national park is closed to private vehicles; a park shuttle bus runs in summer and needs booking through the Servicio de Interpretación de Altas Cumbres."},{"n":"Cazorla","r":"Jaén province","c":"Spain","lat":37.9106,"lon":-3.0031,"h":4.0,"km":378,"b":9,"why":"The largest protected area in Spain. Cazorla town is wedged under the Peña de los Halcones with the Castillo de la Yedra above it and the roofless shell of Santa María de la Asunción in the ravine. Inside the park, the source of the Guadalquivir, the Cerrada del Utrero gorge and the Embalse del Tranco run through limestone and black pine.","e":6,"drive":"Motorway most of the way, then good roads to Cazorla. Inside the park the A-319 and the road up to the Nacimiento del Guadalquivir are narrow, twisting mountain roads with drops — scenic and fine but demanding, and the forest tracks are unsuitable for a low sedan.","toll":"None.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Free open parking on Paseo del Santo Cristo and around Plaza de la Constitución in Cazorla town; free at most park trailheads and miradores. Small paid lot near Plaza de Santa María.","pd":7,"pb":"Cazorla's lower town has space; the upper lanes toward the castle are very tight. Inside the park, watch ground clearance on unsurfaced pull-offs.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE.","cats":["nature","village","history"],"gem":true,"season":"April–June (green, waterfalls, deer) and September–October. It is the wettest part of Andalusia — bring rain gear.","one":"Drive to the Mirador Paso del Aire / Cerrada del Utrero and walk the short loop to the Linarejos waterfall.","book":"4x4 wildlife-watching trips into the restricted reserve areas (deer, mouflon, ibex, lammergeier) are run by licensed operators from Cazorla — book ahead in the autumn rut."},{"n":"Guadix","r":"Granada province, Hoya de Guadix","c":"Spain","lat":37.2939,"lon":-3.1281,"h":4.0,"km":390,"b":8,"why":"The Barrio de las Cuevas is a hillside of ochre badlands where thousands of people still live inside the rock — only whitewashed chimneys and doorframes break the surface. Above it the Mirador Cerro de la Bala frames the whitewashed chimney field against the snow line of the Sierra Nevada, and in town the red-sandstone Catedral de la Encarnación has a full baroque facade by Vicente Acero.","e":8,"drive":"Long haul but easy — A-92/A-92N dual carriageway almost door to door. The tracks up into the cave quarter itself are unpaved dirt in places.","toll":"None. A-92 then A-92N.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free street parking throughout the cave quarter and free open ground at the Mirador Cerro de la Bala; free bays around Plaza de la Constitución in the lower town.","pd":8,"pb":"The lower town is spacious. Up in the cave barrio the lanes are unpaved and rutted — a low-slung E-Class will scrape on the worst of them. Park at the mirador and walk the barrio.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE (population ~18,000).","cats":["quirky","village","history"],"gem":true,"season":"Spring and autumn. It sits at 950m so winter is cold but crystal clear with snow behind; summer is scorching.","one":"Go into the Cueva Museo Costumbres Populares to see a lived-in cave house interior, then drive the 5 min to the Mirador Cerro de la Bala for the chimney-field view.","book":""},{"n":"Ponta da Piedade","r":"Algarve","c":"Portugal","lat":37.0817,"lon":-8.6683,"h":4.0,"km":392,"b":10,"why":"A headland of 20m yellow-limestone sea stacks, natural arches and a grotto system you can motor a small boat through; a 182-step staircase drops from the clifftop to a tiny landing where the water is a flat aquamarine. It is the single most photogenic point on the Algarve coast.","e":7,"drive":"Add 10 min from Lagos on the narrow, winding Rua Porto de Mós / Estrada da Ponta da Piedade. Two-way but tight in places with tour-bus traffic and cyclists. Go early.","toll":"Same as Lagos — A22 electronic toll, ~€8 from the border.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free gravel car park at the Ponta da Piedade lighthouse, at the road's end. No charge, no meter.","pd":7,"pb":"Roomy when empty, chaotic and double-parked from about 11:00 to 17:00 in summer — arrive before 09:30 or after 18:00 and you can park a big sedan without a single reverse manoeuvre.","lez":0,"lezn":"None.","cats":["coast","nature"],"gem":false,"season":"April-June, September-October; go at sunrise any time of year","one":"Take one of the small wooden boats from the bottom of the staircase (about €15-20 pp, 30-40 min) right through the arches — big catamarans cannot enter.","book":"None — the small boats are cash-at-the-jetty, and they only run in calm seas."},{"n":"Sierra Nevada / Pico Veleta","r":"Granada province","c":"Spain","lat":37.0972,"lon":-3.3897,"h":4.0,"km":365,"b":9,"why":"The highest paved road in Europe. From Hoya de la Mora at 2,500m you look across the Corral del Veleta cirque to the 3,396m Veleta summit, and on clear days the entire Alpujarra, the Mediterranean and the Rif mountains of Morocco are visible below you.","e":5,"drive":"The A-395 from Granada up to Pradollano ski station is a superb wide modern mountain highway — genuinely easy. Above Pradollano the GR-3200 continues to a barrier at roughly 2,500–2,600m at Hoya de la Mora / Posiciones. Beyond that barrier private cars are BANNED — the surface degrades to potholes and rockslide debris and a park microbus takes hikers to ~3,100m. So the drive is easy but finite. In winter snow chains are frequently mandatory above Pradollano.","toll":"None. A-92 to Granada then A-395 and A-4025/GR-3200.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free open parking at Hoya de la Mora (the Albergue Universitario area) at the end of the public road, ~2,500m. Pradollano ski station below has large paid garages in ski season.","pd":8,"pb":"Hoya de la Mora is a big open flat lot. The only big-car issue is altitude and cold-start on a 21-year-old diesel at 2,500m — and snow. Carry chains November–April.","lez":0,"lezn":"The A-395 Ronda Sur — the road you use to leave Granada for the sierra — is explicitly EXCLUDED from Granada's ZBE, so an unlabelled diesel can reach the mountain legally without entering the restricted zone.","cats":["nature","quirky"],"gem":false,"season":"Late June to early October for the high road — snow closes the upper section roughly November to May, and even in June there can be drifts. Best light in September–October.","one":"Drive to the barrier at Hoya de la Mora and walk the last stretch toward the Posiciones del Veleta for the view down the Corral del Veleta.","book":"No booking, but CHECK ROAD STATUS before driving 4 hours — the upper GR-3200 is closed by snow much of the year. Sierra Nevada's own site and the Junta publish opening dates."},{"n":"Monchique / Fóia","r":"Algarve","c":"Portugal","lat":37.3153,"lon":-8.5992,"h":4.1,"km":380,"b":7,"why":"Fóia at 902m is the highest point in the Algarve, with a view that on a clear day runs from Cabo de São Vicente to the Serra da Arrábida near Lisbon. The slopes are eucalyptus, cork oak and strawberry-tree scrub, though the 2018 fires left visible scars.","e":6,"drive":"Fine until Portimão, then the N266 climbs steadily with sweeping bends, and the final 8km road to the Fóia summit is a narrow, exposed, switchbacked climb. Nothing dangerous, but the E320's length means committing to the full lane on hairpins. Fog can shut the summit in without warning.","toll":"A22 to Portimão exit (~€6.50 from the border), then free N266.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free tarmac car park at the Fóia summit beside the radar domes. In Monchique village, free lot at the Largo dos Chorões.","pd":8,"pb":"Summit lot is large and easy. Monchique village itself is steep and tight — use the Chorões lot and walk.","lez":0,"lezn":"None.","cats":["nature","village","foodwine"],"gem":true,"season":"February-March for mimosa bloom; October-November for clarity. Avoid hazy midsummer","one":"Drive to Fóia at dawn for the two-coast view, then eat piri-piri chicken and drink medronho (strawberry-tree spirit) in Monchique village.","book":"None."},{"n":"Trevélez","r":"Granada province, Alta Alpujarra","c":"Spain","lat":37.0,"lon":-3.2683,"h":4.2,"km":400,"b":8,"why":"At 1,476m it claims to be the highest village in Spain, spread in three barrios up a slope under Mulhacén, the highest peak on the peninsula. Its dry mountain air cures the famous jamón de Trevélez in open-sided drying sheds you can see from the road.","e":4,"drive":"The A-4132 east through the Alpujarra from Pitres — narrow, twisting, and hanging on the valley side with long drops. Paved and two-lane but slow going and unforgiving; budget 40 minutes for the last 25km.","toll":"None.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free parking on the through-road in the Barrio Bajo and at the lot near the bridge over the Río Trevélez.","pd":7,"pb":"Barrio Bajo along the road has space. The Barrio Alto is steep, narrow and cobbled — walk it.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZBE.","cats":["village","foodwine","nature"],"gem":true,"season":"June–October. It is genuinely cold and can be snowbound in winter.","one":"Visit a secadero (ham drying shed) and eat jamón de Trevélez cured at 1,476m, then start the Mulhacén trail from the village if he's walking.","book":""},{"n":"Chefchaouen","r":"Tanger-Tétouan-Al Hoceïma","c":"Morocco","lat":35.1688,"lon":-5.2636,"h":4.3,"km":305,"b":10,"why":"An entire medina lime-washed in a dozen shades of blue, stacked against the Rif mountains at 600m, with the Ras el-Maa waterfall running straight out of the mountainside at the top of the town and the Spanish Mosque on the ridge opposite for sunset. It genuinely looks like nowhere else on earth.","e":3,"drive":"FERRY plus a real mountain drive. Ferry Tarifa-Tanger Ville 1h or Algeciras-Tanger Med 1h30, then 2 to 2.5 hours on Moroccan roads — the last hour on the N2 is a winding mountain climb shared with trucks, donkeys and unlit vehicles. Do not drive it after dark. HONEST ADVICE: leave the car in Tarifa and take a CTM/grand-taxi or a hired driver from Tanger Ville (about €80-120 for a day car with driver). If you do take the car: Morocco requires the D16ter temporary-admission customs form (free, filled in at the port, valid up to 180 days per calendar year, and the vehicle is electronically tied to your passport — you cannot leave Morocco without it). Your Spanish green card must explicitly list Morocco; most do not, in which case buy third-party insurance at the port kiosk for roughly €50-100. If the car is not registered in his own name he needs a notarised authorisation letter from the owner. Bring the original permiso de circulación, ficha técnica, licence and passport.","toll":"Spanish side free. Moroccan side: Tanger Med to Tétouan motorway is tolled, then the N2 to Chefchaouen is free. Total Moroccan tolls under €4.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Parking on Avenue Allal Ben Abdellah — 10 MAD day / 20 MAD night / 50 MAD for 24h, walking distance to the medina. Parking Place Outa el Hammam is guarded and helpful but fills by midday. Free street parking exists next to the old town but is scarce in season and less secure.","pd":4,"pb":"Chefchaouen's approach streets are steep and narrow and the lots are informal, packed and attendant-managed. A 4.85m sedan is a poor fit. Strongly consider arriving without the car.","lez":0,"lezn":"None in Morocco.","cats":["village","nature","quirky","history"],"gem":false,"season":"April-June and September-October. Winter is cold and wet at altitude; summer is hot but bearable at 600m","one":"Walk up to the Spanish Mosque on the eastern ridge for sunset over the entire blue medina — 30 minutes uphill, free.","book":"Ferry yes. Riads in the medina should be booked ahead in season — and check whether yours can arrange a parking gardien."},{"n":"Sagres & Cabo de S. Vicente","r":"Algarve","c":"Portugal","lat":37.0086,"lon":-8.9464,"h":4.3,"km":425,"b":9,"why":"The southwest corner of continental Europe: 75m vertical cliffs, a red lighthouse with the strongest beam in Europe, and Henry the Navigator's fortress enclosing a 43m stone wind-rose. Atlantic swell hits the base of the cliffs hard enough that you feel it through the ground.","e":8,"drive":"Easy motorway then a good, straight, empty two-lane road across scrubby windswept plateau. The final 6km to the cape is exposed and genuinely windy — a high-sided vehicle would be shoved around; your sedan will be fine.","toll":"A22 to its end at Lagos (~€8 from the border), then free N125/N268 for the last 35km.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free open lot directly outside the Cabo de São Vicente lighthouse, and a second free lot at the Fortaleza de Sagres entrance.","pd":9,"pb":"Both are big flat unmarked lots designed for coaches — the easiest parking on this entire list.","lez":0,"lezn":"None.","cats":["coast","nature","history"],"gem":false,"season":"April-June and September-October. Winter is dramatic but brutally windy","one":"Sunset at Cabo de São Vicente, then a bifana from the lone sausage van in the lighthouse car park — a genuine local institution.","book":"Fortaleza de Sagres entry (€5) is walk-up, no booking."},{"n":"Trujillo","r":"Extremadura","c":"Spain","lat":39.4589,"lon":-5.8819,"h":4.3,"km":425,"b":9,"why":"An enormous granite Plaza Mayor dominated by a bronze equestrian Pizarro, ringed by the conquistador palaces built with Peruvian silver — the Palacio de la Conquista carries carved busts of Pizarro and the Inca princess Inés Yupanqui on its corner. Above it, a Moorish castle on bare granite outcrop with storks on every tower.","e":9,"drive":"All free motorway. Trujillo's lower town is modern and easy; the climb from Plaza Mayor to the castle is steep cobbled lanes on foot.","toll":"None. A-4/A-66/A-5 — all free autovía.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free surface car parks on the ring below the old town — the large lot near the bullring (Plaza de Toros) and along Avenida de Miajadas, both a 5-10 min walk up to Plaza Mayor. Some free bays on the Plaza Mayor itself but they are always taken.","pd":8,"pb":"The lower ring lots are open and unmarked — easy. Above Plaza Mayor the lanes are cobbled and steep with sharp corners; not for this car.","lez":0,"lezn":"None. Trujillo is a small town well under any ZBE threshold. No restriction.","cats":["village","history","foodwine"],"gem":true,"season":"March-June and September-October. The May cheese fair (Feria del Queso) fills the Plaza Mayor","one":"Climb to the castle keep and cistern at sunset — the view over the dehesa is unbroken to the horizon and the whole plain turns gold.","book":"None. Book ahead for the Feria del Queso weekend in early May."},{"n":"Monfragüe","r":"Extremadura","c":"Spain","lat":39.8375,"lon":-6.0431,"h":4.5,"km":435,"b":9,"why":"The Salto del Gitano is a 200m quartzite cliff above the Tagus where you can stand at a roadside viewpoint and watch several hundred griffon vultures, Spanish imperial eagles, black vultures and black storks on the nest — one of the densest raptor concentrations in Europe, visible without a hike. Certified Starlight Reserve for night skies.","e":8,"drive":"Free motorway most of the way, then well-surfaced quiet park roads. The EX-208 through the park is a scenic two-lane with pull-ins. Nothing technical. Watch for wildlife at dawn and dusk.","toll":"None. Free autovía to Trujillo/Plasencia then free EX-208.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free car park at the Salto del Gitano viewpoint on the EX-208, and free parking at Villarreal de San Carlos, the park's visitor-centre village.","pd":9,"pb":"Both are proper laybys/lots sized for coaches. Among the easiest parking on this entire list.","lez":0,"lezn":"None. National park, no restrictions.","cats":["nature","history"],"gem":true,"season":"February-May is peak — nesting raptors and green dehesa. Autumn is good; midsummer is hot and birds are less active","one":"Stand at the Salto del Gitano at first light with binoculars and pick out black storks nesting on the cliff face, then drive up to the Castillo de Monfragüe for the panorama.","book":"None. Bring binoculars — the whole point is at 200m distance."},{"n":"Monsaraz","r":"Alentejo","c":"Portugal","lat":38.4433,"lon":-7.38,"h":4.8,"km":450,"b":10,"why":"A walled medieval village of maybe 800m end to end, entirely whitewashed and slate-paved, on a quartzite ridge above the Alqueva reservoir — the largest artificial lake in Western Europe. It is inside the Alqueva Dark Sky Reserve, the world's first certified starlight tourism destination.","e":7,"drive":"Motorway to Badajoz, then good quiet Alentejo roads. The last 3km is a steep switchback climb up the ridge. Cars are banned inside the walls, which is a gift — you cannot end up trapped in a medieval lane.","toll":"Via Badajoz then free N-roads through Mourão/Reguengos — routing this way costs nothing. Using the A6 adds a ~€9 toll.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free car parks immediately outside both village gates (Porta da Vila lot). No charge at all.","pd":9,"pb":"Open unmarked lots on the ridge, angled bays, easy for a long car. Vehicles are prohibited inside the walls — you physically cannot make the mistake.","lez":0,"lezn":"None.","cats":["village","history","nature","quirky"],"gem":true,"season":"April-June and September-October; go on a new moon for the stargazing","one":"A guided stargazing session at the Dark Sky Alqueva observatory in nearby Cromeleque/Reguengos, or just walk the castle walls at dusk over the lake.","book":"Dark Sky Alqueva observatory sessions must be booked in advance and are moon-phase and weather dependent."},{"n":"Évora","r":"Alentejo","c":"Portugal","lat":38.5714,"lon":-7.9135,"h":4.8,"km":455,"b":9,"why":"A 1st-century Roman temple with fourteen Corinthian columns standing intact in the middle of a living town, a 16th-century aqueduct that houses have been built into the arches of, and the Capela dos Ossos lined floor-to-ceiling with 5,000 skeletons under the inscription 'we bones await yours'.","e":8,"drive":"Almost entirely motorway — A-4 to Sevilla, A-66 north (free autovía), A-5 to Badajoz, A6 into Portugal. The border at Caia is an unmanned drive-through. Long but genuinely easy; the A-66 through Extremadura is fast and empty.","toll":"Route via Sevilla, A-66 to Zafra/Mérida, A-5 to Badajoz, then Portugal's A6 — the A6 Caia-Évora section is a conventional barrier toll, roughly €9-11. Free alternative: N-4/N254 from Badajoz via Elvas and Estremoz, adds about 40 min.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Free surface parking outside the walls at Portas da Lagoa near the aqueduct — the flattest, widest approach, no steep cobbled climb. Paid option: Parque da Alcárcova underground, about €1/hr.","pd":7,"pb":"Portas da Lagoa is specifically the recommended entry for larger cars because every other approach involves steep narrow cobbled streets. Inside the walls, on-street bays are short and require parallel parking — don't.","lez":0,"lezn":"None. Portugal's only car-restricting ZER is Lisbon's, and Évora has no emissions rule — just a paid/restricted historic-centre traffic zone.","cats":["city","history","foodwine"],"gem":false,"season":"March-June and September-October. July-August routinely exceeds 38C","one":"The Capela dos Ossos at Igreja de São Francisco (€6), then dinner of porco preto and an Alentejo red on Praça do Giraldo.","book":"Nothing mandatory, but the Capela dos Ossos queue is long midday — go at opening. Book Alentejo wine-estate visits (Cartuxa, Herdade do Esporão) ahead."},{"n":"Almería","r":"Almería, Andalucía","c":"Spain","lat":36.8381,"lon":-2.4597,"h":5.3,"km":520,"b":7,"why":"The Alcazaba is the largest Muslim fortress built in Spain after the Alhambra and Almería's, sprawling over three walled enclosures on a hill above the port, and it is free to EU citizens. Below it, the cathedral is a fortified church with gun bastions — built to repel Barbary corsairs.","e":8,"drive":"Straightforward motorway. The city has a modern grid near the port and a tighter old quarter below the Alcazaba. Easy compared with Andalusian hill towns.","toll":"None — A-4/A-382/A-92 are all free autovías.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Free surface parking along the Paseo Marítimo and near the Cable Inglés loading pier; paid underground Parking Plaza Puerta de Purchena or Parking Rambla Obispo Orberá, roughly €1.50/hr, €18/day.","pd":7,"pb":"Seafront parking is open and simple. Do not attempt the streets immediately below the Alcazaba.","lez":1,"lezn":"Almería's ZBE is designated but NOT among the 13 Spanish cities enforcing with fines in 2026. No practical restriction on the unlabeled 2005 diesel — but verify locally before a long stay, as Spanish enforcement dates keep moving.","cats":["city","history","coast"],"gem":true,"season":"October-May. July-August is the hottest city in Spain","one":"The Alcazaba of Almería — free for EU citizens, three enclosures, and views over the whole bay.","book":"None. The Tabernas desert film sets (Oasys/Fort Bravo) 30 min north are worth adding and are walk-up."},{"n":"Marvão","r":"Alentejo / Alto Alentejo","c":"Portugal","lat":39.3939,"lon":-7.3767,"h":5.3,"km":490,"b":9,"why":"An eagle's-nest granite village at 862m on a quartzite crag, entirely enclosed by intact 13th-century walls, with a castle keep looking straight down into Spain across the Serra de São Mamede. Saramago wrote that from Marvão you understand the size of the land.","e":5,"drive":"The long haul is motorway and fine, but the final 7km from Portalegre is a genuinely steep, narrow, hairpinned mountain road with drops and no barrier in places. In a 4.85m car you will use the full lane on the tightest bends. Fog is common. Not a night drive.","toll":"Via Sevilla, A-66 to Mérida, A-5 to Badajoz then free N-roads (N246) via Portalegre — no tolls on this routing. Spanish-side alternative through Valencia de Alcántara is also toll-free.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free car park just outside the Porta de Ródão village gate, plus overflow free spaces on the approach road.","pd":6,"pb":"The gate lot is small and sloping; if it is full you park on the approach road, which is narrow. Cars cannot enter the walled village. Arrive before 11:00 on weekends.","lez":0,"lezn":"None.","cats":["village","history","nature"],"gem":true,"season":"April-June and September-October. November brings the chestnut festival; winter is cold and fog-bound","one":"Walk the full walled circuit at sunrise when the valley below is a sea of cloud and you can see into two countries.","book":"None. Book a room in the village itself — there are very few, and staying overnight after the day-trippers leave is the point."},{"n":"Melilla","r":"Ciudad Autónoma de Melilla","c":"Spain (North Africa)","lat":35.2923,"lon":-2.9381,"h":5.3,"km":530,"b":7,"why":"Melilla la Vieja is a complete star-fort peninsula of Renaissance bastions and tunnels, and the modern town has the second-largest concentration of Catalan Modernista architecture anywhere after Barcelona — around 900 buildings by Enric Nieto, a Gaudí collaborator.","e":3,"drive":"HONEST ASSESSMENT: this does not work as a weekender. It is 5+ hours to Almería and then a 6-8 hour ferry each way, so you lose two full days to transit. Realistically a 4-5 day trip, or fly from Málaga/Almería (1 hour) and leave the car at home. Included here for completeness rather than recommendation.","toll":"No road tolls to Almería. Ferry Almería-Melilla is 6-8 hours (Baleària/Trasmediterránea), often overnight; Málaga-Melilla is 7-8 hours. Car plus cabin runs €200-350 return.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Free surface parking around the Puerto Deportivo and Paseo Marítimo; paid Parking Plaza de España underground, about €1/hr.","pd":7,"pb":"Port and Modernista-district streets are wide Spanish grid — fine for a big car. Melilla la Vieja itself is walk-only.","lez":0,"lezn":"No enforced ZBE in Melilla. No restriction on the 2005 diesel.","cats":["city","history","quirky"],"gem":true,"season":"April-June and September-November","one":"The Modernista route around Plaza de España and Avenida Juan Carlos I, then the tunnels and bastions of Melilla la Vieja.","book":"Ferry cabins must be booked well ahead, and sailings are heavily disrupted during Operación Paso del Estrecho in summer."},{"n":"Comporta","r":"Alentejo Litoral / Setúbal","c":"Portugal","lat":38.3856,"lon":-8.7864,"h":5.5,"km":550,"b":8,"why":"Sixty kilometres of uninterrupted white Atlantic sand backed by rice paddies, umbrella pines and storks nesting on every pole — no high-rises at all, because the whole estate was held by one family. Praia do Pego and Praia da Comporta are wide, flat and often empty even in July.","e":8,"drive":"Long but almost all motorway. The A2 through the Alentejo is fast, straight and dull. Local access roads to the beaches are sandy tracks off the N261 — firm enough for a sedan but drive them slowly; do not attempt to park on soft sand.","toll":"Via the Algarve: A22 electronic toll (~€6, EASYToll at Castro Marim) then the A2 north, a conventional barrier toll of roughly €12-14 to Grândola. Cash or card at the booth.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free sand/gravel beach car parks at Praia da Comporta and Praia do Pego, directly behind the dunes.","pd":7,"pb":"Soft sand at the edges — stay on the compacted centre of the lots and you are fine. A heavy rear-drive sedan will bog down in loose sand, so avoid the outer rows.","lez":0,"lezn":"None. Comporta is in Alcácer do Sal municipality, far outside any Lisbon zone.","cats":["coast","nature","foodwine"],"gem":true,"season":"June and September; the water is cold Atlantic year-round","one":"Lunch of grilled fish at a beach shack on Praia do Pego, then drive the rice-paddy road toward Carrasqueira to see the stilted wooden fishing harbour (Cais Palafítico da Carrasqueira).","book":"Comporta restaurants (Sal, Cavalariça, Sublime) need reservations in summer."},{"n":"Cabo de Gata","r":"Almería, Andalucía","c":"Spain","lat":36.7628,"lon":-2.1069,"h":5.8,"km":560,"b":10,"why":"Europe's only hot-desert coastline: black volcanic headlands, fossil coral reefs, and the fossil dunes of Mónsul where the flat-topped basalt rock from Indiana Jones and the Last Crusade sits on the sand. Genoveses is a 1km crescent backed by agave and dunes with zero buildings. Water clarity is Mediterranean-best because the whole coast is a protected marine reserve.","e":7,"drive":"Long but easy — the A-92 across Andalucía is fast, empty and free. The last stretch into the natural park is two-lane and fine. Inside the park the beach access roads to Mónsul and Genoveses are unsurfaced dirt tracks with washboard corrugation — passable in a sedan if driven slowly, but a low-slung E-Class will scrape on the worst ruts. Note the A-92 skirts Granada; Granada city has an enforced ZBE but the motorway ring is outside it.","toll":"Entirely toll-free: A-4/A-382 to Antequera, A-92 across to Almería, then AL-3108. The AP-4 Sevilla-Cádiz toll was abolished in 2019, so no tolls anywhere on this route.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"IN SEASON (roughly 20 June to 27 September 2026) car access to Genoveses, Barronal, Mónsul, Media Luna and Cala Carbón is REGULATED: a barrier limits entry to 216 spaces at Genoveses, 124 at Mónsul, 59 at Barronal, charged at about €5/day. Alternative: the free municipal car park on Calle Entrada de San José, then the shuttle bus (€0.50 one way, €1 return, roughly hourly 10:30-20:00). OUT of season the tracks are open and parking is free.","pd":6,"pb":"Free San José municipal lot is flat and open. The beach lots are unpaved with informal rows — big car fine, but the access tracks are the real constraint. Go out of season and this is trivially easy.","lez":1,"lezn":"Almería has an approved ZBE on paper but it is not among the 13 Spanish cities actually issuing ZBE fines as of 2026. The natural park has no ZBE at all. He can drive here without emissions concern — but see the Cabo de Gata beach access regulation above, which is a conservation measure, not an emissions one.","cats":["coast","nature","quirky"],"gem":true,"season":"May-June and September-October. Avoid July-August (access restricted, hot, crowded); winter is mild and utterly empty","one":"Playa de Mónsul at low sun — walk the fossil dune to the Duna Fósil viewpoint, then the Arrecife de las Sirenas volcanic reef at the Cabo de Gata lighthouse.","book":"Check the current season's regulated-access rules before travelling (Junta de Andalucía / Parque Natural Cabo de Gata-Níjar) — booking or a barrier fee may apply in the restricted window."},{"n":"Salamanca","r":"Castilla y León","c":"Spain","lat":40.9651,"lon":-5.664,"h":5.8,"km":575,"b":10,"why":"Everything is built from Villamayor sandstone, which turns gold at sunset — hence 'la ciudad dorada'. The Plaza Mayor is the finest Baroque square in Spain, the university facade is a Plateresque relief carving where finding the hidden frog is a local ritual, and there are two cathedrals fused into one building.","e":8,"drive":"Long but almost entirely free dual carriageway through Extremadura and Castilla. Salamanca is compact with a clear ring road; the historic core is largely pedestrian.","toll":"None if you stay on the free A-66 Ruta de la Plata the entire way from Sevilla. Do not detour onto the AP-66 further north.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Paid underground Parking Lazarillo de Tormes (by the Roman bridge, closest to the old town), roughly €1.50/hr and €18-20/day. Free option: surface parking across the Tormes on the south bank near Avenida de los Cipreses and the Puente de Enrique Estevan, 15 min walk over the bridge with the classic postcard view of the cathedral.","pd":6,"pb":"Lazarillo de Tormes has typical Spanish underground bays — usable but tight. The south-bank free parking is street-side and mostly parallel, so scout for the wider stretches near the park.","lez":0,"lezn":"None enforced. Salamanca is on the mandatory-ZBE list but is NOT among the 13 Spanish cities actually fining in 2026. The unlabeled 2005 diesel can drive into the centre legally today — but Castilla y León is expected to activate more zones, so re-check before a 2027 trip.","cats":["city","history","foodwine"],"gem":false,"season":"April-June and September-October. Castilian winters are bitter; the Plaza Mayor at Christmas is beautiful but freezing","one":"Cross the Roman bridge at dusk and watch the sandstone of the two cathedrals go from cream to deep gold, then climb the Ieronimus towers walkway inside the cathedral roofline.","book":"Ieronimus cathedral-towers tickets and the Casa Lis Art Nouveau museum are walk-up. Nothing needs advance booking."},{"n":"Toledo","r":"Castilla-La Mancha","c":"Spain","lat":39.8581,"lon":-4.0226,"h":5.8,"km":585,"b":10,"why":"A granite gorge of the Tagus wraps three sides of a hill carrying a Gothic cathedral, a Moorish-Christian-Jewish layer cake of synagogues and mosques turned churches, and the Alcázar — the whole composition is what El Greco painted in View of Toledo and it has barely changed. Damascened steel and marzipan are still made in the same streets.","e":7,"drive":"Long but almost entirely free motorway; the A-4 is fast and monotonous. Toledo's old town is a genuinely hostile medieval maze of one-way cobbled lanes with 2.2m width restrictions — under NO circumstances drive into it in this car. Park below and use the escalators.","toll":"None. A-4 north via Córdoba and Bailén, then the free CM-42 or A-42. Avoid the AP-41 (tolled); it is unnecessary.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Aparcamiento de Azarquiel — FREE, large, paved, next to the AVE station across the river, and specifically recommended for big vehicles. From there the Miradero mechanical escalators lift you into the old town in under 10 minutes. Other free lots: Toletum (by the architecture school), Santa Teresa, and Circo Romano/Venta de Aires.","pd":9,"pb":"Azarquiel is exactly what he wants: free, paved, spacious, no parallel parking, and it removes any need to drive a 4.85m sedan into a medieval city. Best parking answer on this entire list.","lez":0,"lezn":"None enforced. Toledo is on the mandatory-ZBE list but is NOT among the 13 Spanish cities fining in 2026. The old town has an APR/restricted-traffic scheme (residents and hotel guests only, camera-enforced) — that is a traffic restriction, not an emissions one, but it will fine you just the same. Park at Azarquiel and the issue never arises.","cats":["city","history","foodwine"],"gem":false,"season":"March-June and September-November. Toledo in July-August is over 38C on stone streets","one":"Stand at the Mirador del Valle across the gorge at sunset for the El Greco view, then see The Burial of the Count of Orgaz in Santo Tomé.","book":"The Toledo Pulsera Turística (bracelet, ~€12) covers seven monuments including Santa María la Blanca and San Juan de los Reyes — buy at any of them. The cathedral sells timed tickets online; worth booking in season."},{"n":"Mojácar","r":"Almería, Andalucía","c":"Spain","lat":37.1394,"lon":-1.8514,"h":5.9,"km":600,"b":8,"why":"Mojácar Pueblo is a sugar-cube village spilling down a 175m crag 2km inland, whitewashed under a Moorish street plan, with the indalo stick-figure symbol painted on doorways as a good-luck charm. From the Mirador del Castillo the view runs over the Sierra Cabrera to the sea.","e":6,"drive":"Motorway to Vera then a short climb. The road up into the pueblo is a steep, narrow one-way loop with hairpins — go up, park, walk down. Mojácar Playa below is a long flat beach strip, easy driving.","toll":"None. A-92 then A-7 — the former AP-7 Vera-Cartagena tolls were abolished, so the whole route is free.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free public car park on Calle Rambla / Parking del Pueblo at the base of the village, with an escalator and steps up. Free parking is also plentiful along Mojácar Playa outside July-August.","pd":6,"pb":"Use the Rambla lot at the bottom — it is open and manageable. Do NOT drive up into the pueblo in a 4.85m car; several corners are effectively single-vehicle with walls both sides.","lez":0,"lezn":"None. Mojácar is far below the 50,000-inhabitant ZBE threshold. No restriction.","cats":["village","coast","quirky"],"gem":true,"season":"April-June and September-October","one":"The Mirador del Castillo at the top of the pueblo at sunset, then follow the indalo symbols through the lanes.","book":"None."},{"n":"Lisbon","r":"Lisboa","c":"Portugal","lat":38.7223,"lon":-9.1393,"h":6.2,"km":620,"b":9,"why":"Seven hills of pastel and azulejo dropping to the Tagus, with the Manueline Jerónimos Monastery and Belém Tower at the water's edge and yellow trams grinding up Alfama's stepped lanes. The light off the estuary in late afternoon is the reason painters keep moving there.","e":5,"drive":"The motorway approach is easy; Lisbon itself is not. Steep cobbled hills, tram tracks that grab tyres, aggressive lane discipline, and a one-way system that will funnel you somewhere you did not intend. Park once, on the flat, and use the metro and trams. The 25 de Abril bridge is tolled northbound only.","toll":"Two routings. Via Badajoz and the A6: barrier tolls of roughly €20 Caia-Lisboa, plus the Vasco da Gama or 25 de Abril bridge toll (~€2). Via the Algarve: A22 electronic (~€6, EASYToll at the border) plus A2 barrier tolls of roughly €22. Either way budget €20-25 each way.","pf":false,"pp":false,"ps":"Parque Chão do Loureiro (EMEL), Calçada do Marquês de Tancos, at the foot of the Castelo hill in Baixa — roughly €1.30/hr, around €15-18/day, and it has a free rooftop viewpoint. Cheaper: Parque das Nações or Oriente garages at about €10/day with a 10-min metro ride in.","pd":3,"pb":"Chão do Loureiro is a multi-storey with tight helical ramps and narrow bays — feasible in the E320 but you will be careful. If you want easy: park at the surface lots at Parque das Nações (flat, wide, modern) or the free/cheap lots at Algés or Oeiras and take the train to Cais do Sodré. Street parking in Lisbon is almost entirely parallel and almost entirely too short for 4.85m.","lez":0,"lezn":"IMPORTANT AND GOOD NEWS: Lisbon's ZER is Euro-standard based, not sticker based. ZER 1 (Avenida da Liberdade / Baixa / Chiado) requires Euro 3 or better; ZER 2 (the wider ring bounded by Av. de Ceuta, Eixo Norte-Sul, Av. das Forças Armadas, Av. dos EUA, Av. Marechal Spínola and Av. Infante D. Henrique) requires Euro 2 or better. A 2005 E320 CDI is Euro 4 and therefore LEGALLY ALLOWED IN BOTH ZONES. Restrictions apply weekdays 07:00-21:00 only; weekends and holidays are unrestricted regardless. EMEL is rolling out automatic plate-reading cameras in 2026, so compliance now actually matters — but he complies. Note this is the opposite of Spain: Portugal has no DGT-style sticker, so the Spanish 'sin distintivo' status is irrelevant here.","cats":["city","history","foodwine","coast"],"gem":false,"season":"March-June and September-October","one":"Tram 28 end to end early morning, or if that feels touristy, the Mosteiro dos Jerónimos cloister in Belém followed by pastéis de Belém from the original 1837 shop.","book":"Jerónimos Monastery and Belém Tower sell timed tickets online — book ahead, the walk-up queue is over an hour. Book Time Out Market dinner tables nothing, but fado houses in Alfama yes."},{"n":"Madrid","r":"Comunidad de Madrid","c":"Spain","lat":40.4168,"lon":-3.7038,"h":6.3,"km":655,"b":7,"why":"Not a beautiful city so much as a magnificent one: the Prado's Velázquez and Bosch rooms, the Retiro's Crystal Palace on its lake, and the Plaza Mayor and Palacio Real. The daily life — vermouth at midday, dinner at 22:00 — is the actual attraction.","e":2,"drive":"The drive up is a straightforward 6+ hours of free A-4 motorway. The problem is arrival, and it is severe — see the low-emission-zone note. Realistically Madrid is a train trip, not a car trip, from Rota.","toll":"None on the A-4. The radial toll motorways (R-4 etc.) are unnecessary.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"BECAUSE THE CAR CANNOT LEGALLY ENTER MADRID, park OUTSIDE the municipal boundary and take the Metro. Best option: Rivas-Vaciamadrid, which has over 35,000 free public parking spaces and sits on Metro Line 9 (stations Rivas Futura, Rivas Urbanizaciones, Rivas Vaciamadrid) — about 30 minutes into central Madrid on a zone B1 ticket. Do NOT use the Aluche or Aviación Española park-and-rides: those are inside Madrid municipality and reaching them means committing the offence.","pd":8,"pb":"Rivas parking is suburban surface parking — wide, free, no parallel needed. This is the workaround that makes Madrid possible at all.","lez":2,"lezn":"CRITICAL — HE CANNOT DRIVE INTO MADRID. A 2005 diesel gets NO DGT environmental label (label C requires a diesel first registered from January 2006). From 2026, with the last moratorium expired, vehicles WITHOUT a DGT label are prohibited from circulating throughout the ENTIRE municipality of Madrid — not just Distrito Centro, not just inside the M-30, but the M-30 ring road itself and the whole city. Enforcement is by ANPR camera; the fine is €200 (€100 if paid promptly), and it can be issued repeatedly. There is no tourist exemption, no 'driving to a booked garage' exemption for unlabeled vehicles, and no way to buy a label for a pre-2006 diesel. Plan: drive to Rivas-Vaciamadrid (outside the boundary), park free, Metro Line 9 in. Alternatively leave the car in Toledo or Segovia and take a train.","cats":["city","history","foodwine"],"gem":false,"season":"April-June and September-November. August is hot and half the city is closed","one":"Two hours in the Prado with Las Meninas and the Bosch Garden of Earthly Delights, free entry 18:00-20:00 Mon-Sat.","book":"Prado free-slot tickets still need to be collected/booked; Palacio Real timed entry; Reina Sofía for Guernica. Book online. And plan the parking before you set off — this is the one destination where getting it wrong costs money."},{"n":"Cascais","r":"Lisboa","c":"Portugal","lat":38.6979,"lon":-9.4215,"h":6.4,"km":645,"b":7,"why":"A former royal seaside retreat with a small fishing harbour, tiled 19th-century villas and the Boca do Inferno sea chasm; the Marginal coast road runs from here past Estoril's belle-époque frontage back toward Lisbon. Guincho beach 5km north is a wild dune-backed Atlantic break used for windsurfing world tours.","e":7,"drive":"Motorway to the A5, then straightforward. The Marginal coastal road from Lisbon is slower but far prettier. Cascais town centre is pedestrianised with a confusing one-way ring.","toll":"Same as Lisbon (~€20-25) then the free A5/Marginal.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Free surface parking along the Guincho road and at the Parque Marechal Carmona edges; paid underground at Parque de Estacionamento da Cidadela / Praça 5 de Outubro, about €1/hr.","pd":6,"pb":"Guincho and the Cidadela area have open bays. The town-centre streets are narrow with short parallel bays — stay on the perimeter.","lez":0,"lezn":"None. Cascais is outside Lisbon's ZER entirely.","cats":["coast","city","foodwine"],"gem":false,"season":"May-June and September-October","one":"Drive the coast road to Cabo da Roca — the westernmost point of continental Europe — and stand at the 140m cliff edge; free parking at the lighthouse.","book":"None."},{"n":"Sintra","r":"Lisboa","c":"Portugal","lat":38.7979,"lon":-9.3906,"h":6.5,"km":650,"b":10,"why":"A Romantic-era hill of microclimate mist and exotic gardens: the Pena Palace in yellow, red and tiled Manueline pastiche on a volcanic peak, the Moorish castle walls snaking along the ridge, and Quinta da Regaleira's Initiation Well — a 27m inverted spiral tower descending underground into tunnels.","e":4,"drive":"Getting to Sintra is easy motorway. Driving IN Sintra is the problem: the roads up to Pena and the Moorish Castle are single-lane, one-way in sections, hairpinned, and clogged with tuk-tuks and buses. Sintra is the single worst place on this list to have a large car on the hill roads. Do not drive up.","toll":"Same as Lisbon (~€20-25 each way) plus the IC19/A16 approach, which is free.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Lourel car park — around 540 FREE spaces, operated by Parques de Sintra as the official visitor reception point, with an information desk and ticket sales on site. From there take bus 434 (Pena, Moorish Castle) or 435 (Monserrate, Regaleira). This is confirmed active as of mid-2026 and is by far the best answer for this car.","pd":9,"pb":"Lourel is a purpose-built modern lot with proper marked bays and coach access — completely straightforward for a 4.85m sedan. Combined monument + 24hr bus tickets can be bought online in advance.","lez":0,"lezn":"None. Sintra is a separate municipality well outside Lisbon's ZER 1 and ZER 2. No emissions restriction.","cats":["history","nature","quirky"],"gem":false,"season":"April-June and September-October. Sintra generates its own fog — check the forecast, a misty day ruins the views but makes Regaleira better","one":"Descend the Initiation Well at Quinta da Regaleira, then walk the tunnels out to the grotto.","book":"ESSENTIAL. Pena Palace uses timed-entry tickets that sell out days ahead in season — book online at parquesdesintra.pt and pick a slot. Quinta da Regaleira also benefits from advance booking. Buy the combined monument + bus pass with your Lourel parking plan."},{"n":"Cuenca","r":"Castilla-La Mancha","c":"Spain","lat":40.0761,"lon":-2.1289,"h":6.8,"km":690,"b":9,"why":"The Casas Colgadas hang their wooden balconies over a sheer drop into the Huécar gorge, reached by a vertiginous iron footbridge (Puente de San Pablo) with the old town rising on a knife-edge ridge between two river canyons. Above town, the Ciudad Encantada is a karst field of mushroom and anvil-shaped limestone.","e":7,"drive":"Long but easy free motorway, and the A-40 routing keeps you well south of Madrid — important, since the unlabeled diesel is banned there. Cuenca's old town is a single steep ridge road with hairpins and narrow sections; the modern town below is normal.","toll":"None if routed A-4 north to Ocaña then the free A-40 east to Cuenca — this also conveniently bypasses Madrid entirely, which matters for this car.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Free parking at the top of the ridge near the Castillo and along the Hoz del Huécar road below the hanging houses. Paid: Parking Alfonso VIII / Parking San Julián in the lower town, roughly €1.20/hr.","pd":6,"pb":"The Castillo-end parking is open and workable, but the ridge road up to it is narrow with passing difficulty — go up early or park below and walk. The hanging-houses street itself is not a place to be in a 4.85m car at midday.","lez":0,"lezn":"None enforced. Cuenca is not among the 13 Spanish cities fining in 2026. No restriction. The A-40 route also avoids Madrid's ban.","cats":["city","nature","history","quirky"],"gem":true,"season":"April-June and September-October","one":"Walk out onto the Puente de San Pablo footbridge over the Huécar gorge and look back at the Casas Colgadas — then walk to the parador side for the classic view.","book":"None. The Museo de Arte Abstracto Español inside the hanging houses is walk-up and excellent."},{"n":"Óbidos","r":"Centro / Oeste","c":"Portugal","lat":39.3606,"lon":-9.1572,"h":6.8,"km":700,"b":8,"why":"A complete whitewashed walled town given as a wedding gift to Portuguese queens for four centuries — you can walk the full unguarded circuit of the 13th-century ramparts, looking down on lime-washed houses trimmed in blue and yellow with bougainvillea over every doorway.","e":8,"drive":"Motorway virtually to the gate. Cars are barred from the walled town, which removes all difficulty. The ramparts have no handrail — genuinely dangerous in wind or with children.","toll":"Lisbon routing tolls (~€20-25) plus the A8 north, roughly €5.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free surface car parks outside the Porta da Vila gate along the EN8 (several large lots). Some closer lots charge about €2-3/day in high season.","pd":8,"pb":"Large flat lots with marked bays. No issue whatsoever.","lez":0,"lezn":"None.","cats":["village","history","quirky","foodwine"],"gem":false,"season":"Shoulder season; the July medieval festival and the December Vila Natal are big draws but very crowded","one":"Walk the full wall circuit, then drink ginjinha (sour-cherry liqueur) from an edible chocolate cup at one of the Rua Direita stalls.","book":"None, except accommodation during the Festival Medieval."},{"n":"Segovia","r":"Castilla y León","c":"Spain","lat":40.9481,"lon":-4.1177,"h":7.1,"km":745,"b":10,"why":"A 1st-century Roman aqueduct 28m high and 800m long, built of unmortared granite blocks and running straight through the middle of the city, plus the Alcázar — a fairy-tale castle on a rock spur that reportedly inspired Disney's Cinderella castle — and a late-Gothic cathedral called the 'lady of cathedrals'.","e":6,"drive":"The long haul is easy free motorway, but the Madrid problem shapes the route: you must NOT enter Madrid municipality with this car. Use the M-50 orbital, which stays outside the city boundary, and verify your satnav is not routing you through the M-30 or M-40 inner sections. Segovia itself is a small hill city, easy to drive around the perimeter.","toll":"Route to AVOID Madrid municipality entirely: A-4 north, then the M-50 orbital (which stays outside Madrid city limits) to the A-6, then the free N-VI/AP-61 corridor. The AP-61 Segovia spur is tolled at roughly €3-4; the free alternative is the N-603 over the Navacerrada/Guadarrama, which is scenic but slow and can be snowy in winter.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free surface parking on Paseo de Santo Domingo de Guzmán and along the Cuesta de los Hoyos below the city walls, both a 10-min walk up. Paid: Parking Padre Claret near the aqueduct, roughly €1.40/hr, €16/day.","pd":7,"pb":"The below-walls free parking is roadside and part-parallel — arrive early for a pull-in space. The Padre Claret garage is modern with decent bay sizes if he prefers to pay.","lez":1,"lezn":"Segovia itself: no enforced ZBE, and not among the 13 Spanish cities fining in 2026 — he can drive into Segovia freely. THE RISK IS THE ROUTE: the drive from Rota passes Madrid, where the unlabeled car is banned across the whole municipality. Route via the M-50, not through the city.","cats":["city","history","foodwine"],"gem":false,"season":"April-June and September-October. Winter is very cold but the aqueduct in snow is spectacular","one":"Cochinillo asado (suckling pig, carved with the edge of a plate) at Mesón de Cándido directly under the aqueduct arches.","book":"Book Mesón de Cándido or Restaurante José María ahead for lunch — they fill. Alcázar tickets are walk-up but queue in summer."},{"n":"Nazaré","r":"Centro / Oeste","c":"Portugal","lat":39.6014,"lon":-9.0703,"h":7.2,"km":740,"b":8,"why":"An underwater canyon 5km offshore focuses swell into the largest surfable waves ever ridden — the 26m+ records were all set at Praia do Norte, below the Sítio headland fort. In winter you can stand on the clifftop and watch three-storey walls of water break a hundred metres away.","e":8,"drive":"Long motorway haul, easy. The funicular links the lower town to the Sítio clifftop, but you can also drive up on a decent road.","toll":"Lisbon routing tolls plus A8 north, roughly €25-30 total each way.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free car park at Sítio near the Forte de São Miguel Arcanjo / lighthouse — the big-wave viewpoint. Lower-town seafront bays are metered in summer, free in winter.","pd":8,"pb":"The Sítio lot is large and open. It fills completely on big-swell days when the surf world descends — arrive early.","lez":0,"lezn":"None.","cats":["coast","nature","quirky","foodwine"],"gem":false,"season":"NOVEMBER TO FEBRUARY for the giant waves — that is the entire point. Summer Nazaré is an ordinary crowded beach town","one":"Stand at the Forte de São Miguel Arcanjo lighthouse on a north-west swell day and watch tow-in surfers at Praia do Norte. Check magicseaweed/Windy before committing to the 7-hour drive.","book":"None, but check the swell forecast — this destination is entirely conditions-dependent."},{"n":"Valencia","r":"Comunitat Valenciana","c":"Spain","lat":39.4699,"lon":-0.3763,"h":7.5,"km":760,"b":8,"why":"Calatrava's Ciutat de les Arts i les Ciències sits in a drained riverbed turned into a 9km linear park, all white ribs and reflecting pools; the old town has a Gothic silk exchange (La Lonja, UNESCO) and the Mercado Central's Modernista iron-and-glass hall. Malvarrosa beach is city-adjacent.","e":5,"drive":"Long free motorway, tedious but simple. The complication is arrival: Valencia now enforces its ZBE with fines. The Turia riverbed park and the Ciutat de les Arts sit at the edge of the restricted areas, so plan carefully — see the ZBE note.","toll":"Now toll-free: A-4/A-92 to Murcia then the A-7/A-31 — the former AP-7 tolls on the Cartagena-Alicante-Valencia corridor were abolished, so the whole route is free.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"BECAUSE THE ZBE BLOCKS THE CENTRE: park in Mislata, a separate adjacent municipality outside Valencia's ZBE, with free street parking and Metro lines 3/5/9 at Mislata station (about 10 min to the centre). If entering is ever permitted, Parking Mercado Central or Parking Plaza de la Reina run about €2/hr. Beach-side free parking exists near Malvarrosa outside the ZBE areas.","pd":5,"pb":"Mislata street parking is mostly parallel — but on wide suburban streets rather than medieval lanes, so it is manageable. Central Valencia garages have tight ramps.","lez":2,"lezn":"HE IS BANNED FROM THE VALENCIA ZBE. A 2005 diesel has NO DGT label. Valencia began issuing fines on 1 December 2025 for non-labelled vehicles registered OUTSIDE Valencia province — which is exactly his situation (Cádiz plate). Fine is €200 (€100 reduced). The ZBE covers five sectors: Área Centro (bounded by C/ Colón, C/ Xàtiva and C/ Guillén de Castro), Área Norte 1 and 2, and Área Sur 1 and 2, all camera-enforced. Park outside the city (Mislata is the practical answer) and use the metro. Note the restriction tightens further in 2027 and 2028 for local vehicles.","cats":["city","coast","foodwine","history"],"gem":false,"season":"March-June and September-October. Las Fallas in mid-March is spectacular but the city is impossible to park in","one":"Paella valenciana (rabbit and chicken, not seafood) cooked over orange wood in El Palmar, on the Albufera lagoon 20 min south — and the Albufera is outside the ZBE so he can drive there.","book":"Ciutat de les Arts / Oceanogràfic tickets online. El Palmar paella restaurants need a Sunday booking. And sort out the ZBE-compliant parking plan before departure."},{"n":"Porto","r":"Norte","c":"Portugal","lat":41.1579,"lon":-8.6291,"h":7.9,"km":815,"b":9,"why":"Granite and azulejo stacked vertically up the Douro gorge, crossed by Eiffel's pupil's double-deck Dom Luís I iron bridge, with port lodges on the Gaia bank and the blue-tiled São Bento station concourse. The Livraria Lello staircase and the Baroque Clérigos tower are within a few streets of each other.","e":6,"drive":"Realistically a 3-day trip, not a weekender — roughly 8 hours each way. Porto's centre is steep, one-way and narrow with tram lines; park once and walk or use the metro.","toll":"Best route is via Badajoz then Portuguese A23/A1 or the Spanish A-66 to Vilar Formoso. Portuguese barrier tolls total roughly €25-30 each way. This is at the outer edge of weekender range.","pf":false,"pp":false,"ps":"Parque de Estacionamento Trindade or Parque Silo Auto (Rua Guedes de Azevedo), roughly €12-18/day. Free alternative: park at a metro park-and-ride on the periphery (e.g. Senhor de Matosinhos or Estádio do Dragão) and ride in.","pd":4,"pb":"Central garages have tight ramps typical of old Portuguese multi-storeys. The metro park-and-ride option is much kinder to a 4.85m car.","lez":0,"lezn":"Porto has a ZER covering the historic centre and Baixa, but — confirmed for 2026 — it does NOT restrict private cars by Euro standard or fuel. It manages through-traffic and heavy vehicles. A 2005 diesel can drive in freely. No sticker exists in Portugal.","cats":["city","history","foodwine"],"gem":false,"season":"May-June and September-October; Porto is genuinely wet October-March","one":"Cross the upper deck of the Ponte Dom Luís I on foot at sunset from the Sé to the Gaia side, then a port tasting at Taylor's or Graham's.","book":"Livraria Lello requires a timed ticket bought online. Port lodge tours should be booked ahead."},{"n":"Las Médulas","r":"Castilla y León (El Bierzo)","c":"Spain","lat":42.4586,"lon":-6.77,"h":8.3,"km":830,"b":10,"why":"A landscape of jagged blood-red pinnacles left behind when the Romans dismantled an entire mountain range with the ruina montium technique — flooding tunnels to collapse the rock and wash out the gold. It is an industrial ruin 2,000 years old, now buried in sweet-chestnut forest, and a UNESCO site.","e":5,"drive":"HONEST ASSESSMENT: 8+ hours each way puts this at the very outer edge of a weekender — it is really a 4-day trip, ideally combined with Salamanca or Zamora on the way. Roads are free motorway most of the way, then good regional roads; the final climb to the Mirador de Orellán is a narrow lane with a rough final stretch.","toll":"Free the whole way if you use the A-66 Ruta de la Plata to Benavente, then the A-52/A-6 — but note the A-6 has some tolled AP sections further north; stick to the free A-6 mainline.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free car park at the Mirador de Orellán (the top viewpoint) and free parking in Las Médulas village at the visitor centre.","pd":7,"pb":"The Orellán lot is unpaved and the access lane is narrow with rough surface — drive it slowly in a low sedan. The village lot is easier.","lez":0,"lezn":"None. Rural León, no restrictions anywhere on the route if you avoid Madrid (which this route does naturally).","cats":["nature","history","quirky"],"gem":true,"season":"October-November for chestnut colour, which is genuinely the best time. April-June also good","one":"The Mirador de Orellán at sunset when the low light turns the pinnacles crimson, then walk the Galería de Orellán — the actual Roman mining tunnel.","book":"The Orellán tunnel galleries have limited access and are worth checking/booking via the Fundación Las Médulas visitor centre."},{"n":"Picos de Europa","r":"Cantabria / Asturias / León","c":"Spain","lat":43.1467,"lon":-4.8117,"h":9.5,"km":950,"b":10,"why":"Limestone massifs rising to 2,650m barely 20km from the Atlantic — the Fuente Dé cable car lifts 750m vertically in four minutes to a rock amphitheatre, and the Ruta del Cares cuts a 12km path into a gorge wall 200m above the river. Green, wet and utterly unlike southern Spain.","e":3,"drive":"NOT REALISTICALLY A WEEKENDER FROM ROTA. It is 9-10 hours each way — roughly the length of Spain. Treat it as a week-long trip or fly to Asturias/Santander. Once there, the mountain roads (the Desfiladero de la Hermida, the climb to Lagos de Covadonga) are narrow, steep and often single-track with drops; the Covadonga lakes road is closed to private cars in peak season and served by shuttle. Included for completeness with a clear caveat.","toll":"None on the free A-66/A-6/A-67 routing, but this is a very long haul.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Free car park at Fuente Dé cable-car base station; Covadonga lakes require the paid shuttle from El Repelao/Cangas de Onís in high season (about €9 return including parking).","pd":6,"pb":"Fuente Dé is a proper large lot. The mountain approach roads are the real issue for a 4.85m car — several are single-lane with passing places.","lez":0,"lezn":"None. No ZBEs anywhere in the Picos region.","cats":["nature"],"gem":false,"season":"June-September. Snow closes high routes October-May and the region is genuinely rainy","one":"The Fuente Dé cable car up to the Mirador del Cable, then walk the high path toward Áliva.","book":"Fuente Dé cable car tickets sell out in August — book online. Covadonga lakes shuttle is compulsory in peak season."},{"n":"Douro Valley","r":"Pinhão, Alto Douro, northern Portugal","c":"Portugal","lat":41.1886,"lon":-7.5442,"h":8.9,"km":850,"b":10,"why":"The oldest demarcated wine region on earth, and it looks like it: the Douro cuts a gorge through schist mountains that have been hand-terraced for port vines over three centuries, a UNESCO landscape where every bend of the N-222 opens another amphitheatre of vineyard. Quintas with whitewashed names painted on their terraces run tastings above the river, and the rabelo boats that once carried the barrels still float at Pinhão.","e":6,"drive":"Motorway almost to Régua via Porto, then the famous part: the N-222 Régua–Pinhão riverside road, repeatedly voted among the world's best drives — curling, slow and spectacular. Nothing technical, just don't plan on averaging more than 40 km/h once you're in the valley.","toll":"Portuguese motorways are tolled and several northern stretches are electronic-only — carry a toll device or register your plate online before the trip.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free riverside parking at Pinhão near the station and along the N-222 pull-offs; the quintas you book tastings at have their own free lots.","pd":8,"pb":"Easy at the quintas — gravel courtyards with room to turn. Pinhão village fills on harvest weekends in September–October. The only stress is the steep single-track lanes up to some hillside quintas; check the access before committing a long car.","lez":0,"lezn":"Rural wine country — no restrictions.","cats":["nature","foodwine","village"],"gem":false,"season":"September–October for the harvest bustle and turning vines; May–June for green terraces and quiet","one":"Book a tasting at a river-facing quinta above Pinhão, then drive the N-222 to Régua at golden hour with the terraces glowing across the water.","book":"Quinta tastings in harvest season want a few days' notice."},{"n":"Peneda-Gerês","r":"Parque Nacional da Peneda-Gerês, Minho, Portugal","c":"Portugal","lat":41.7566,"lon":-8.1567,"h":9.4,"km":900,"b":9,"why":"Portugal's only national park: granite mountains, oak forest, wild Garrano ponies and long-horned Barrosã cattle on the road, Roman milestones along a legion road, and stone villages — Soajo, Lindoso — with clusters of espigueiros, coffin-shaped granite granaries on stilts that look like a tiny necropolis. Waterfalls and natural pools everywhere in spring.","e":5,"drive":"Motorway past Porto and Braga, then mountain roads — well-surfaced but narrow, twisty and slow, with livestock wandering freely. The park's interior roads demand patience rather than skill.","toll":"Portuguese electronic tolls to Braga — sort a device or plate registration beforehand.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free village parking at Soajo and Lindoso by the espigueiros; free trailhead pull-offs throughout the park. Vila do Gerês town has metered street parking in summer.","pd":7,"pb":"Village lots are open and easy. Some waterfall trailheads (Arado, Tahiti falls) have small rough lots that overflow by mid-morning in August, pushing people into risky verge parking — go early. A few access tracks are cobbled and steep.","lez":0,"lezn":"National park — no urban restrictions.","cats":["nature","village","history","quirky"],"gem":true,"season":"May–June: full waterfalls, flowering broom, no crowds. Avoid August weekends.","one":"Stand among the espigueiros of Soajo at dusk — a platform of granite granaries on a bare rock dome, in use since the 18th century — then drive out past ponies grazing the verges.","book":""},{"n":"Guimarães","r":"Minho, northern Portugal","c":"Portugal","lat":41.4416,"lon":-8.2957,"h":8.5,"km":830,"b":8,"why":"'Aqui nasceu Portugal' — Portugal was born here, and the medieval centre is UNESCO-listed and almost absurdly intact: the castle where the first king was reputedly born, the Paço dos Duques with its forest of brick chimneys, and Praça de São Tiago's tangle of half-timbered houses now full of student café tables. Less touristed than Porto, more atmospheric than most of it.","e":9,"drive":"Motorway door to door — one of the easiest long hauls in Iberia. The historic centre is pedestrianised; you never need to drive into it.","toll":"Portuguese tolls, including electronic-only stretches north of Porto.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Large paid underground garages ring the old town (Campo da Feira, Toural); free street parking a 10–15 minute walk out in the newer quarters.","pd":8,"pb":"The garages are modern with normal bays; the ring-road approach is well signed. Nothing in the pedestrian core to tempt you into a mistake.","lez":0,"lezn":"No low-emission zone.","cats":["city","history","foodwine"],"gem":true,"season":"Year-round; spring and early autumn are best, and the Festas Gualterianas in early August are rowdy fun.","one":"Take the Penha cable car up from town, look back over the birthplace of a country, then come down for francesinha and vinho verde in Praça de São Tiago.","book":""},{"n":"Santiago de Compostela","r":"Galicia, Spain","c":"Spain","lat":42.8806,"lon":-8.5449,"h":9.6,"km":1030,"b":9,"why":"The end of the Camino, and arriving by car doesn't dull it: the granite old town is one of Spain's great urban spaces, the baroque cathedral facade over Praza do Obradoiro is a genuine jaw-dropper, and the daily theatre of exhausted, elated pilgrims arriving on foot is moving whether or not you believe in any of it. Galician food — pulpo, percebes, tarta de Santiago — is its own argument.","e":8,"drive":"The Vía de la Plata (A-66) north through Extremadura is empty, fast and beautiful, then west on the A-52. A long day but an easy one — this is the classic first overnight of a Galicia loop.","toll":"Mostly toll-free via the A-66; the coastal AP-9 around Santiago is tolled.","pf":false,"pp":false,"ps":"Paid underground garages at Praza de Galicia and Xoán XXIII (the one tour buses use, closest to the cathedral); free park-and-walk in the newer town ~20 minutes out.","pd":7,"pb":"Garage bays are standard modern size and the approach is straightforward. Do not attempt the old town itself — it's pedestrian, granite-bollarded, and camera-controlled.","lez":1,"lezn":"Over the ZBE population threshold; restrictions on the historic core exist for through-traffic but as of 2026 no sticker-based ban affects visitors who park in the garages. Watch it in coming years.","cats":["city","history","foodwine"],"gem":false,"season":"May–June and September; Galicia is greenest (and wettest) outside summer. Holy-year crowds aside, mornings are calm.","one":"Be in Praza do Obradoiro at noon when the day's pilgrims arrive, then eat pulpo á feira at a granite counter in Rúa do Franco.","book":""},{"n":"Ribeira Sacra","r":"Sil canyon, Galicia, Spain","c":"Spain","lat":42.4166,"lon":-7.5951,"h":9.3,"km":990,"b":9,"why":"A river canyon that almost nobody outside Spain has heard of: the Sil runs 500 metres deep between vineyard terraces so steep the harvest is called 'heroic viticulture', with a dozen Romanesque monasteries hidden in the chestnut woods above. Catamarans run the gorge, miradoiros like the Balcóns de Madrid hang over the drop, and the mencía wine is superb and cheap.","e":5,"drive":"Fast to Ourense, then slow, narrow canyon-rim roads — well-paved but sinuous, often single-track to the viewpoints. Distances on the map lie; allow twice what the kilometres suggest.","toll":"Toll-free on the A-66/A-52 route.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free lots at the monasteries (Santo Estevo, San Pedro de Rocas) and at the catamaran quays and miradoiros.","pd":8,"pb":"Lots are open and uncontested outside August. The single-track lanes to some viewpoints need confidence about meeting oncoming cars, not about parking.","lez":0,"lezn":"Deep countryside — nothing.","cats":["nature","foodwine","history","quirky"],"gem":true,"season":"April–June and September–October; autumn chestnut colour is the secret best.","one":"Take the catamaran through the Sil gorge beneath the terraces, then drink a glass of mencía at a miradoiro looking down on where you just sailed.","book":"Catamaran sailings sell out summer weekends — book a few days ahead."},{"n":"Cudillero & the Asturian coast","r":"Asturias, Spain","c":"Spain","lat":43.5626,"lon":-6.1455,"h":9.3,"km":960,"b":9,"why":"An amphitheatre of coloured fishermen's houses stacked around a tiny port on Spain's green north coast, with the best of Asturias either side: Playa del Silencio's silver crescent under cliffs, the wild beaches toward Cabo Vidio, and cider poured from above head height in every bar. This is the Spain that stays green all summer — eucalyptus, mist, and 20-degree August days.","e":7,"drive":"A-66 north over the Pajares pass (spectacular) then the A-8 coastal motorway. Long but all dual carriageway until the last kilometres of village lane.","toll":"The A-66 Huerna pass section is tolled; modest.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Big paid lot at the port entrance in summer (free off-season); free upper-village lots with a steep walk down. Playa del Silencio has a free clifftop lot.","pd":6,"pb":"Cudillero's single access lane down to the port is tight and jammed in August — use the upper lots and walk. Beach lots are open gravel, no drama.","lez":0,"lezn":"No zones on this coast.","cats":["coast","village","foodwine","nature"],"gem":true,"season":"June and September; even peak August is cool. Pack a rain layer always.","one":"Eat fabada then drink culín-poured cider on the port at Cudillero, and take the evening light at Playa del Silencio ten minutes west.","book":""},{"n":"Bilbao","r":"Biscay, Basque Country, Spain","c":"Spain","lat":43.263,"lon":-2.935,"h":8.4,"km":870,"b":8,"why":"The Guggenheim is still, thirty years on, one of the buildings of the age — Gehry's titanium fish glowing over the ría, with Puppy the flower dog out front — and the city around it has quietly become one of Spain's best: the Casco Viejo's pintxo bars, the riverside walk, the Ribera market, the funicular up Artxanda for the view. A perfect one-night city stop en route to the French border.","e":8,"drive":"Fast motorway the whole way via the A-66 and AP-1; the last hour through the Basque hills is genuinely pretty. City traffic is orderly.","toll":"AP-1/AP-68 sections tolled.","pf":false,"pp":false,"ps":"Underground garages everywhere; the one under the Guggenheim itself and Pío Baroja garage are the convenient pair. €20–25/day.","pd":7,"pb":"Modern garages, normal bays, clear signing. Street parking is resident-zoned — don't bother.","lez":1,"lezn":"Bilbao has a ZBE on paper, but as of 2026 enforcement targets the most polluting local traffic and visitor rules keep shifting — check the ayuntamiento site the week you go, and default to a garage, which is exempt-friendly.","cats":["city","foodwine","quirky"],"gem":false,"season":"Any month; September's film-festival-adjacent buzz and October's chill are both good. Rainier than you expect.","one":"Walk the ría at dusk when the titanium turns gold, then do a three-bar pintxo crawl in the Casco Viejo — one bite and one glass per bar, standing.","book":""},{"n":"San Juan de Gaztelugatxe","r":"Biscay coast, Basque Country, Spain","c":"Spain","lat":43.447,"lon":-2.7854,"h":8.8,"km":900,"b":10,"why":"A hermitage on a rock island tied to the mainland by a man-made stone causeway that switchbacks over the sea — 241 steps up, a bell to ring three times at the top, and the Basque coast's flysch cliffs marching off in both directions. Game of Thrones borrowed it for Dragonstone, which tells you what it looks like.","e":6,"drive":"Motorway to Bilbao then the BI-2101 coast road — narrow, pretty, and slow behind cyclists. The drama is all on foot at the end.","toll":"As for Bilbao.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free lots at the trailhead by Eneperi restaurant; an overflow lot opens in summer. From there it's a 30–40 minute walk down (and back up).","pd":6,"pb":"Open lots, easy bays — the problem is capacity, not geometry. In July–August arrive before 9:30 or after 17:00, or you'll queue on the road.","lez":0,"lezn":"None.","cats":["coast","nature","history","quirky"],"gem":false,"season":"May–June and September–October. Summer needs the (free) timed access ticket — book online.","one":"Climb the causeway early, before the coaches, ring the bell three times and make your wish with the Atlantic exploding on the rocks below.","book":"Free timed entry ticket required in high season — reserve on the Biscay tourism site."},{"n":"San Sebastián","r":"Gipuzkoa, Basque Country, Spain","c":"Spain","lat":43.3183,"lon":-1.9812,"h":9.3,"km":960,"b":10,"why":"Maybe Europe's most beautiful city beach — La Concha's perfect shell of sand between two green headlands — attached to the best eating town on the continent: more Michelin stars per head than almost anywhere, and an old-town pintxo culture where the counter of a €3 bar can outclass a starred kitchen. Small, walkable, elegant, and an easy staging post for France.","e":8,"drive":"Motorway throughout via Bilbao. Arrival is the only fiddle: the centre is compact, one-way and busy.","toll":"AP-1/AP-8 tolls.","pf":false,"pp":false,"ps":"Underground garages — La Concha/Okendo for the beach and old town, Kursaal for Gros. €25–30/day and worth it.","pd":6,"pb":"Some of the older garages (Okendo) have tight ramps and bays for a 4.85 m car — Kursaal is the more generous choice. Street parking is effectively residents-only.","lez":1,"lezn":"A ZBE covering the centre is in the pipeline; as of 2026 garage-bound visitors are unaffected. Re-check before a 2027 trip.","cats":["city","coast","foodwine"],"gem":false,"season":"June and September–October are ideal; La Concha works even in winter sun. August is packed and pricey.","one":"Swim La Concha in the morning, then graze the Parte Vieja at 13:00 sharp: txuleta bites, gilda skewers, and txakoli poured from height, one bar at a time.","book":"Book pintxo-bar-adjacent restaurants and any starred table weeks ahead."},{"n":"Laguardia & Rioja Alavesa","r":"Álava, Basque Country / La Rioja border, Spain","c":"Spain","lat":42.555,"lon":-2.585,"h":8.6,"km":900,"b":8,"why":"A walled hilltop wine village where the entire hill beneath the streets is honeycombed with medieval cellars, surrounded by the vineyards of Rioja Alavesa and the Sierra de Cantabria wall behind. Winery architecture got weird here in the best way — Gehry's titanium-ribboned Marqués de Riscal and Calatrava's wave-roofed Ysios are both minutes away.","e":8,"drive":"Motorway to Logroño's doorstep then quiet wine roads. One of the easiest 'far' trips on this chart.","toll":"AP-68 sections tolled if you route that way; avoidable.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free lots ring Laguardia's walls (the village core is car-free); wineries have their own free parking.","pd":9,"pb":"Open lots at the walls, generous bays, no competition outside festival weekends. As easy as wine touring gets.","lez":0,"lezn":"None.","cats":["village","foodwine","history"],"gem":true,"season":"September–October harvest is the moment; late spring is quiet and green.","one":"Tour a subterranean calado cellar under Laguardia, then taste at Ysios or Riscal with the Sierra de Cantabria filling the windscreen on the way in.","book":"Winery visits are by appointment — book a couple of days out."},{"n":"Bardenas Reales","r":"Navarre, Spain","c":"Spain","lat":42.205,"lon":-1.475,"h":8.8,"km":920,"b":9,"why":"A badlands desert in the middle of green Navarre — eroded clay castles, gullies and the improbable striped cone of Castildetierra, which you will recognise from a dozen films and car adverts. A 34 km dirt loop road circles the Bardena Blanca; it feels like Utah with vultures.","e":6,"drive":"Motorway to Tudela, then the park's unpaved but graded loop — fine for a normal car driven gently, dusty and washboarded in stretches. Closed after heavy rain.","toll":"AP-68 avoidable.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free — the information centre lot at the loop entrance and signed pull-offs along the circuit.","pd":9,"pb":"Nothing but space. The care point is the surface, not the parking: take the washboard slow in a low-slung car and check the loop is open at the info centre.","lez":0,"lezn":"It's a desert.","cats":["nature","quirky"],"gem":true,"season":"October–May; summer afternoons are furnace-hot and shadeless. Golden hour transforms it.","one":"Drive the loop late in the day and be at Castildetierra as the sun drops sideways across the clay — then out before the park gate closes at dusk.","book":""},{"n":"Albarracín","r":"Sierra de Albarracín, Aragón, Spain","c":"Spain","lat":40.408,"lon":-1.444,"h":7.4,"km":780,"b":9,"why":"Regularly voted Spain's most beautiful village and somehow still under-visited: pink-plastered medieval houses corbelling out over lanes barely a mule wide, a curtain wall climbing the ridge above, all wedged into a bend of the Guadalaviar gorge. The surrounding sierra hides prehistoric rock art and the protected pine forests of the Montes Universales.","e":7,"drive":"Fast to Teruel via Valencia's hinterland, then a pretty 30-minute gorge road. Straightforward.","toll":"Toll-free routing available the whole way.","pf":true,"pp":false,"ps":"Free lots below the village on the river approach; a small paid lot closer to the gate. The village itself is unenterable by car — happily.","pd":8,"pb":"The lower lots are open with easy bays, then it's a 5–10 minute uphill walk. Weekend middays in summer fill; mornings never.","lez":0,"lezn":"A village of a thousand people.","cats":["village","history","nature"],"gem":true,"season":"Any; autumn light on the pink plaster is special, winter brings snow-dusted magic and empty lanes.","one":"Walk the curtain wall up to the Torre del Andador at sunset, when the whole village goes rose-gold in the gorge below.","book":""},{"n":"Aínsa & Ordesa","r":"Sobrarbe, Aragonese Pyrenees, Spain","c":"Spain","lat":42.418,"lon":0.138,"h":9.6,"km":1000,"b":10,"why":"Aínsa's arcaded stone plaza is one of the finest in the Pyrenees, and it's the gateway to Ordesa y Monte Perdido — a national park of thousand-metre canyon walls, the Cola de Caballo waterfall, and lammergeiers overhead. The Añisclo canyon road and the villages of Sobrarbe (Torla, Broto) fill as many days as you give them.","e":5,"drive":"Long haul via Zaragoza then mountain roads — good surfaces, big views, slow averages. The Añisclo loop is single-track with passing bays and one-way in summer: spectacular, not for the nervous.","toll":"Avoidable.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Free lots below Aínsa's old town; Ordesa access is via paid parking at Torla with a shuttle bus into the valley in season (the valley road closes to cars).","pd":7,"pb":"Aínsa and Torla lots are open and manageable. Skip the Añisclo single-track in a 4.85 m car on an August weekend; do it early on a weekday instead.","lez":0,"lezn":"None.","cats":["nature","village","history"],"gem":false,"season":"June for waterfalls and green, September–early October for gold beech and quiet trails. The Ordesa shuttle runs roughly Easter–October.","one":"Walk the Ordesa valley floor to the Cola de Caballo under the canyon walls — a long but gradient-gentle out-and-back — then dinner on Aínsa's medieval plaza.","book":""},{"n":"Barcelona","r":"Catalonia, Spain","c":"Spain","lat":41.3874,"lon":2.1686,"h":10.2,"km":1060,"b":9,"why":"Gaudí's drip-castle Sagrada Família and Park Güell, the Gothic quarter's alleys, Barceloneta's beachfront, the Boqueria market — it's all as good as advertised, which is why everyone is there. As the far anchor of a Mediterranean-coast road trip it's unmatched; just plan the car side carefully.","e":7,"drive":"Motorway the whole way down the Mediterranean corridor. The city's ring roads are busy but legible.","toll":"Most AP-7 tolls were removed; minor exceptions.","pf":false,"pp":false,"ps":"Long-stay garages or your hotel's; better, park free at a suburban rodalies station (or stay just outside) and train in.","pd":4,"pb":"Garage bays are tight and expensive in the centre. The park-and-ride play removes all of it.","lez":2,"lezn":"The ZBE Rondes covers effectively the whole city on weekdays 07:00–20:00 and a pre-2006 diesel does not qualify — no DGT sticker, no entry, and foreign-plated cars must register in advance regardless. Park outside the zone and train in; weekends are unrestricted.","cats":["city","coast","foodwine","history"],"gem":false,"season":"April–June and September–October. August is hot, crowded and half-shut.","one":"Book the Sagrada Família's tower-and-nave slot for early morning light through the stained glass, before the day's crowds compress the experience.","book":"Sagrada Família and Park Güell sell out days ahead — book both before you leave."},{"n":"Cadaqués & Cap de Creus","r":"Costa Brava, Catalonia, Spain","c":"Spain","lat":42.2888,"lon":3.277,"h":11.4,"km":1190,"b":9,"why":"The whitewashed fishing town where Dalí lived — his egg-topped house at Portlligat is kept exactly as he left it — at the end of a mountain road that has protected it from everything. Beyond, Cap de Creus is a lunar headland of wind-torn schist where the Pyrenees fall into the Mediterranean: the mainland's easternmost point, olive terraces, and coves you swim from rocks.","e":5,"drive":"Motorway to Figueres, then the famous corniche over the Serra de Rodes — hairpins, slow, gorgeous. There is no fast way in; that's the point.","toll":"Largely toll-free now.","pf":false,"pp":false,"ps":"Big paid lot at the entrance to Cadaqués (the town is not driveable); free-ish rough parking out at the Cap de Creus lighthouse road pull-offs.","pd":6,"pb":"The entrance lot is open-air with normal bays but fills by late morning in summer. Absolutely do not enter the village lanes.","lez":0,"lezn":"None.","cats":["coast","village","quirky","foodwine"],"gem":true,"season":"June and September; the tramuntana wind can blow hard any month and makes the light extraordinary.","one":"Tour Dalí's Portlligat house (tiny timed groups), then drive out to the Cap de Creus lighthouse for suquet de peix as the wind hammers the headland.","book":"The Portlligat house is strictly timed-entry — book online well ahead."},{"n":"Andorra","r":"Andorra la Vella & the high parishes","c":"Andorra","lat":42.5063,"lon":1.5218,"h":10.6,"km":1090,"b":8,"why":"A microstate stacked into three Pyrenean valleys: tax-free fuel and shopping in the capital's canyon of stores, but the real reasons are above — the Coma del Clot and Tristaina lakes, the Romanesque chapels on outcrops (Sant Joan de Caselles), and in winter the biggest ski area in the Pyrenees at Grandvalira. Caldea's giant thermal spa glows like a glass shard in the valley.","e":6,"drive":"Motorway to Lleida then a solid two hours of mountain road climbing to 2,000 m passes. Winter needs chains-or-winter-tyres readiness; summer is just bends.","toll":"Spanish approach largely toll-free. Fill the tank in Andorra — fuel runs notably cheaper.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Cheap garages throughout Andorra la Vella; free trailhead lots in the high parishes (Ordino, El Serrat).","pd":8,"pb":"Garages are modern and inexpensive by Spanish standards. Border queues re-entering Spain on Sunday evenings are the real time cost.","lez":0,"lezn":"No emission zones.","cats":["nature","city","quirky"],"gem":false,"season":"June–September for lakes and ridges; December–March for snow. Shoulder months can close high passes.","one":"Hike the Tristaina lakes circuit in the morning, then soak at Caldea and fill the tank with cheap fuel before rolling back down to Spain.","book":""},{"n":"Saint-Jean-de-Luz & Biarritz","r":"Basque coast, France","c":"France","lat":43.3887,"lon":-1.6625,"h":9.8,"km":1010,"b":9,"why":"The French Basque coast an hour past the border: Saint-Jean-de-Luz is the charmer — a protected bay, striped awnings, the church where Louis XIV married, gâteau basque in every window — while Biarritz brings faded-imperial glamour, surf culture at the Côte des Basques, and the Rocher de la Vierge walkway into the waves. Between them, corniche roads and red-and-white Basque villages like Ainhoa and Espelette (of pepper fame).","e":8,"drive":"Motorway to the border at Irun, then you're there. Trivially easy for the distance.","toll":"French A63 autoroute tolls from the border — a few euros; Spanish AP-8 tolls before it.","pf":false,"pp":false,"ps":"Paid central lots and garages in both towns (Saint-Jean's Grande Plage garage; Biarritz Bellevue/Grande Plage). Free parking exists a 15-minute walk out.","pd":6,"pb":"Both towns are compact with tight-ish French garages — Bellevue is the roomier pick in Biarritz. In August, park once and walk everywhere.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZFE on the Basque coast. France requires a Crit'Air sticker only inside designated zones — none here — but note a pre-2006 diesel rates Crit'Air 4, which matters in big French cities elsewhere.","cats":["coast","city","foodwine"],"gem":false,"season":"June and September; surf is best in autumn. August is la rentrée-crowded.","one":"Morning market and macarons Adam in Saint-Jean-de-Luz, an afternoon watching longboarders at the Côte des Basques, and espelette-spiked seafood for dinner.","book":""},{"n":"Bordeaux & Saint-Émilion","r":"Gironde, Nouvelle-Aquitaine, France","c":"France","lat":44.8378,"lon":-0.5792,"h":11.0,"km":1200,"b":9,"why":"Bordeaux's 18th-century riverfront — the Place de la Bourse doubled in the world's largest reflecting pool — leads a city that has spent twenty years polishing itself into France's most liveable, with the Cité du Vin as its wine-temple landmark. Half an hour east, Saint-Émilion stacks a medieval village over a monolithic church carved down into the rock, surrounded by the most famous vineyard names on earth.","e":8,"drive":"Motorway effectively door to door via the Basque border. Long but effortless.","toll":"French autoroute tolls add up — budget ~€20+ each way from the border.","pf":false,"pp":false,"ps":"Park-and-ride tram lots on Bordeaux's edge are the smart move (cheap, includes tram tickets); central garages otherwise. Saint-Émilion has big paid lots at the village edge.","pd":6,"pb":"Bordeaux's centre is tram-tracked, one-way and camera-enforced — the P+R removes all stress. Saint-Émilion's lots are open-air and easy; its cobbled lanes are not for cars.","lez":1,"lezn":"Bordeaux's ZFE has been repeatedly postponed and France's national ZFE rules were being unwound through 2025–26 — as of mid-2026 a Crit'Air 4 diesel can enter, but verify the month you travel; the sticker itself costs a few euros online and takes weeks to arrive, so order early.","cats":["city","foodwine","history"],"gem":false,"season":"May–June and September–October; the vineyards turn crimson in late October.","one":"Climb Saint-Émilion's king's tower, tour the underground monolithic church, then taste at a grand cru classé château you booked — many take visitors, unlike Bordeaux legend suggests.","book":"Château visits are appointment-only; the good ones book out a week or more ahead."},{"n":"Dune du Pilat","r":"Arcachon Bay, France","c":"France","lat":44.5891,"lon":-1.2136,"h":11.3,"km":1230,"b":9,"why":"Europe's tallest sand dune — a 100-metre wall of white sand a kilometre wide, pine forest on one side, the Atlantic and the Banc d'Arguin sandbank on the other. You climb the staircase (or the sand, if you're proud), and the view down the Arcachon basin is unreasonably good for something an hour from a major city. Oysters in the villages of Cap Ferret across the water complete it.","e":8,"drive":"As for Bordeaux plus 45 minutes of pine-forest dual carriageway. Easy.","toll":"As for Bordeaux.","pf":false,"pp":false,"ps":"The official paid forest lot at the dune base (per-day rate, gets very full); overflow lots in season. Arcachon town has paid seafront lots.","pd":7,"pb":"Big open forest lot, normal bays, arrive before 11:00 in July–August. Don't try the verges of the access road — enforcement is real.","lez":0,"lezn":"None at the coast.","cats":["coast","nature","quirky"],"gem":false,"season":"June and September; sunset from the crest is the event, and the dune is open (and free) at all hours even when the lot booth isn't.","one":"Climb the dune for sunset over the Banc d'Arguin with a picnic of Arcachon oysters and white Bordeaux carried up in a cool bag.","book":""},{"n":"Sarlat & the Dordogne","r":"Périgord Noir, France","c":"France","lat":44.8898,"lon":1.2166,"h":12.4,"km":1290,"b":10,"why":"The France of the daydream: Sarlat's honey-stone medieval centre and its foie-gras-and-walnut market, the Dordogne river swinging beneath cliff castles — Beynac and Castelnaud glaring at each other across the water — the hanging village of La Roque-Gageac, and the gardens of Marqueyssac's clipped-box terraces. Canoe down the river past all of it. Lascaux's painted caves (the faithful replica) are twenty minutes north.","e":7,"drive":"Autoroute to Brive then rolling D-roads — French country driving at its most pleasant. The river-valley lanes are narrow but civilised.","toll":"French tolls ~€25 each way from the border.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Paid lots ring Sarlat's old town (free-ish further out); castle and village sites have their own lots, mostly paid in season.","pd":7,"pb":"Lots are open-air with fair bays. August is the Dordogne's one flaw — Dutch and British plates everywhere and lots full by 10:30. June or September and it's all yours.","lez":0,"lezn":"Deep rural France — nothing.","cats":["village","history","foodwine","nature"],"gem":false,"season":"May–June and September; Sarlat's market runs Wednesday and Saturday mornings year-round.","one":"Canoe the Dordogne from Vitrac to Beynac — two hours of drifting under La Roque-Gageac and three castles — then Sarlat's market for a picnic of everything Périgord.","book":"Lascaux IV timed tickets and canoe hire both want advance booking in summer."},{"n":"Carcassonne","r":"Aude, Occitanie, France","c":"France","lat":43.213,"lon":2.3491,"h":12.2,"km":1280,"b":8,"why":"The largest walled medieval city in Europe — a double ring of ramparts and 52 witch-hat towers restored by Viollet-le-Duc into everyone's mental image of a castle. Touristy in the lists by day, but stay to dusk when the coaches leave and the lices between the walls empty out, and it's magic. The Canal du Midi runs through the lower town for a plane-tree-shaded counterpoint.","e":8,"drive":"Motorway the whole way via Toulouse or the coast. Far, but zero-effort far.","toll":"French tolls on the A61/A62.","pf":false,"pp":false,"ps":"Official paid lots outside the Cité's Narbonne gate; the lower town (Bastide) has cheaper garages and a 20-minute walk up.","pd":7,"pb":"The Cité lots are open-air, well-marshalled, easy for any car. Nothing inside the walls is driveable.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZFE in Carcassonne.","cats":["history","city","quirky"],"gem":false,"season":"May–June and September–October; the July 14 fireworks over the ramparts are France's best, and its biggest crowd.","one":"Walk the full circuit of the lices between the inner and outer walls at dusk, after the day-trippers drain away and the stone goes amber under the floodlights.","book":""},{"n":"Collioure","r":"Côte Vermeille, Roussillon, France","c":"France","lat":42.527,"lon":3.083,"h":11.8,"km":1240,"b":9,"why":"The little harbour that invented Fauvism — Matisse and Derain painted its bell tower and coloured boats in 1905 and the town has hung reproductions where the easels stood. A royal castle on the water, pebble coves, anchovy salting houses still in business, and vineyard terraces (Banyuls, Collioure AOC) climbing straight off the sea into the Albères.","e":7,"drive":"Motorway to Perpignan then the corniche. The last stretch in summer is slow seaside traffic.","toll":"French A9 tolls.","pf":false,"pp":false,"ps":"Paid lots on the town's edge (Cap Dourats shuttle lot in high season); parking inside town is near-impossible in summer.","pd":5,"pb":"Use the edge lots and walk ten minutes — the village lanes are tight, blind and enforcement is brisk. September solves everything.","lez":0,"lezn":"None.","cats":["coast","village","foodwine","quirky"],"gem":true,"season":"June and September–October; the vendange along the terraces in early autumn is lovely.","one":"Anchovies three ways and a glass of Banyuls at a harbourside table, then swim off the pebbles beneath the exact bell tower Matisse painted.","book":""},{"n":"Gordes & the Luberon","r":"Vaucluse, Provence, France","c":"France","lat":43.911,"lon":5.2,"h":14.2,"km":1500,"b":9,"why":"Provence distilled: Gordes stacked grey-gold on its ridge, the lavender abbey of Sénanque in the valley behind, Roussillon's ochre cliffs the colour of paprika, and market mornings — Apt, Coustellet — of tapenade, melons and rosé. This is the far end of what's sane by road from Rota, and the reward is the most painted landscape in France.","e":7,"drive":"Motorway to Avignon's edge, then D-roads among the vines. The distance is the only difficulty — split the run with a night in Catalonia.","toll":"Spanish + French tolls; budget properly for the A9.","pf":false,"pp":false,"ps":"Paid village lots (Gordes charges, Roussillon charges); Sénanque has a free-ish lot that overflows in lavender season.","pd":6,"pb":"Village lots are terraced and smallish — arrive early. Lavender-season mornings at Sénanque (late June–mid July) are a scrum by 9:00.","lez":0,"lezn":"No ZFE in the Luberon villages. Avignon and Marseille have zones — a Crit'Air 4 diesel should route around Marseille's centre entirely.","cats":["village","nature","foodwine"],"gem":false,"season":"Late June–mid July for lavender; May and September for everything else without the heat.","one":"Be at the Abbaye de Sénanque at 8:00 when the lavender rows are still in shadow and the coaches haven't arrived, then breakfast in Gordes' square as the valley lights up.","book":""},{"n":"Gorges du Verdon","r":"Alpes-de-Haute-Provence, France","c":"France","lat":43.7496,"lon":6.3286,"h":15.0,"km":1600,"b":10,"why":"Europe's grand canyon — 700-metre limestone walls above water of an unreal glacial turquoise, with the Route des Crêtes' balcony bends, griffon vultures riding the thermals below you, and pedalo-and-kayak lakes (Sainte-Croix) at the outlet. Moustiers-Sainte-Marie, a faience-making village pinned to a cliff under its star on a chain, guards the entrance. The far edge of this chart, and worth every hour.","e":5,"drive":"The approach is all motorway to Aix; the gorge roads themselves are narrow, vertiginous and slow — the Route des Crêtes is one-way in sections and closed in winter. Not a drive to hurry.","toll":"As for Provence.","pf":"mixed","pp":false,"ps":"Free belvedere pull-offs along the rim roads; paid lots at Moustiers and the Sainte-Croix beaches.","pd":7,"pb":"Rim pull-offs are short but plentiful. Moustiers' lots involve a steep walk. August fills everything by mid-morning.","lez":0,"lezn":"None in the gorge country.","cats":["nature","village","quirky"],"gem":true,"season":"June and September. July–August is beautiful and busy; the Crêtes road typically closes November–March.","one":"Rent a kayak or electric boat where the Verdon enters Lac de Sainte-Croix and paddle up the turquoise slot between the cliffs — then drive the Route des Crêtes with the vultures.","book":""}];
